\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGRBulletin: CHF Loan Law A Remote Risk For Slovenian Banks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 2022\par 18:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2 Feb 2022 12:44 ET  Press Release: S&PGRBulletin: CHF Loan Law A Remote Risk For Slovenian Banks\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from S&P Global Ratings: \par  \par FRANKFURT (S&P Global Ratings) Feb. 2, 2022--S&P Global Ratings said today \par said that the Slovenian parliament's vote to introduce a law requiring banks \par to compensate loan holders for exchange-rate losses on Swiss franc (CHF) loans \par poses a remote risk to the Slovenian banking sector. Consequently, we do not \par see the law as having a direct immediate bearing on our banking industry \par country risk assessment for Slovenia. \par  \par The law retroactively introduces a cap on the exchange rate on retail loans \par issued between 2004 and 2010 and denominated in Swiss francs, or denominated \par in the domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but with the amount of interest or principal linked \par to the Swiss franc. The cap would be activated when a change in the \par euro-to-Swiss franc exchange rate caused the credit balance or repayment \par installments to differ by more than 10% compared to the exchange rate when the \par loan was drawn down. The law largely concerns long-term mortgages, regardless \par of any violations of the requirement to inform clients of certain risks when \par they took out the loans, or of whether the loans have been fully repaid, \par terminated, or sold to third parties. \par  \par We understand that the retroactive application of the law could contradict \par Slovenian and the European legislation, making the implementation of the law \par highly doubtful. The Bank of Slovenia  and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2   have \par claimed that the law violates both Slovenian and EU  law, and we expect that \par Slovenian banks will challenge the law in the constitutional court on these \par grounds. We note, however, that there is a general election scheduled for \par April this year in Slovenia. This may affect the legislative process, and thus \par adds uncertainty to the outcome. \par  \par The banks have estimated that, if enacted, the law would cause a EUR300 \par million loss. This would materially strain their profitability in the year the \par law was enacted, but we believe that the effect would remain manageable thanks \par to the banks' solid capital buffers and operating performance. Slovenian banks \par recorded sector-wide net profits of EUR473 million in the year to November \par 2021. Additionally, about 65% of domestic banking assets belong to banks that \par are part of larger foreign groups, which, in our view, would provide a second \par line of defense if necessary. However, we acknowledge that the estimate of \par loss is highly uncertain, and that it depends, among other things, on the \par evolution of the euro-to-Swiss franc exchange rate. Moreover, the banks will \par likely be affected to varying degrees. At the same time, the share of Swiss \par franc-linked loans in the private sector is less than 2% of total loans, and, \par as such, adverse movements in exchange rates would be unlikely to increase \par credit losses materially. \par  \par If losses occur, we view the estimated amount as unlikely to destabilize the \par Slovenian banking industry. However, the adoption of the law on a retroactive \par basis could bring into question the predictability of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovenia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's legal \par framework and increase uncertainty about negative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the future \par government in the banking sector. As a result, if we thought that the \par likelihood of the law being enacted was increasing, we could consider revising \par our view of industry risk in Slovenia's banking industry to a weaker \par assessment. We could take a similar action if the discussions around the \par introduction of the law turned out to be the starting point for a series of \par unfavorable laws for the domestic banking sector. We will also monitor \par potential negative shifts in foreign investor sentiment, as these could \par increase economic risks in the country. \par  \par This report does not constitute a rating action. \par  \par Primary Credit Analyst: Gabriel Zwicklhuber, Frankfurt +  49(0)6933999169; \par                         gabriel.zwicklhuber@spglobal.com \par Secondary Contacts: Cihan Duran, CFA, Frankfurt +  49 69 3399 9177; \par                     cihan.duran@spglobal.com \par                     Anna Lozmann, Frankfurt +  49 693 399 9166; \par                     anna.lozmann@spglobal.com \par                     Lukas Freund, Frankfurt +  49-69-3399-9139; \par                     lukas.freund@spglobal.com \par  \par  \par No content (including ratings, credit-related analyses and data, valuations, \par model, software, or other application or output therefrom) or any part thereof \par (Content) may be modified, reverse engineered, reproduced, or distributed in \par any form by any means, or stored in a database or retrieval system, without \par the prior written permission of Standard & Poor's Financial Services LLC or \par its affiliates (collectively, S&P). The Content shall not be used for any \par unlawful or unauthorized purposes. S&P and any third-party providers, as well \par as their directors, officers, shareholders, employees, or agents (collectively \par S&P Parties) do not guarantee the accuracy, completeness, timeliness, or \par availability of the Content. S&P Parties are not responsible for any errors \par