\par\par \b \uc2 *S&PGR Revises Slovenia Outlook To Pos; Affirms 'A/A-1' Ratings\b0\par\par\uc2 3,062 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2016\par 17:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 16 Dec 2016 11:36 ET  Press Release: S&PGR Revises Slovenia Outlook To Pos; Affirms 'A/A-1' Ratings\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par The following is a press release from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2 : \par OVERVIEW \par  \par      -- We project Slovenia's robust economic growth will persist in 2017-2019. \par      -- Monetary conditions are gradually normalizing and non-performing \par exposures have reduced sizably, and, despite the current budgetary pressures, \par we expect Slovenia's government debt relative to GDP to continue on a downward \par path over 2017-2019. \par      -- As a result, we are revising our outlook on Slovenia to positive from \par stable, and affirming our 'A/A-1' long- and short-term sovereign credit \par ratings. \par      -- The positive outlook reflects our view of at least a one-in-three \par likelihood that we will raise our ratings on Slovenia over the next two years \par if we observe further improvements in the country's transmission of monetary \par policy and budgetary position, with a decline in general government debt. \par  \par RATING ACTION \par  \par On Dec. 16, 2016, S&P Global Ratings revised its outlook on the Republic of \par Slovenia to positive. At the same time, we affirmed our 'A/A-1' long- and \par short-term foreign and local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sovereign credit ratings on Slovenia. \par  \par RATIONALE \par  \par The outlook revision reflects our expectation that the recovery of the \par Slovenian economy will continue at a solid pace. We now estimate that the \par Slovenian economy will grow by about 2.5% in 2016 and accelerate to 2.7% in \par 2017, supported by strong net exports and increasing private consumption. The \par latter will be supported by higher disposable incomes thanks to improving labor \par market conditions, low, albeit slowly increasing, inflation and likely public \par sector wage hikes, as well as growing household credit. In addition to domestic \par demand, Slovenia's economy will continue to be supported by the country's \par competitive export-oriented economic structure. Private investment will further \par underpin export potential, for example as production in the automotive sector \par is extended. In that regard, we expect manufacturing to remain an important \par sector for Slovenian economic growth over the projection horizon. As the output \par gap narrows and wages continue to increase, we anticipate that inflation will \par be over 1% in 2017. \par  \par We also believe that the implementation of growth-enhancing structural reforms, \par including in judicial and administrative areas, could further boost Slovenia's \par medium-to-long-term economic growth prospects, while health care system reform \par could improve prospects for government finances over time. Furthermore, the \par educational system plays an increasingly important role in preserving the \par international competitiveness that the open Slovenian economy enjoys. Although \par the economy benefits from its openness to international trade (exports of goods \par and services will amount to about 80% of GDP this year), in our view, \par significant administrative barriers, e.g., in judiciary, still inhibit business \par and investments. \par  \par The continuing deepening of international linkages will remain positive for the \par Slovenian economy and, with strengthening trade and services balances, we \par expect the current account to remain in surplus of over 6% of GDP on average \par over 2016-2019 despite rising imports as investment and consumption increase. \par While the external deleveraging of the financial sector continues, firms have \par increasingly relied on alternatives to bank funding to finance investments, for \par example own reserves, intercompany loans, or foreign debt issuance. Despite the \par pickup in consumer credit in recent months, we believe credit growth will \par likely remain subdued in 2017. While we still view the monetary and credit \par transmission mechanism to the real economy in Slovenia as hampered due to the \par recent banking crisis, despite the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's (\f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's) expansionary \par monetary stance, we expect banking sector activities and monetary conditions to \par continue to normalize in 2017. In that vein, we anticipate that the ratio of \par nonperforming loans of 6.4% (as of September 2016) will further decline, given \par the economic growth and further restructuring efforts, especially in the small \par and midsize enterprises sector. We also note that, following years of \par deleveraging, the corporate debt-to-GDP ratio has reduced to below the euro \par area average. \par  \par We believe that rising domestic demand and the recent strong labor market \par performance--with the unemployment rate decreasing from around 8% on average in \par 2016 to 7.7% at year-end 2017--will support the authorities' budgetary \par consolidation efforts. We project the general government deficit to average \par 1.8% of GDP over 2016-2019, with the deficit in 2017 likely slightly higher \par than envisaged by the government, depending on the outcome of ongoing \par negotiations on public sector wages and the stance on relieving consolidation \par measures introduced in past years. We also expect government investment to \par resume following the break in 2015-2016 owing to the start of the new \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2  budget \par cycle. We nonetheless expect that the government will continue to focus on \par further narrowing the fiscal deficit and reducing the general government \par debt-to-GDP ratio. To this end, lawmakers have adopted a fiscal rule act that \par establishes limits for general government expenditures so as to achieve a \par structurally balanced budget in the medium term. \par  \par We expect that, besides successively lower budget deficits, the drawdown of the \par government's existing cash buffer will drive the decrease in general government \par debt in nominal terms by 0.2% of GDP on average per year over 2016-2019. In our \par view, this will support a reduction in the gross general government debt as a \par share of GDP to about 69% in 2019 from 81% in 2015. \par  \par At the same time, we project that the decline in the net government debt-to-GDP \par ratio will be around 2% of GDP over the same period. As with our treatment of \par the debt of all asset management companies ("bad banks"), our estimates of \par Slovenia's gross and net general government debt include obligations related to \par the Bank Asset Management Company (BAMC), issued to purchase loans and other \par distressed assets from participating Slovenian banks at a discount to market \par value. At the same time, we exclude from the general government debt stock the \par guarantees related to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Financial Stability Facility\f28 \uc2 \f2  (EFSF; see "S&P \par Clarifies Its Approach To Accounting For EFSF Liabilities When Rating The \par Sovereign Guarantors," published Nov. 2, 2011, on RatingsDirect). We forecast \par the ratio of general government interest expenditures to general government \par revenues will be about 6% in 2016, down from 6.5% in 2015, before declining \par further thanks to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Public Sector Purchase Programme, which is \par currently scheduled to run until the end of 2017. \par  \par In our forecast we do not incorporate any future proceeds from the ongoing \par privatization process (for example, of the largest state-owned bank \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nova \par Ljubljanska Banka\f28 \uc2 \f2 ) or sale of assets from the BAMC, which took over \par nonperforming claims totaling EUR5 billion (about 12% of 2016 GDP) from the \par banking sector. If these sale revenues were applied to government debt \par reduction, net general government debt relative to GDP could decline at a \par faster pace than we currently anticipate. \par  \par Following a protracted banking crisis, Slovenia's banking sector returned to \par profitability in 2015 and appears well capitalized. According to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of \par Slovenia\f28 \uc2 \f2  data, the average capital adequacy ratio (on a solo basis) was 21.4% \par in September 2016, up by 0.5 percentage points compared with year-end 2015. We \par believe that the regulatory framework has materially strengthened, driven by \par the transfer of the supervisory functions to the \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  for the largest part of \par the banking system and a successful transition of local regulatory oversight. \par Market confidence in local banks remains weak, though, in our view, as they \par still have to demonstrate success and sustainability under the new setup after \par the recent years' restructuring. The repayment of substantial external \par liabilities and loan deleveraging, together with growth of customer deposits, \par has led to improvements in banks' funding positions, which we expect to \par persist. \par  \par Although we consider that policy risks in Slovenia have receded since 2013 and \par the current government has survived several no-confidence votes, we cannot rule \par out disagreements among the government coalition parties along ideological \par lines, e.g., on the pace of the abovementioned reforms or on the role of the \par very large state-owned or -controlled enterprises in the economy, which could \par lead to early elections. We think that long-entrenched political patronage and \par only slowly improving institutional and corporate governance could hamper the \par pace and effectiveness of the abovementioned reforms, due to the vested \par interests of incumbents in these sectors. This could potentially slow the \par economic restructuring under way and prevent a more efficient allocation of \par resources in the economy, with negative implications for future economic growth \par prospects. In this context, we would view negatively any potential legislative \par efforts or government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aimed at weakening the independence of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovenia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par OUTLOOK \par  \par The positive outlook reflects our view that there is at least a one-in-three \par probability of an upgrade over the next two years if, alongside the continued \par economic recovery, we observe further improvements in the transmission of \par monetary policy to the real economy and budgetary position, with a decline in \par general government debt. \par  \par We could revise the outlook to stable if we consider there is a risk of a \par material slippage in Slovenia's budgetary trajectory and a reversal in net \par general government debt dynamics, or if the current account turns into a \par deficit position, including for example due to a significantly weakened \par external macroeconomic environment. A weakening of Slovenia's key institutions \par or policy consensus across the currently governing coalition with the potential \par to hamper policy cohesiveness could also lead us to revise the outlook to \par stable. \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 16 Dec 2016 11:36 ET \par Press Release: S&PGR Revises Slovenia Outlook To -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par KEY STATISTICS \par  \par RATINGS SCORE SNAPSHOT \par  \par RELATED CRITERIA AND RESEARCH \par  \par Related Criteria \par      -- Criteria - Governments - Sovereigns: Sovereign Rating Methodology - \par December 23, 2014 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology For Linking Short-Term And Long-Term \par Ratings For Corporate, Insurance, And Sovereign Issuers - May 07, 2013 \par      -- General Criteria: Use Of CreditWatch And Outlooks - September 14, 2009 \par      -- General Criteria: Methodology: Criteria For Determining Transfer And \par Convertibility Assessments - May 18, 2009 \par  \par Related Research \par      -- Sovereign Risk Indicators - December 14, 2016. For an interactive \par version, go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.spratings.com/sri"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.spratings.com/sri}}}\uc2 \f2  \par      -- Global Sovereign Rating Trends Mid-Year 2016 - July 13, 2016 \par      -- 2015 Annual Sovereign Default Study And Rating Transitions - May 24, \par 2016 \par      -- Global Aging 2016: 58 Shades Of Gray - April 28, 2016 \par  \par In accordance with our relevant policies and procedures, the Rating Committee \par was composed of analysts that are qualified to vote in the committee, with \par sufficient experience to convey the appropriate level of knowledge and \par understanding of the methodology applicable (see 'Related Criteria And \par Research'). At the onset of the committee, the chair confirmed that the \par information provided to the Rating Committee by the primary analyst had been \par distributed in a timely manner and was sufficient for Committee members to make \par an informed decision. \par  \par After the primary analyst gave opening remarks and explained the \par recommendation, the Committee discussed key rating factors and critical issues \par in accordance with the relevant criteria. Qualitative and quantitative risk \par factors were considered and discussed, looking at track-record and forecasts. \par  \par The committee agreed that the fiscal assessment: debt burden had improved. All \par other key ratings factors remained unchanged. \par  \par The chair ensured every voting member was given the opportunity to articulate \par his/her opinion. The chair or designee reviewed the draft report to ensure \par consistency with the Committee decision. The views and the decision of the \par rating committee are summarized in the above rationale and outlook. The \par weighting of all rating factors is described in the methodology used in this \par rating action (see 'Related Criteria and Research'). \par  \par RATINGS LIST \par  \par                                            Rating \par                                            To                  From \par Slovenia (Republic of) \par  Sovereign Credit Rating \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A/Positive/A-1      A/Stable/A-1 \par  Transfer & Convertibility Assessment      AAA                 AAA \par  Senior Unsecured \par   Foreign and Local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                A                   A \par  \par Certain terms used in this report, particularly certain adjectives used to \par express our view on rating relevant factors, have specific meanings ascribed to \par them in our criteria, and should therefore be read in conjunction with such \par criteria. Please see Ratings Criteria at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2  for further \par information. Complete ratings information is available to subscribers of \par RatingsDirect at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.globalcreditportal.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.globalcreditportal.com}}}\uc2 \f2  and at spcapitaliq.com. All ratings \par affected by this rating action can be found on S&P Global Ratings' public Web \par site at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.standardandpoors.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.standardandpoors.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . Use the Ratings search box locate