\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Eyed Amid Volatility -- WSJ Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2013\par 11:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This story has been posted on The Wall Street Journal Online's Emerging Europe blog at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 By Sean Carney\par \par \uc2 TODAY'S CALENDAR\par \par \uc2 Local/GMT\par \par \uc2 1300/1100 Slovenia \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor to address banking seminar\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in central Europe are set for another day of volatile trade Wednesday after the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sees room for continued monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its headline interest rate to a record low of 3.60% from 3.80% Tuesday in a move to help revive the country's stagnant economy ahead of parliamentary elections next spring.\par \par \uc2 The forint weakened by about 0.4% on the day following the decision Tuesday, and the Polish zloty weakened by about 0.3%. In early trade Wednesday, the forint and Czech koruna were both a tad weaker while the zloty is clawing back some of the previous day's losses.\par \par \uc2 Despite tentative signs of an economic rebound in the region, inflation rates in Hungary, Poland and in the Czech Republic remain below levels targeted by respective central banks and in each country interest rates are at historic lows.\par \par \uc2 "With inflation below target and the economic recovery still fragile, it looks like the dovish monetary policy committee [in Budapest] will seek to ease policy as far as it can," said Capital Economics economist \uc2 William Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Czechs are due to decide on monetary policy and any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In Ljubljana, \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Bostjan Jazbec will address a seminar on the banking industry in his country and in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Slovenian state-owned banks, which dominate the country's financial sector, are suffocating under about 7 billion euros ($9.44 billion) of bad loans, equal to about a fifth of the nation's total economic output.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to clean up those banks' balance sheets has stoked fears that the country may have to seek some form an international bailout, becoming the sixth euro-zone country needing assistance.\par \par \uc2 OTHER NEWS\par \par \uc2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Former Czech Prime Minister Petr Necas has married Jana Nagyova, the ex-top aide involved in an alleged bribery and power-abuse scandal that toppled his conservative government in June, local media reported Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 When Ms. Nagyova arrived at a downtown Prague police station for questioning Tuesday she introduced herself using the surname Necasova, Czech dailies Mlada Fronta Dnes and Hospodarske Noviny reported on their websites.\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY: Hungary's Export-Import Bank Zrt. or Eximbank announced Tuesday that it issued EUR400 million ($540 million) worth of bonds, maturing Feb. 13, 2019. This is the second issuance under the company's EUR2 billion international bond program set up Dec. 4, 2012. The previous issuance took place on Dec. 5 last year, worth $500 million.\par \par \uc2 HUNGARY: \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday revised its inflation forecasts downward and raised its economic growth forecasts for this year and 2014 on the same day that it cut its policy rate to a new low.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its forecast for annual average inflation next year to 2.4% from June's forecast of 3.2%. Its view on this year's inflation rate was little changed at 2.0% compared with 2.1% previously.\par \par \uc2 BULGARIA: The central banks of Ukraine and Bulgaria both added to their gold reserves in August, despite a sharp rise in the price of the metal.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine added 80,000 troy ounces of the metal to its official reserves last month, which now stand at 1.33 million ounces, according to data Tuesday from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 --Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 25-09-13 0922GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130925e99p000bs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
