\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Eyed Amid Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2013\par 04:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 1300/1100 Slovenia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor to address banking seminar \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in central Europe are set for another day of volatile trade Wednesday after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it sees room for continued monetary easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its headline interest rate to a record low of 3.60% from 3.80% Tuesday in a move to help revive the country's stagnant economy ahead of parliamentary elections next spring.\par \par \uc2 The forint weakened by about 0.4% on the day following the decision, and the Polish zloty weakened by about 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Despite tentative signs of an economic rebound in the region, inflation rates in Hungary, Poland and in the Czech Republic remain below levels targeted by respective central banks and in each country interest rates are at historic lows.\par \par \uc2 "With inflation below target and the economic recovery still fragile, it looks like the dovish monetary policy committee [in Budapest] will seek to ease policy as far as it can," said Capital Economics economist \uc2 William Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Czechs are due to decide on monetary policy and any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday.\par \par \uc2 In Ljubljana, \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Bostjan Jazbec will address a seminar on the banking industry in his country and in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 Slovenian state-owned banks, which dominate the country's financial sector, are suffocating under about 7 billion euros ($9.44 billion) of bad loans, equal to about a fifth of the nation's total economic output.\par \par \uc2 Efforts to clean up those banks' balance sheets has stoked fears that the country may have to seek some form an international bailout, becoming the sixth euro-zone country needing assistance.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.866-907 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.861-913 \par %Chg 0.00 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 299.13-96 \par Previous 2150 GMT 299.06-66 \par % Chg +0.06 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.2082-182 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.2081-165 \par % Chg +0.02 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    4.52%  4.55% \par 5 yrs    4.98%  5.10% \par 10yrs    5.75%  5.77% \par  \par Poland \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    3.18%  3.22%(1) \par 5 yrs    3.66%  3.70% \par 10yrs    4.32%  4.36% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    0.39%  0.39% \par 5 yrs    1.12%  1.15% \par 10yrs    2.35%  2.39%(1) \par (1) 11 yrs \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,393.06 +4.40 +0.18% \par  \par BUX \par 18,389.32 +193.24 +1.06% \par  \par PX \par 962.02 +4.89 +0.51% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Former Czech Prime Minister Petr Necas has married Jana Nagyova, the ex-top aide involved in an alleged bribery and power-abuse scandal that toppled his conservative government in June, local media reported Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When Ms. Nagyova arrived at a downtown Prague police station for questioning Tuesday she introduced herself using the surname Necasova, Czech dailies Mlada Fronta Dnes and Hospodarske Noviny reported on their websites.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's Export-Import Bank Zrt. or Eximbank announced Tuesday that it issued EUR400 million ($540 million) worth of bonds, maturing Feb. 13, 2019. This is the second issuance under the company's EUR2 billion international bond program set up Dec. 4, 2012. The previous issuance took place on Dec. 5 last year, worth $500 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday revised its inflation forecasts downward and raised its economic growth forecasts for this year and 2014 on the same day that it cut its policy rate to a new low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered its forecast for annual average inflation next year to 2.4% from June's forecast of 3.2%. Its view on this year's inflation rate was little changed at 2.0% compared with 2.1% previously.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: The central banks of Ukraine and Bulgaria both added to their gold reserves in August, despite a sharp rise in the price of the metal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ukraine added 80,000 troy ounces of the metal to its official reserves last month, which now stand at 1.33 million ounces, according to data Tuesday from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to this article.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 25-09-13 0209GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130925e99p0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\