\par\par \b \uc2 CEE Morning Briefing:  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Eyed Amid Volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 765 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2013\par 04:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 1300/1100 Slovenia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor to address banking seminar \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in central Europe are set for another day of volatile trade Wednesday after the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sees room for continued monetary easing. \par \par \uc2 Hungary's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its headline interest rate to a record low of 3.60% from 3.80% Tuesday in a move to help revive the country's stagnant economy ahead of parliamentary elections next spring. \par \par \uc2     The forint weakened by about 0.4% on the day following the decision, and the Polish zloty weakened by about 0.3%. \par \par \uc2     Despite tentative signs of an economic rebound in the region, inflation rates in Hungary, Poland and in the Czech Republic remain below levels targeted by respective central banks and in each country interest rates are at historic lows. \par \par \uc2     "With inflation below target and the economic recovery still fragile, it looks like the dovish monetary policy committee [in Budapest] will seek to ease policy as far as it can," said Capital Economics economist \uc2 William Jackson\uc2 . \par \par \uc2     The Czechs are due to decide on monetary policy and any potential \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday. \par \par \uc2     In Ljubljana, \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Bostjan Jazbec will address a seminar on the banking industry in his country and in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2     Slovenian state-owned banks, which dominate the country's financial sector, are suffocating under about 7 billion euros ($9.44 billion) of bad loans, equal to about a fifth of the nation's total economic output. \par \par \uc2     Efforts to clean up those banks' balance sheets has stoked fears that the country may have to seek some form an international bailout, becoming the sixth euro-zone country needing assistance. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.866-907 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.861-913 \par %Chg 0.00 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 299.13-96 \par Previous 2150 GMT 299.06-66 \par % Chg +0.06 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.2082-182 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.2081-165 \par % Chg +0.02 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    4.52%  4.55% \par 5 yrs    4.98%  5.10% \par 10yrs    5.75%  5.77% \par  \par Poland \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    3.18%  3.22%(1) \par 5 yrs    3.66%  3.70% \par 10yrs    4.32%  4.36% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par          Tues   Mon \par 3 yrs    0.39%  0.39% \par 5 yrs    1.12%  1.15% \par 10yrs    2.35%  2.39%(1) \par (1) 11 yrs \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,393.06 +4.40 +0.18% \par  \par BUX \par 18,389.32 +193.24 +1.06% \par  \par PX \par 962.02 +4.89 +0.51% \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   CZECH REPUBLIC: Former Czech Prime Minister Petr Necas has married Jana Nagyova, the ex-top aide involved in an alleged bribery and power-abuse scandal that toppled his conservative government in June, local media reported Tuesday. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   When Ms. Nagyova arrived at a downtown Prague police station for questioning Tuesday she introduced herself using the surname Necasova, Czech dailies Mlada Fronta Dnes and Hospodarske Noviny reported on their websites. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   HUNGARY: Hungary's Export-Import Bank Zrt. or Eximbank announced Tuesday that it issued EUR400 million ($540 million) worth of bonds, maturing Feb. 13, 2019. This is the second issuance under the company's EUR2 billion international bond program set up Dec. 4, 2012. The previous issuance took place on Dec. 5 last year, worth $500 million. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   HUNGARY: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungary's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Tuesday revised its inflation forecasts downward and raised its economic growth forecasts for this year and 2014 on the same day that it cut its policy rate to a new low. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lowered its forecast for annual average inflation next year to 2.4% from June's forecast of 3.2%. Its view on this year's inflation rate was little changed at 2.0% compared with 2.1% previously. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   BULGARIA: The central banks of Ukraine and Bulgaria both added to their gold reserves in August, despite a sharp rise in the price of the metal. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par   Ukraine added 80,000 troy ounces of the metal to its official reserves last month, which now stand at 1.33 million ounces, according to data Tuesday from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \par   --Leos Rousek in Prague contributed to this article. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \par   Write to Sean Carney at sean.carney@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par \par   Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope/}}}\uc2 \f2  for the WSJ blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region.  [\par   09-24-13 2209ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020130925e99p0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20\b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Boss Welcomes Fiscal Union Deal\b0\par\par\uc2 sm/tz/gz   \par \uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2011\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o   \par \par \uc2 Ljubljana, 13 December (STA) - Marko Kranjec, the governor of Slovenia's\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has labelled the recent EU agreement on a tighter fiscal\par \par \uc2 union a "good achievement of politicians in Brussels". This is the most\par \par \uc2 that could be expected, he told TV Slovenija late on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Kranjec said "radical changes" had been achieved with regard to the\par \par \uc2 harmonisation of public finances. "We are in a process of harmonisation\par \par \uc2 that has been difficult to imagine so far."\par \par \uc2 He is not surprised that rating agencies are less pleased with the deal,\par \par \uc2 but said he would not make too much out of it. As for capital markets, it\par \par \uc2 is still too early to make any conclusions.\par \par \uc2 Kranjec is convinced capital markets will "realise that this deal from\par \par \uc2 Brussels will be an important contribution to stabilisation and prevention\par \par \uc2 of the eurozone crisis."\par \par \uc2 The euro will persevere and Slovenia needs to keep it as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he\par \par \uc2 said, noting that it would be bad for any country to abandon the euro and\par \par \uc2 revert back to its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With public finances looking increasingly shaky, some economists and\par \par \uc2 pundits have been warning that \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will eventually have to accept some\par \par \uc2 sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the EU or the International Monetary Fund (IMF).\par \par \uc2 But Kranjec labelled that notion an "excessive projection of our future\par \par \uc2 situation," arguing that politicians will find enough wisdom to take\par \par \uc2 appropriate measures on public finances, he said.\par \par \uc2 "It is not impossible to reduce the structural deficit to the required\par \par \uc2 level," he said. But he explained that the price for that will be lower GDP\par \par \uc2 growth, which will be the case for the entire EU.\par \par \uc2 Dokument STA0000020111213e7cd000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
