\par\par \b \uc2 DJ INTERVIEW: Slovene Central Bk: New Govt Must Stay Course  \b0\par\par\uc2 832 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2004\par 22:04\par \uc2 OsterDowJones Commodity Wire\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2004, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  Standard Attribution Statement: News Provided by COMTEX.  \par \par \uc2 LJUBLJANA, Sep 30, 2004 (ODC via COMTEX) --  \par \par \uc2 (This story was originally published Wednesday)  \par \par \uc2 By Nick Carey Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \par \uc2 Whichever party wins Slovenia's general elections Oct. 3 must stick to the country's agreed fiscal policy or risk failure to join the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor told Dow Jones Newswires. "Any loosening of fiscal policy would be the greatest threat to our prospects of adopting the euro," Mitja Gaspari said earlier this week. "I would be very disappointed in any government that took that path." Gaspari also called on any future government to create fiscal reserves as the Slovene economy picks up after several years of healthy but unremarkable growth, and said he prefers to maintain a restrictive interest rate policy for as long as it's feasible. Slovenia entered the European exchange rate mechanism known as ERM2 - June 27, and is aiming to adopt the common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2007. Slovenia, which broke away the from former Yugoslav Republic in 1991 and has a population of just 2 million, joined the European Union May 1. Two other small new E.U. members, Estonia and Lithuania, joined ERM2 on the same day. Part of the logic behind the move is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of these small countries are particularly vulnerable to speculation, making the euro a more stable bet for business and trade. The Slovene central exchange parity rate agreed on with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is 239.64 tolar to the euro. The tolar has remained within 0.2% of that rate - the maximum fluctuation band is 15% - since late June. Gaspari said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made one small \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in July to maintain parity, but declined to comment on the size of that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The general elections to be held in \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  this Sunday are expected to be a near-run thing. After 12 years as the main partner in successive coalition governments, the left-leaning Liberal Democrats of Slovenia, or LDS, face a serious challenge from the center-right Slovenian Democratic Party, or SDS. The country's main daily Delo gives SDS 21.5% of the vote, just 0.2 percentage points ahead of LDS, although polling agency GfK Gral-Iteo puts LDS at 16.6%, 3.6 points ahead of SDS. Either way, a close race is predicted. LDS' onetime lead of 10 percentage points has evaporated in recent weeks. Gaspari said the election promises of the main parties are irresponsible, as they would require significant increases in spending. Slovenia's fiscal deficits for 2004 and 2005 are expected to reach around 2% of gross domestic product, within the 3% GDP limit set by the Maastricht criteria for adopting the euro. "Any changes to fiscal policy would be counterproductive and would result in speculative attacks on the tolar," he said. The Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added, is 100% committed to the euro adoption agreement brokered with the current government. Gaspari said one potential obstacle that must be overcome is reducing inflation. Although price rises have slowed from 9% in 2000 to an expected level of 3.5% this year, Gaspari said inflation will have to go lower if the country is to gain European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  approval to adopt the euro. Under ERM2, Slovenia's inflation must be no higher than the average of the three lowest inflation rates in the euro zone, plus 1.5%. That currently gives a figure of around 2.5%. Gaspari said he expects inflation to fall to 3% next year, but that it may not reach 2.5% by the end of 2006, particularly if oil prices stay high. "Whether or not we're allowed to adopt the euro will be a matter for the ECB," he said. "Our task is to get as close as possible and then take it from there." Interest rates in Slovenia are higher than in the euro zone, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key 60-day tolar bill rate at 4%, in comparison with the ECB's 2% key rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last cut rates by 25 basis points in mid-June. Although the country's economy is beginning to take off, Gaspari said he is reluctant to raise interest rates any time soon. The Slovene economy picked up in the second quarter, with GDP growth of 4.6% recorded by the country's statistical office, up from 3.7% in the first quarter. Gaspari predicts GDP growth of 4% for the year, up from 2.5% in 2003 as the euro-zone economies gather steam. Almost 70% of Slovenia's trade is with other E.U. member states. Slovenia is the richest of all the new E.U. members, with an average monthly salary of around EUR800. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Web site:  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.bsi.si"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.bsi.si}}}\par \par \uc2 -By Nick Carey, Dow Jones Newswires; 42 0 22 108 5272; nick.carey@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Copyright 2004 Dow Jones. All rights reserved.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020040930e09u001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
