\par\par \b \uc2 Slovenian cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  for first time since ERM-II.  \b0\par\par\uc2 102 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2004\par 15:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LJUBLJANA, July 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday for the first time since joining the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2) last month as a step towards adopting the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Slovenia said it had sold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but declined to elaborate. Traders said the bank had sold 20 million euros.  \par \par \uc2 The tolar was trading at 239.89/240.01 at 1301 GMT. The central parity of Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under ERM-II was set at 239.640 tolars per euro.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040727e07r001ty\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  News Roundup - Tuesday, 28 January. \b0\par\par\uc2  By eho. \par \uc2 2,540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2003\par 00:48\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 28 January (STA) - Below is a roundup of major events on Tuesday, 28 January: \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - After the coalition-proposed motion on the consultative EU and NATO referendums is expected to be dabated in parliament either tommorrow or Thursday, the government and the opposition are still engaged in talks in a bid to reach a consensus of all parties, a POP TV report said. A compromise would involve two changes to the Constitution, TV Slovenija reported, and added that legal experts have already gathered to draft a compromise deal. Speaker of the Parliament Borut Pahor could neither deny nor confirm the TV reports about the talks. Furthermore, the public broadcaster TV Slovenija said that MPs are expected to discuss the calling of both referendums, scheduled for 23 March, on Thursday rather than late tommorrow evening. Head of the LDS deputy faction Tone Anderlic, however, told STA that the motions could well be debated as early as tomorrow. LJUBLJANA - The coalition SLS expects the Defence Ministry to produce a detailed study of both scenarios of Slovenia's integration into NATO, namely one if Slovenia's chooses NATO membership and the other if it decides to remain outside the Alliance. The study should include the costs of both scenarios, SLS president Franci But told the press. According to But, citizens need to be informed about the exact cost of NATO membership and the impact it would have on the position of the country, which is part of the region where conflicts are frequent. The ministry should also explain what would happen to the professionalization of the armed forces in either scenario and how much neutrality would cost. LJUBLJANA - Slovenia has joined the EU's stance on the Iraqi crisis, reiterating that an efficient and complete disarmament of Iraq is the solution to the crisis, the Foreign Ministry, which is following the developments carefully and with concern, said in a press release. The country believes that the supreme authority to solve disputes in the international community is the UN Security Council, so Slovenia believes Iraq should fulfil its obligations stemming from UN Security Council resolutions. Meanwhile, a news report said that the U.S. has sent a second request to \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take part in a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iraq. According to POP TV, which quoted "well informed sources", the U.S. asked Slovenia in a "semi-official way" whether the country would be willing to offer additional, different form of expert help, namely experts in medicine, biological, chemical and nuclear weapons as well as IT experts. The most surprising request, according to the TV station, was that for translators for Arabic. LJUBLJANA - The coverage of the president's speech before the World Economic Forum in Davos, alleging that he warned that the referendum on Slovenia's accession to NATO might not succeed if the U.S. decides to take unilateral action against Iraq without the approval of the UN, stirred a lot of attention in Slovenia. Until the Davos forum press release summarising his statement, Slovenian officials never linked the issues of attack on Iraq and a NATO referendum, said the national TV Slovenija. To clarify any misunderstanding after the Davos press office apologised to the president's office earlier today for wrongly reporting on his statement, the state-owned TV network aired the relevant excerpt from President Janez Drnovsek's speech in Davos. At the forum's plenary session on trans-Atlantic relations, Drnovsek said that a unilateral U.S. action against Iraq "would certainly not help the partnership, and I must certainly say that it would not help me or our government in Slovenia to win the referendum on NATO...". LJUBLJANA - Irish Minister for Europe Dick Roche will be arriving for a one-day visit to Slovenia tomorrow for talks dominated by concerns of the future make-up of the soon-to-be enlarged EU. The issue is being debated within the European Convention, a forum responsible to draft a European Constitution by mid-year. The visiting minister is scheduled to hold talks with Foreign Minister Dimitrij Rupel, Europe Minister Janez Potocnik and head of the parliamentary foreign affairs commitee Jelko Kacin. LJUBLJANA - Macedonian Deputy Prime Minister Radmila Sekerinski is scheduled to pay a visit to Slovenia tomorrow. She is to be hosted by Europe Minister Janez Potocnik, who is to discuss with her Slovenia's experiences in accession to the EU. Slovenian authorities have said on several occasions that helping Balkan countries in their efforts to join the EU is an opportunity as well as duty for Slovenia as a soon-to-be EU member with vast experiences gained in the accession process. Sekerinski is first expected to hold talks with Slovenian Economics Minister Tea Petrin, followed on Wednesday afternoon with a meeting with Potocnik and Labour Minister Vlado Dimovski. A meeting with Parliament Speaker Borut Pahor is scheduled for Thursday. LJUBLJANA - Terms for the presidential function and the organisation of the president's office in Slovenia have been set in the bill on the presidential office, which was passed in 44-28 vote in parliament. The new act also defines the rights and privileges of former state presidents. The bill on reducing the consumption of alcohol was also passed today. Backed in a 37:12 vote, the new act is related to the fact that consumption of alcohol per capita ranks Slovenia at the top in the world, while harmful consequences of alcohol abuse are one of Slovenia's major health issues. LJUBLJANA - The non-parliamentary Environmental Movement Party (SEG) has joined the debate about the construction of a mosque in Ljubljana, stating that no religion should meddle with politics. "Religion in politics sows discord instead of building unity and arouses hatred instead of love," the party said, voicing its support for the construction of a mosque in Ljubljana. The commercial TV station POP TV meanwhile, reported yesterday that Muslim Mufti Osman Djogic quoted Saudi Arabia, Qatar, UAE and Turkey as the countries that could help by providing financial resources for the construction of a mosque. STRASBOURG - Slovenian parliament's permanent delegation to the Council of Europe (CoE) has addressed a letter to President of the CoE Parliamentary Assembly Peter Schieder, drawing his attention to the only round-the-clock radio programme of the Slovenian ethnic minority in Austria, which is to stop broadcasting due to a lack of funds. The delegation - currently attending the first part of the regular session of the CoE Parliamentary Assembly in Strasbourg - wanted to inform Schieder on the problem the Slovenian minority has in acquiring funds for Radio Dva. Schieder was also asked to help resolve the problem. The letter was handed over to Schieder by the head of Slovenian delegation Roman Jakic. LJUBLJANA - Croatia has sent to the Slovenian government the last request before arbitration to settle issues related to the jointly-owned N-plant in Krsko (NEK). The Croatian side is asking Slovenia to purchase Croatia's stake in NEK. That the request has been received was confirmed by the Slovenian Environment Ministry, which is to respond to the offer early next week. However, while the Slovenian government intends to study the document, it insists on the stance that all NEK-related issues have been adequately settled in the Slovenian-Croatian NEK agreement. The government will therefore try to get the accord ratified by the Slovenian parliament. The last Croatian attempt to resolve the NEK dispute with Slovenia before resorting to arbitration has been made because arbitration has to be called in not later than five years after the dispute has emerged, that is before 30 July 2003. LJUBLJANA - The council of the Bank of Slovenia confirmed amendments to the act on banking, which has already been forwarded to the Finance Ministry, and amended decrees on money transfers with abroad and reporting market players on securities trading. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the amended act also sets down new regulations on the acquisition of qualified stakes of foreign banks in Slovenian banks and vice versa. As a result of the changes to decree governing money transfers with banks abroad, Slovenians will be able to buy and sell foreign securities directly from 1 February, without having to go through a licensed Slovenia-based trader. The change has been made to comply with the EU accession agreement, which stipulates a four-year transitional period that expires on the first day of February. MARIBOR - It is good that the government has drafted a programme that enables more systematic monitoring of prices which are not directly influenced by the market, and has defined tax \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in areas where this is required by a monopoly or oligopoly market situation. This is how Bank of Slovenia governor Mitja Gaspari assessed the government's anti-inflation programme for the daily Vecer. What is most important with the anti-inflation programme is that the government decided that the planned price increases will take place in proportion to the general price rise, Gaspari said in an interview for the Maribor-based daily. LJUBLJANA - The Economics Ministry earmarked EUR 31m to encourage the development of companies this year, EUR 15.6m of which have already been distributed to companies through public tenders to encourage entrepreneurship and competition. These are development encouragement and not classic state aids given to troubled companies, which is why the invitations for applications are intended for solid companies, particularly from the sectors vital for successful breakthrough on international markets, Economics Ministry State Secretary Mateja Mesl told the press yesterday. The amount allocated for development encouragement is higher that last year's, when EUR 20m were provided. LJUBLJANA - A delegation of the Polish Ministry for Infrastructure led by relevant minister and Deputy PM Marek Pol is to arrive for a three-day visit to Slovenia tommorrow upon the invitation of Transport Minister Jakob Presecnik. An agreement on sea transport is to be signed by both officials on Thursday. The delegations are to discuss the development of transport infrastructure, while the economic co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the two countries in transport is also to be touched on, said Transport Ministry. Poland is a very important business partner of Slovenia's. According to the GZS figures, Poland holds the tenth place among importers of Slovenian goods, with a 2.6% share of Slovenia's total exports. It is also at the 17th place among exporters to Slovenia, with Polish goods making 1.4 percent of all Slovenian imports. KOPER - Istrabenz, a company which specialises in energy, tourism and investments, generated a net profit of EUR 14.3m in 2002, with revenues at EUR 37.6m, according to preliminary estimates. Istrabenz chairman Igor Bavcar told a press conference that the company is expected to earn EUR 19.9m this year, with a net profit of EUR 9.3m. The company, which has 16 subsidiaries, plans to expand its business in 2003, most notably to offer integral energy solutions and conduct real estate business. Two dependent companies will be set up to manage these businesses, each with a starting capital of about one million euros. The value of Istrabenz's real estate deals is expected to amount to EUR 25m in five years. SKOPJE - A court in Skopje is expected to decide by the end of January whether to issue a temporary injunction prohibiting owners of Macedonia's largest publishing house Nova Makedonija, the largest being Slovenian company Jug storitve, from exercising ownership rights over their stakes in the company, the Skopje-based newspaper Utrinski vesnik reported. According to the daily, this would freeze shares of Nova Makedonija, which Jug storitve purchased at a public privatisation tender last year, until the final solution of a dispute on one of the most compromised privatisation cases in the country. The Slovenian group has been facing troubles ever since it bought 70 percent of the publishing house for EUR 2.3m in September. LJUBLJANA - This year will be crucial for the leading Slovenian clothes maker Mura, which will have to reverse negative trends of the last three years, CEO Borut Meh told the daily Dnevnik. According to Meh, this will depend on the goodwill of the state, for which he believes it would be better to take part in helping the ailing Mura back on track rather than having to deal with social problems if the restructuring programme fails. Moving Mura's production into countries with cheaper workforce might seem the easiest solution, however this scenario would leave 5,000 or 4,000 people out of job in SE Slovenia, which is the country's poorest region, causing a major social problem both for the region and for the state. LJUBLJANA - Slovenia's tourism industry posted a three-percent rise in the number of foreign visitors and tourist nights. With a total of 2,158,240 tourists and 7,311,846 overnight stays registered in 2002, the "magic number" of two million tourists was beaten for the second year in a row. As head of Slovenian Tourist Board (STO) Bojan Meden told the press, last year was very successful, as revenues from tourism are expected to exceed one billion euros, according to unofficial business results. Most foreign visitors to Slovenia came from Italy, Germany, Austria and Croatia. The number of Slovenian tourists and tourist nights went down one percent last year with a total of 857,382 domestic visitors. LJUBLJANA - Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves totalled US$ 7.84bn at the end of November 2002, up US$ 843m from the previous month and up US$ 2.45bn over November 2001. Meanwhile, the country's foreign debt amounted to US$ 8.23bn, thus exceeding the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves by US$ 391m. The bulk of all \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, US$ 6.61bn, were held by the Bank of Slovenia, while the rest, US$ 1.23bn, was held by the commercial banks. Since the end of 2001, the reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased by US$ 2.36bn, while those in commercial banks dropped by US$ 270m in the first eleven months of 2002. According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bulletin, the long-term foreign debt totalled US$ 8.12bn, while the short-term debt stood at US$ 112m. Over US$ 3bn of the long-term debt was public and state-guaranteed debt, while the remaining amount of around US$ 5bn was private debt. LJUBLJANA - The Ljubljana bourse engaged in modest trading, the volume of trade amounting to EUR 4.7m after 2,090 deals were made, including three package deals. Most indices dropped, including the benchmark SBI 20 index. The latter dropped by nearly 0.75 percent to 3,323.74 points. BREZICE - The Slovenian 16th century composer Iacobus Handl Gallus (1550-1591) is to receive special attention at the festival of classical music in the SE Slovenian town of Brezice. According to the festival director Klemen Ramovs, the major works works of Gallus - whop was a contemporary of Orlando di Lasso, Giovanni Pierluigi da Palestrina, Giovanni Gabrieli and Claude Monteverdi - are to be performed at the Brezice Festival in the next few years. The festival intends to become the European centre reviving and presenting Gallus's music. Gallus's motets are to be performed by soloists accompanied by old instruments. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020030128dz1s001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  News Roundup - Saturday, 14 September. \b0\par\par\uc2  By vh/lpr. \par \uc2 865 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2002\par 21:36\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 14 September (STA) - Below is a roundup of major events on Saturday, 14 September: \par \par \uc2  BRATISLAVA - The co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  between candidate countries for EU membership within the Central European Free Trade Agreement (CEFTA) has definitely contributed to making them fit for the integration in the EU, said Slovenian PM Janez Drnovsek at the CEFTA summit in the Slovakian capital, which wound up with the signing of a joint declaration. Drnovsek, who has also met with his Hungarian counterpart Peter Medgyessy, believes that "just as CEFTA has served for the preparation of our countries to EU membership, so it will in the future". Drnovsek pointed out that Slovenia, which is to host next year's CEFTA summit, will try to maintain co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  between signatory countries, while keeping CEFTA's door open for new members. At the end of next year, CEFTA is expected to lose some of its signatories - which currently include Slovenia, Hungary, Poland, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Bulgaria and Romania - because all countries that are integrated into the EU have to give up all free trade agreements that do not involve the EU. NEW YORK - Slovenian Foreign Minister Dimitrij Rupel yesterday met with his Yugoslav counterpart Goran Svilanovic on the sidelines of the 57th UN General Assembly. The two discussed the development of bilateral relations, agreeing that a legal base is required in order to further them, particularly in economy. Rupel expressed support for Yugoslav efforts for membership in the Council of Europe. The Slovenian minister moreover met representatives of the American Jewish Committee at their request, and attended a working dinner of the Human Security Network, hosted by Austrian Foreign Minister Benita Ferrero-Waldner, as Austria currently chairs the initiative. MARIBOR - A lot of co-ordination will be necessary if the Swiss multinational Novartis actually takes over the Ljubljana-based drug maker Lek, believes the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mitja Gaspari. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market with such transactions as is Novartis's, tolar could gain as much as twenty percent in nominal terms, and such appreciation could not be endured," Gaspari told the daily Vecer. The co-ordination will be necessary also so as to prevent great oscillations on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Gaspari explained. Such sterilisation policy, which has to be carried out by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make the exchange policy relatively successful, also requires somewhat higher interest rates, Gaspari said. If the prices fail to steady at the same time, such policy of interest rates will be present for a longer period of time, Gaspari explained. LJUBLJANA - Slovenian agriculture is far from being unfit for the EU, Emil Erjavec, professor at the Biotechnical Faculty in Ljubljana and member of the negotiation group for Slovenia's accession to the EU, told the daily Delo. Farmers may have been even overly informed about what is expecting them, but the issue here is not only about the EU or EU integration, Erjavec thinks. Slovenian farmers will be increasingly exposed to market and capitalism laws, regardless of Slovenia's integration into the EU. Erjavec also said that at this moment what counts most for Slovenian agriculture is not so much the amount of subventions that it obtains, but rather the position of the Slovenian market and the competitive position of its food industry. LJUBLJANA - Slovenian oil traders Petrol and Istrabenz, which both export to Bosnia-Herzegovina, are quite satisfied with a decree issued by the Bosnian council of ministers by which the country will from now on import only liquid oil fuel that is in line with European standards. Since Petrol has been maintaining the requested quality standards for a long time now, the Ljubljana-based company expects the decree to increase its competition advantages on the Bosnian market, director of Petrol BH Oil Company Silvo Komar has told STA. The Bosnian subsidiary of Istrabenz, OMV Istrabenz BiH, has not yet examined the decree, but essentially the company thinks it is a positive move which will hopefully settle the circumstances on a rather difficult market. LJUBLJANA - The trading on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange last week took place under speculations about the announced takeover of the Slovenian pharmaceutical Lek by the Swiss multinational Novartis. Investors felt a bit uneasy because Bank Austria sold a lot of Lek shares, most of them in foreign ownership, below the announced takeover price of SIT 95,000 (EUR 416), as low as at SIT 87,800 (EUR 385). The benchmark SBI 20 index closed the week at 3,312.08 points, down 10.6 points (0.32 percent) over the previous week. The turnover amounted to EUR 51m. OBREZJE - Around 2,000 dead birds were discovered by Slovenian customs officers in an Italian van with a trailer at the Slovenian-Croatian border crossing Obrezje on Wednesday. A fowling piece was also found inside a double wall of the vehicle, the Slovenian Customs Administration said. According to the smugglers, the birds - protected by the 1979 Bern Convention on the conservation of European wildlife and natural habitats - were shot in the surroundings of Osijek, in eastern Croatia, where the two had been spending a hunting week. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020020914dy9e00209\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Takeover of Lek will Require a Lot of Co-ordination,  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Governor Says. \b0\par\par\uc2  By vh/bdo. \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2002\par 14:29\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Maribor, 14 September (STA) - A lot of co-ordination will be necessary if the Swiss multinational Novartis actually takes over the Ljubljana-based drug maker Lek, believes the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Mitja Gaspari. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market with such transactions as is Novartis's, tolar could gain as much as twenty percent in nominal terms, and such appreciation could not be endured," Gaspari told the Saturday issue of the daily Vecer. \par \par \uc2  The co-ordination will be necessary also so as to prevent great oscillations on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Gaspari explained. Such sterilisation policy, which has to be carried out by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make the exchange policy relatively successful, also requires somewhat higher interest rates, Gaspari said. If the prices fail to steady at the same time, such policy of interest rates will be present for a longer period of time, Gaspari explained. The governor explained that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been in contact with banks ever since the announcement about the intended Lek takeover. On a technical level, the bank is also in contact with the potential bidder. The main reason for that is to explain how the system works, so as to make sure that the transaction - if it takes place - is carried out without difficulties, Gaspari said. "We also told the potential bidder in what way such a transaction cannot be carried out in Slovenia, as there has been some incomprehension," said Gaspari, adding that this was mainly about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how the offer can be prepared. Commenting on the future of Slovenian banks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes that the achieved share of foreign capital in banking is big enough, so that there is no need to further increase it substantially. The combination of domestic, foreign, private and public shares will gradually stabilise, according to the governor. Gaspari also touched on the announced preparation of a new privatisation programme of Slovenia's second largest bank, Nova Kreditna banka Maribor (NKBM). The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects to be presented with the plans sometimes in autumn, to see whether they are in line with the views of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  about a stable functioning of the banking market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said the net inflow to Slovenia this year amounted to around EUR 500m. The transactions from Lek sale and the sale of a 34-percent of Nova Ljubljanska banka (NLB), Slovenia's largest bank, to the Belgian banking group KBC, will raise the share to that comparable to last year's, he explained. As for inflation movements, the Bank of Slovenia expects the government not to raise government-controlled prices substantially by the end of the year, which was agreed at a meeting in May. Gaspari believes that the inflation can still be cut to four percent by 2004, although there are some unknowns, such as the price of oil derivatives. "Therefore it is important that we do as few mistakes as possible. The time dimension has become essential - we are running out of time," he pointed out. Gaspari expects that next year already Slovenia will reach the point so as to be able to fix the exchange rate to the euro after EU accession. This will happen only if there are no substantial deviations from current movements and if the exchange depreciation steadies gradually, provided there are no unpredictable events. We expect the fixation could be reached in a year and a half. But, foremost, inflation rate must drop to four percent, Gaspari told Vecer. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020020914dy9e000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Economic Expert Mencinger Blames Government for High Inflation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By ep/sm. \par \uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2002\par 11:30\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 22 May (STA) - It is pointless to stop inflation by force as it is only the consequence of some other economic developments. In the case of Slovenia, it is chiefly the problem of growing expenses, as we usually distinguish between inflation of expenses and inflation of demand, economist Joze Menciger said in an interview for the national television and added that it was the government and its unsuccessful pay policy that is to blame for high inflation. \par \par \uc2  Mencinger, who is also the chancellor of the University of Ljubljana, does not believe it sensible to curb inflation by means of money supply. That would not be productive and it would also cause a stagnation. Since the Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been trying to help the net exporters by means of the exchange rate of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it has been doing what it can. "The blame is therefore fully on the part of the government," Mencinger said late on Tuesday commenting on the dispute between Economics Minister Tea Petrin and Governor of the Bank of Slovenia Mitja Gaspari on who is to blame for such a high inflation rate in Slovenia. In April the annual inflation rate stood at 8.4 percent. Mencinger does not think an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the money market by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  necessary to stop a further increase in inflation. According to him, it is a well-konwn fact that ever since Slovenia became independent a decade ago, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been taking care of the relatively weak tolar and thus helped the exporters. "Inflation is related to money indeed, but in the long run. In the short run, the two factors are not related, especially in the transition economies," he explained. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor's forecast of a four-percent inflation rate in 2003 is attainable in Mencinger's opinion, but it will largely depend on movements in world oil prices. "Should these drop, his (Gaspari's) forecast holds true". Commenting on the announced budget supplement, Mencinger said that the adoption of the two-year budget plan was a nonsense, which was applauded by everybody. The government renounced fiscal policy in advance, and only three months later a budget supplement was announced. "I believe that the government has responded too quickly and, above all, nonsensically by suspending contracts for 25 days. That will only raise costs," Mencinger added in concern with the government decision to suspend the implementation of the budget for 25 days and stop assuming all new contractual obligations for budget-funded goods and services, subsidies, investments, and the purchase and construction of basic assets. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020020522dy5m000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Review of the Slovene Economy - December 1999 to November 2000. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 09 December (STA) - Higher prices of oil and raw materials had a considerable impact on the economic conditions in the year 2000. Nevertheless, the economic trends were quite positive both in Slovenia and in the world. Worse trading conditions, a higher external debt and the lack of direct foreign investment in particular in the second half of the year, however, caused an imbalance in Slovenia's current payments, while the current deficit was up and got close to the record figures of 1999. Slovenia's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell below US$ 4 billion last August for the first time in the last three and a half years, while the external debt rose to US$ 5.78 billion. Due to the fluctuation of oil prices on the world market the government adopted in April a price-setting model for petrol and, in October, also for natural gas. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia continued the alignment of its legislation with EU standards. While insurance legislation was passed, the telecommunications bill, which should determine the rules of the game before Slovenia opens its telecommunications market for foreign competition in 2001, had less luck. Brussels criticised Slovenia for not having privatised banks, insurance companies and the biggest state-owned companies. In the year of political changes personnel replacements in state-owned companies and other state-run institutions were frequent. The attempt to merge the Port of Koper and the forwarding agent Intereuropa into a logistics centre, which would be one of the biggest in the Adriatic region, has failed. Still, in the autumn the Port of Koper and its partners were granted a licence for the management of the container berth in the Italian port of Trieste. A number of takeovers took place in the year 2000, in particular in the production of beverages, in the trade and dairy sectors as well as in \par \par \uc2  the chemical industry. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's economic growth in 2000, previously expected to reach 4.75 percent, will probably be slightly lower. The inflation rate, however, will be higher than the planned 6.1 percent. Due to a number of unfavourable international factors it is likely to reach 8.9 percent. Slovenia's industrial production rose by 7.2 percent in the first ten months of 2000 in comparison with the same period of 1999. The unemployment rate was by 1.4 percent lower in September than a year before, reaching 11.7 percent. Measured at ILO standards it stood at just 6.7 percent. The average gross wages rose by 1.5 percent in real terms between January and September in comparison with the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  DECEMBER 1999 \par \par \uc2  1st SEATTLE - A Slovene delegation, led by Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur, attended a WTO ministerial conference. 17th LJUBLJANA - The amended veterinary act took effect. 21st ZAGREB - Meetings of the Slovene-Croatian commission for economic cooperation and the joint committee for the implementation of the free trade agreement. 22nd LJUBLJANA - The price of electricity for households rose two by percent, while that for industry remained unchanged. 22nd LJUBLJANA - Joze Lenic was appointed Director of the Pension Management Fund. 23rd LJUBLJANA - Marjan Rekar was appointed President of the Slovene Development Corporation (SRD). 28th LJUBLJANA - The amended act on tax procedure took effect. 29th MARIBOR - Mirko Zagar, TAM's trustee in bankruptcy, and the Consortium of Purchasers of TAm's property signed a contract to sell the entire TAM's property for SIT 9.4 billion (US$ 39 million). \par \par \uc2  JANUARY \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - Slovenia liberalised its trade in agriculture and food products with all CEFTA members. 1st LJUBLJANA - The dairy Gorenjska mlekarna became a part of the dairy Ljubljanske mlekarne. 6th LJUBLJANA - The government adopted the direct investment promotion programme for 2000. 14th MARIBOR - The banks SKB and NKBM signed a letter of intent to merge, but as the NKBM failed to privatise the plan was not realized and the SKB began searching for a new strategic partner. 17th KRANJ - Rubber goods producer Sava acquired a majority stake in Chemo. 21st LJUBLJANA - The parliament passed the 2000 budget. 22nd LJUBLJANA - The act on the supervision of state aids took effect. 23rd LJUBLJANA - The key provisions of the act on financial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of companies took effect. The provisions set down the measures that have to be taken in case of insolvency as well as the guarantees for capital adequacy. They also stipulate personal responsibility of the management. 27th DAVOS - Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek attended 30th World Economic Forum. 30th PRISTINA - A Slovene government and business delegation visited Kosovo. \par \par \uc2  FEBRUARY \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - The veterinary and sanitary inspection rules took effect. 9th LJUBLJANA - Slovenia and Hungary signed an agreement on the use of the Port of Koper and logistics centres. 10th LJUBLJANA - The Centre for Tourist Promotion of Slovenia was renamed the National Tourist Association of Slovenia (SNTO). 27th LJUBLJANA - The Slovene Development Corporation organised a transfer of property to the authorized investment companies for bridging the privatisation gap. The gap was reduced from SIT 158.1 billion (US$ 658 million) to SIT 76.3 billion (US$ 316 million) in the year 2000. \par \par \uc2  MARCH \par \par \uc2  3th LJUBLJANA - The parliament defeated a motion of interpellation against Finance Minister Mitja Gaspari, which was proposed by the SKD and the SLS. 3rd LJUBLJANA - The acts on the privatisation of insurance companies and on the insurance industry took effect. The latter opens the insurance market for foreign competition. 8th ROME - Slovenia and Italy signed agreements on investment promotion and on cooperation in education, science and culture. 9th MURSKA SOBOTA - The dairy Pomurske mlekarne confirmed the acquisition of the dairy Mlekarne Ptuj. 22nd SKOPJE - Soft drinks producer Fructal acquired a majority stake in the Macedonian producer of fruit juices Konzeks. 28th MOKRICE - A meeting of Slovene and Croatian negotiators for an inter-state agreement on the Krsko nuclear power plant. \par \par \uc2  APRIL \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - An interbank agreement an the ways of cashing cheques took effect. Cheques can be cashed solely in the bank that runs the account. 4th LJUBLJANA - The price-setting model for petrol took effect. The price of petrol altered thirteen times in 2000; ten times it was up and three times down. 5th LJUBLJANA - A member of the executive committee of the governors of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tommas Padoa-Schioppe, met with Governor of the Bank of Slovenia France Arhar. 6th LJUBLJANA - The government dismissed Ivo Banic from his post of Director of Elektro Slovenije (ELES) and appointed Vekoslav Korosec Acting Director of this public electricity supplier. 8th LJUBLJANA - Slovenia and Russia signed an investment promotion and protection agreement. 9th LJUBLJANA - Farmers and owners of agricultural land elected their representatives to the Agriculture and Forestry Chamber. 12th PODGORICA - Minister of Small Business and Tourism Janko Razgorsek visited Montenegro and met with Minister of Tourism Vladimir Mitrovic. 16th WASHINGTON - Governor of the Bank of Slovenia France Arhar attended an annual IMF and WB meeting. 18th LJUBLJANA - The beginning of a three-day conference at which Slovenia presented its experience on the way to the market economy to the countries of SE Europe. \par \par \uc2  MAY \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - The prices of electricity were up by four percent for households and three percent for industry. 10th LJUBLJANA - Several provisions of the act on the prevention of black economy took effect. The law as a whole will take effect in May 2001. 19th SARAJEVO - A Slovene government and business delegation visited Bosnia. 27th LJUBLJANA - The act on exceptional tax remission for all those whose gross income in 1999 did not exceed 45 percent of the average annual wage in Slovenia took effect. 30th LJUBLJANA - Ervin Kuhar was appointed Director of the Agriculture and Forestry Chamber. 30th PRAGUE - Slovenia and Great Britain signed an agreement on international overland transport of people and goods. \par \par \uc2  JUNE \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - The government dismissed the members of the Krsko N-plant managing board and appointed 15 members of the board of the Pension Management Fund. 1st LJUBLJANA - Liberalization of trade between Slovenia and Turkey. 8th LJUBLJANA - The Bank of Slovenia decided to abolish custody accounts by the end of 2002. 13th LJUBLJANA - Croatian Minister of Maritime Affairs, Transport and Communications Alojz Tusak visited Slovenia. 15th LJUBLJANA - Marjan Podobnik, Deputy Prime Minister, was appointed Director of Telekom Slovenija. The government also appointed Marija Ribic Director of the Post of Slovenia and dismissed members of the managing board of the Pension Management Fund. The government also appointed 20 new state secretaries. 15th KRSKO - The annual overhaul of the Krsko N-plant was finished. The long-term modernisation of the plant, during which two steam generators were replaced and a series of additional security measures taken, was thus concluded. 18th HANOVER - Slovenia's day at the Expo 2000. 19th LJUBLJANA - The government adopted the bill on telecommunications, which however was later rejected by the responsible parliamentary committee. According to the committee, the problem of investments of individuals and local communities in the building of telecommunication network was not solved adequately. 21st WARSAW - The CEFTA sub-committee for agriculture agreed not to propose further liberalisation of trade in agriculture products among CEFTA members. 22nd LJUBLJANA - The government adopted an ordinance on state aids. 27th LJUBLJANA - The government discussed tax debt issues. One hundred largest tax debtors owed SIT 38 billion (US$ 158 million) at the end of June. The government did not officially reveal the names, but they were published in a tabloid. 29th LJUBLJANA - The dairy Ljubljanske mlekarne took over the dairy Mariborska mlekarna. \par \par \uc2  JULY \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - Banks ceased to guarantee payment of cheques without cover. 1st LJUBJANA - The agriculture act took effect. 1st LJUBLJANA - The Ljubljana Stock Exchange began calculating and publishing additional stock exchange indices. 4th LJUBLJANA - A Dutch government and business delegation in Slovenia, led by Minister of Foreign Trade Gerrit Ybem. 4th KRANJ - Sava acquired a majority stake in Teol. 5th LJUBLJANA - Joze Lenic was dismissed from his post of President of the Pension Management Fund. He was replaced by Tomaz Toplak. 6th LJUBLJANA - The Triglav insurance company acquired a 28.8-percent stake in the bank Abanka. 6th TRIESTE - Minister of Transport and Communications Anton Bergauer met with Italian Minister of Maritime Affairs and Transport Pierluigi Bersani. 6th LJUBLJANA - The government adopted the aggregative economics scenario for 2001 and began the 2001 budget debate. 10th BUDAPEST - The Ministers of Transport of Slovenia and Hungary, Anton Bergauer and Lazslo Nogradi, met. 10th SKOPJE - Slovenia and Macedonia reached an agreement on wine quotas. 18th LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. 19th RADOVLJICA - The Agriculture Ministers of Slovenia and Croatia, Ciril Smrkolj and Bozidar Pankretic, met. 21st LJUBLJANA - The act on the closure of the Trbovlje and Hrastnik coal mines took effect. 28th PODCETRTEK - Unofficial meeting of Slovene Economics Minister Joze Zagozen and Croatian Deputy Prime Minister Goran Granic about the Krsko N-plant. \par \par \uc2  AUGUST \par \par \uc2  1st LJUBLJANA - Most Slovene banks raised deposit interest rates for Slovene tolar deposits. 1st LJUBLJANA - The government dismissed President of the Krsko N-plant managing board Uros Korze and appointed Severin Maffi new President. Joze Koprivnikar was appointed Acting Director of the Agency for Energy. 2nd LJUBLJANA - A distinguished finance expert, Aristobulos De Juan, visited Slovenia. 3rd LJUBLJANA - The government decided to take economy measures to cover the budget deficit. 22nd LJUBLJANA - Secretary General of OPEC Rilwanu Lukman in Slovenia. 22nd LJUBLJANA - The electronic trading act took effect. 23rd LJUBLJANA - The act on consumer credits took effect. \par \par \uc2  SEPTEMBER \par \par \uc2  3rd MARIBOR - A meeting of the finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of Slovenia and Austria. 3rd VELENJE - Fire destroyed one of the plants of Gorenje, Slovenia's leading household appliances group. 3rd LASKO - The brewery Pivovarna Lasko purchased the company Prearada from Split, Croatia. 8th LJUBLJANA - The banks NLB, Tutunska (Macedonia) and NLB International Handelsbank agreed on partnership. 11th LJUBLJANA - The transfer of accounts from the Public Accounting Service to banks began. 14th LJUBLJANA - Slovenia and the European Commission signed a wine deal which stipulates mutual recognition of preferential quotas. 14th LJUBLJANA - Canadian Minister of International Trade Pierre Pettigrew in Slovenia. 14th SYDNEY - A business conference of Slovene companies on the sidelines of the Olympics. 18th LASKO - The brewery Pivovarna Lasko issued a takeover bid for the shares of Radenska, a mineral water producer. 19th LJUBLJANA - After two months the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. 21st LJUBLJANA - The government adopted a restructuring programme for the Slovene steel industry. 21st LJUBLJANA - The government appointed Vitoslav Tuerk Acting Director of ELES. 22nd KRANJ - Sava issued a takeover bid for the shares of Color. 23rd PRAGUE - Slovenia attended a regular annual meeting of the IMF and WB with the finance ministers of the EU candidate countries. 27th LJUBLJANA - The amended act which bans the production and trade of products containing asbestos took effect. \par \par \uc2  OCTOBER \par \par \uc2  3rd LJUBLJANA - The price-setting model for gas took effect. 6th BLED - Meeting of the Slovene-Croatian commission for economic co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 6th LJUBLJANA - The government dismissed Stojan Grilj from the post of Director of the National Tax Administration (DURS). 6th BRUSSELS - Slovenia became a full member of the European Environment Agency. 10th LJUBLJANA - Finance Minister Zvonko Ivanusic met with a government delegation from Montenegro. 10th LJUBLJANA - The Agency for Agriculture Markets and Development of Rural Areas received a licence, a prerequisite for the implementation of the SAPARD programme. 11th LJUBLJANA - The act on the national housing savings scheme took effect. 11th WARSAW - Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur attended an annual meeting of the CEFTA joint committee. 19th LJUBLJANA - Vitoslav Tuerk was appointed Director of ELES with a four-year term of office. 21st LJUBLJANA - The price-setting model for natural gas took effect. 27th LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  NOVEMBER \par \par \uc2  1th LJUBLJANA - The Slovene Steelworks become an associated member of Eurofer. 2nd LJUBLJANA - The Bank of Slovenia fixed a new base rate on the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. 7th LJUBLJANA - Police arrested Boris Sustar, a state secretary in the Ministry of Economic Activities, on charges of corruption. 8th BRUSSELS - The European Commission in its annual report criticised Slovenia for slow implementation of structural reforms. 11th LJUBLJANA - The prices of electrical energy up by four percent for households and two percent for industry. 14th LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. 14th LJUBLJANA - The Ljubljana and London Stock Exchanges signed a co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement. 15th WARSAW - A regular annual meeting of premiers of the CEFTA member countries. 16th LJUBLJANA/KRANJ - A business delegation from the FRY in Slovenia. 16th LJUBLJANA - A Macedonian business delegation in Slovenia. 16th LENTI - Slovenia and Hungary signed an agreement on border railway transport. 1th. LJUBLJANA -The government granted licences for the GSM 1800 services to Western Wireless, Mobitel and Simobil. 17th SARAJEVO - Governor of the Bank of Slovenia France Arhar attended a meeting of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the former Yugoslav successor states. 21st LJUBLJANA - The brewery Pivovarna Union acquired a majority stake in Fructal. 21st TRIESTE - The port of Koper was granted a licence for the management of the container berth in the Italian port of Trieste. 22nd BELGRADE - State Secretary Anton Grabeljsek from the Ministry of Economic Relations and Development met with Yugoslav Deputy Minister of Foreign Economic Relations Boran Karadjolo. 25. LJUBLJANA - A study conducted by a group of Slovene and foreign experts stated that the Krsko N-plant is earthquake-safe. 29th LJUBLJANA - The government took a number of measures to prevent BSE from spreading to Slovenia. A total ban was imposed on the import of cows and beef from the countries in which BSE has appeared. 30th LJUBLJANA - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates rose considerably between 1 December 1999 and 30 November 2000. The U.S. dollar rose by SIT 47.37 to SIT 242.34 and the euro by SIT 13.53 to SIT 210.35. The LJSE share index rose by 82.92 point or 4.77 percent to 1737.22 point. (konec)sk/ep/eho STA010 2000-12-09/12:39. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwc90064z\par }\page {\par\fs20\b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Stops Changing Exchange Rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 118 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 14 November (STA) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  stoped on Tuesday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by fixing the base exchange rate and the maximum difference. Commercial banks are now able to set selling and buying rates freely as of 14 November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said yesterday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began changing the base exchange rate for the euro and the maximum difference between the highest selling rate and the lowest buying rate on 27 October to direct the desired growth of the exchange rate. The base exchange rate has been changed four times. (konec)eho STA003 2000-11-14/09:28. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwbe005lb\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  News Roundup - Monday, 13 November. \b0\par\par\uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 13 November (STA) - Below is a roundup of major events on Monday, 13 November: \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Slovene President Milan Kucan is due to propose a candidate for prime minister to the parliament on Tuesday, Kucan's office said in a press release following his consultations with representatives of parliamentary parties. Tomorrow morning Kucan will meet the leader of the Liberal Democracy of Slovenia (LDS) Janez Drnovsek, the most likely candidate for a new prime minister given the distribution of seats in parliament after the October general elections, and a nearly completed coalition talks. \par \par \uc2  MARSEILLES - Slovene Foreign Minister Lojze Peterle and Defence Minister Janez Jansa attended a ministerial session of the Western European Union (WEU). This will probably be the last ministerial session of the WEU, as the majority of the tasks of this organisation are being progressively taken over by the European Union. Peterle and Jansa announced at the session Slovenia's intention to offer Slovene forces for the establishment of European military forces at the conference for the establishment of EU forces on 21 November in Brussels. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Elected to the post of Speaker of the National Assembly last Friday, the leader of the third largest parliamentary party, Borut Pahor, stressed in an interview for Delo that Parliament is no front, but a sovereign branch of power, and should consolidate its position in politics. ZLSD leader Pahor, a 37-year old political scientist who has been an MP for ten years, added: "I find it wrong that after ten years of parliamentary democracy some still strongly underestimate Parliament as a domain in which power is created." \par \par \uc2  WARSAW - Foreign Ministries of Poland, the Czech Republic, Slovakia, Hungary and Slovenia took part in a consultation organized by the Foreign Ministry of Poland, holding this year's presidency of Visegrad countries, reported the Slovene Embassy from the Polish capital. The participants mainly discussed situation in South Eastern Europe and the potential for regional co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by fixing the base exchange rate and the maximum difference. Commercial banks will be able to set selling and buying rates freely as of 14 November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. The Bank of Slovenia started changing the base exchange rate for the euro and the maximum difference between the highest selling rate and the lowest buying rate on 27 to direct the desired growth of the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - One of the major Zagreb-based Croatian banks is allegedly interested in merging with the Slovene commercial bank SKB, according to paper Finance. The Croatian bank would like to merge with the SKB as a member of an international finance group. Two most important Croatian banks, Zagrebacka banka and Privredna banka, both have close links with international financial institutions. The chairwoman of the SKB board, Cvetka Selsek, recently said that several foreign financial institutions were interested in the SKB, but did not wish to discuss potential merger details. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Slovenia's exports increased by 0.6 percent to US$ 773.59 million while imports dropped by 1.8 percent to US$ 831.31 million in September over September 1999. The deficit totaled US$ 57.71 million, while exports covered 93.1 percent of imports in September, the National Statistical Office said. Preliminary data provided by the Office show a 20 percent increase in exports and a 40 percent increase in imports in September over a month before. In the first nine months Slovenia's exports increased by 3.5 percent to US$ 6.51 billion and imports by 1.8 percent to US$ 7.55 billion so that the trade gap stood at US$ 1.04 billion and the exports-imports ratio at 86.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Prices of oil derivatives will drop on Tuesday. Retail prices of motor fuel will decrease by approximately six percent, reported both Slovene oil companies, Petrol and OMV Istrabenz. A decrease in retail prices of four to six percent will also affect diesel and fuel oil. \par \par \uc2  LONDON - Slovenia's promotion at the London Tourist Bourse, a major international event of its kind which kicked off today, is focussing on trekking in the Julian Alps and the Triglav National Park, backed by a presentation of a record-breaking achievement accomplished by Slovene Alpine skier Davo Karnicar, who has recently skied down the Mount Everest. The bourse has also been visited by Slovene Minister of Small Business and Tourism Janko Razgorsek, who took this opportunity to hold a series of informal meetings with his counterparts and representatives of the world tourist industry. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Slovene share index, SBI 20, dropped 0.35 percent to stand at 1722.71 points at today's close. (konec)eho/ep STA022 2000-11-13/18:58. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwbd005ll\par }\page {\par\fs20\b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Will Stop Changing  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange \highlight\uc2  Rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 120 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 13 November (STA) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by fixing the base exchange rate and the maximum difference. Commercial banks will be able to set selling and buying rates freely as of 14 November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Slovenia started changing the base exchange rate for the euro and the maximum difference between the highest selling rate and the lowest buying rate on 27 to direct the desired growth of the exchange rate. The base exchange rate has been changed four times. (konec)ep STA016 2000-11-13/15:29. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwbd005kg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
