\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia \highlight\uc2  ends  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 85 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2000\par 14:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would end its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised the number of tolars which commercial banks pay to obtain euros five times since October 27. \par \par \uc2  Commercial banks which are participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment have to buy euros from companies at a minimum rate of 210.3 tolars and sell them at a maximum rate of 211.2 tolars. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbd027cj\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of the Slovene Economy from 28 October to 3 November. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,075 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 05 November (STA) - Slovene motorists experienced what is becoming a familiar story this year: another increase in the prices of petrol. The tenth rise in petrol prices this year alone, saw most types of petrol, with the exception of diesel and heating oil, increase in price by a good 5 percent. The reason behind all of this was the rising U.S. dollar, which, did however, fall SIT 11.17 during the week to stand at SIT 239.88. Following all the increases in the price of petrol, among other things, Slovenia's year-to-year inflation in October stood at 9 percent. \par \par \uc2  CHRONOLOGY: MONDAY, 30 October LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a bid to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expressed belief that the growth of the exchange rate was still too low. The base exchange rate was fixed at SIT 210.40 (previous Friday it was set at SIT 210.25) for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. The measure took effect on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  WEDNESDAY, 1 November LJUBLJANA - Following several years of negotiations, the Slovene Steelworks (SZ) became an associate member of the European Confederation of Iron and Steel Industries (Eurofer). As STA had found out from a member of the SZ board, the chief advantage of membership is being in continuous contact with 95 percent of West European steel makers associated in the Brussels-based confederation. LJUBLJANA - The price of natural gas from the fixed underground network increased to stand at SIT 41.07 from SIT 39.48. The rise in the U.S. dollar stands behind this latest increase. This price increase only affected industry and distributors and did not concern household users. \par \par \uc2  THURSDAY, 2 November LJUBLJANA - Prices of petrol increased at filling stations around Slovenia, it was reported from the two largest petrol companies in Slovenia, Petrol and OMV Istrabenz. The latest price increase, which saw the price of the most widely used 95-octane Eurosuper increase by 5.7 percent to stand at SIT 162.60 (0.78 euro), comes about due to the rising U.S. dollar. ZAGREB - The new leadership of the Croatian and Slovene national electrical distributors HEP and ELES met, with the aim to re-establish the traditionally good relations which have been suspended by the unresolved status of the Krsko Nuclear Plant (NEK). \par \par \uc2  FRIDAY, 3 November LJUBLJANA - Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third time in a week. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Central Bank Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market Again The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again changed the base exchange rate on Friday, which is the third \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a week. The new base exchange rate for the euro is SIT 210.51. The difference between the base rate and the highest selling rate or the lowest buying rate must not exceed 0.2 percent. All three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  come in a bid to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, which according to the bank is still too low. \par \par \uc2  TAXATION Tax Office Issues Orders on Income Tax Statements By the end of September the Slovene Tax Administration had received a total of 1,139,059 income tax statements for 1999. At the end of October the Administration had issued orders for 98.9 percent of these statements. According to the orders, 14.7 percent of tax payers will have to make supplementary payments, 67.22 percent will receive a return, while 18.08 percent will neither receive a return or have to make supplementary payments. The average supplementary payment stands at SIT 73,742, while the average return stands at SIT 36,263. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Brewery Carries Out Takeover Bid for Mineral Water Producer Radenska Slovene brewery Pivovarna Lasko had successfully concluded its takeover bid for mineral water producer Radenska after it gained 73.73 percent of Radenska's shares in the period from 18 September to 27 October. This had brought Pivovarna Lasko's total ownership stake in Radenska to 83.93 percent. Officials of both companies expressed belief that the takeover and the consequent joint appearance on the market will bring better results in addition to an greater market share. Pivovarna Lasko informed Radenska of its intent of purchasing the majority stake in the company on 6 September. At an extraordinary general meeting on 24 October, shareholders of Pivovarna Lasko took a decision on the exchange of Pivovarna Lasko's shares for that of Radenska at a ratio of 1 to 1.7. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS October Inflation at 0.6 Percent Prices stabilized in October after having risen considerably in September. The monthly inflation rate in October amounted to 0.6 percent, which is still slightly up over October 1999. Consumer prices have risen by 7.6 percent since the beginning of the year and gained nine percent in a year-on-year comparison. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Ljubljana Stock Exchange Sees Low Turnover The turnover at the Ljubljana Stock Exchange totaled SIT 1.5 billion (7.1 million) last week. The turnover with securities at the official market accounted for 43 percent of the overall turnover. The share index SBI 20 remained practically unchanged, closing the week at 1,680.89 points. The authorised investment companies' index PIX increased by 1.56 percent to stand at 1375.21 points, while the free market index increased by 0.05 percent to 1473.06 points. Most turnover at the free market was recorded with company shares (SIT 396 million/euro 1.8 million), followed by authorised investment companies' shares (SIT 296 million/euro 1.4 million), bonds (SIT 142 million/euro 676,000) and pension vouchers (SIT 17 million/euro 81,000). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  Drastic Fall for U.S. Dollar A roundup of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as they stood against the Slovene tolar at the end of this week, can be seen below: &#60;pre> \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro\tab \tab     SIT 209.44\tab  up  0.14\par U.S. dollar\tab    SIT 239.88     down 11.47\par Swiss franc\tab    SIT 136.97     down  1.48\par Deustche mark\tab  SIT 107.08\tab  up  0.07\par Austrian schilling  SIT  15.22\tab  up  0.01\par 100 Italian lira    SIT  10.81\tab  up  0.01\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par &#60;/pre> (konec)gj STA005 2000-11-05/12:26.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwb50055l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  News Roundup - Monday, 30 October. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,053 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 30 October (STA) - Below is a roundup of major events on Monday, 30 October: \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Slovenia is pleased with a heavy turnout at the Kosovo local elections, and is convinced that despite persistent problems the elections represent a step towards democratisation and stabilisation of the province. This was written in a Foreign Ministry press release. The ministry also expressed content over the facts that during the elections no major incidents occurred and that the elections were carried out in line with international democratic standards, the ministry said. Moreover, the ministry expects that such conditions will be created as soon as possible so as to enable all eligible voters to go to the polls. \par \par \uc2  PORTOROZ - A two-day OSCE seminar on trust and security building measures in the Mediterranean opened with an address by Foreign Minister Lojze Peterle, who attached the key importance in building trust and security among nations to what he called "the culture of transparency". OSCE Secretary General Jan Kubis and the President of OSCE Parliamentary Assembly Adrian Severin, who addressed the meeting as well, stressed the need to consolidate the ties among the countries members of the Organisation for Peace and Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe and Mediterranean countries. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The LDS, a party with 34 seats in parliament, resumed informal talks on the formation of a new government coalition. Talks are being held today with the Pensioners' Party, which has four seats, the Party of the Slovene Youth, also four seats, and the United List of Social Democrats, which has eleven seats in parliament. The DeSUS revleaed that "the contents of informal talks with the LDS on the formation of a new government coalition have been conducted so as to expect that the DeSUS might enter a coalition," its President Janko Kusar said. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - On the eve of the Reformation Day, a central ceremony will be held in the Slovene capital later in the evening. Slovenia, a country whose population is mainly Catholic, remembers the Protestant Reformation movement inspired by Luther and embodied in Slovenia in Protestant priest Primoz Trubar (1508-1586). It was Trubar who published the first Slovene book 450 years ago. According to Senior of the Evangelical Church in Slovenia Geza Ernisa, the Reformation Day is an occasion to regard the holiday not merely as a church holiday, but a holiday of all Slovenes. "It is right for Slovenia to pay a lot of attention to the holiday not only to mark Trubar's memory but to reevaluate the impact of his work on the present situation," said Ernisa. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Only a fortnight after this year's parliamentary elections Delo published a monthly opinion poll in which the LDS won the support of 38.6 percent of those questioned, which is over two percent more than in the 15 october elections and considerably more than in the survey a month ago, when the LDS enjoyed the support of 19 percent of those questioned. According to the daily's commentator, the situation is different in the case of other parliamentary parties as these got much lower support in the survey than they did in the elections, while all improved their rating compared to the survey published by Delo a month ago. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in a bid to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes that the current growth of the exchange rate is still too low. The base exchange rate was fixed today at SIT 210.40 (on Friday it was set at SIT 210.25) for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. The measure takes effect on 2 November. The base exchange rate and the maximum difference serve as the basis to calculate the lowest purchase price and the highest selling price at which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold by banks bound by contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These banks will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lowest price of SIT 209.9792 (SIT 209.8295 set on Friday) for one euro. The rates apply to the market on which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purchased and sold by companies \par \par \uc2  BRUSSELS - Two Slovene environmental projects will be partly funded from the ISPA, the fund through which the EU helps ten Central European EU candidate countries in the fields of transport and environment. This was approved today by the fund. Slovenia will use the funds for a new purifying plant for waste waters in the city of Celje, 100 kilometres northeast of Ljubljana, and for modernisation of the sewage system in Lendava, northeastern Slovenia. The ISPA fund will allocate 8.84 million euros for the cleaning device worth 14.74 million euros, and 5.12 million euros for the other project in the value of 11.56 million euros. Together with the Phare and SAPARD programmes, the ISPA is one of the main three instruments of financial aid provided by the EU to Central European candidate countries for EU membership. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Slovenia's Trade and Investment Office was presented 15,000 D-marks' worth technical equipment by a representative of the German Embassy, Michael Herrmann. A laptop and a projector were donated by the German government under a bilateral advisory programme called Transform and will be used for Slovenia's promotion abroad, said Office Director Matej Kovac, who accepted the gift. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Prices stabilized in October after having risen considerably in September. The monthly inflation rate in October amounted to 0.6 percent, which is still slightly up over October 1999. Consumer prices have risen by 7.6 percent since the beginning of the year and gained nine percent in a year-on-year comparison, the National Statistical Office said. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Slovene share index, SBI 20, has dropped 0.26 percent since Friday to stand at 1676.40 points at today's close on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange. \par \par \uc2  LIMA - Slovenia's Miro Kregar has won the triathlon of Inkaman on Lake Titicaca, the world's largest lake, which separates Peru from Bolivia, and thus repeated his success of 1998. He secured the victory in a 38-kilometre long cycling race via a 4,150-metre high mountain pass. (konec)ep/eho STA021 2000-10-30/18:56. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwau005nd\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  BS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  on the  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange \highlight\uc2  Market Again. \b0\par\par\uc2 311 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 30 October (STA) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BS) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday in a bid to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes that the current growth of the exchange rate is still too low. The base exchange rate was fixed at SIT 210.40 (on Friday it was set at SIT 210.25) for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. The measure takes effect on 2 November. \par \par \uc2  The base exchange rate and the maximum difference serve as the basis to calculate the lowest purchase price and the highest selling price at which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold by banks bound by contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These banks will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lowest price of SIT 209.9792 (SIT 209.8295 set on Friday) for one euro. The rates apply to the market on which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purchased and sold by companies. \par \par \uc2  In exchange offices the exchange rate for the Deutsche mark calculated in euros can be up to 0.2 percent higher than the highest selling price or lower than the lowest purchase price. In the case of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the other members of the European Economic and Monetary Union, Switzerland, the U.K. and the U.S. the maximum difference is 0.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Slovenia will change the base exchange rate according to trends on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the domestic money market and in agreement with the objectives of its monetary policy. The exchange rate change has to be respected by all 22 commercial banks that signed a contract on co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2000. (konec)eho STA018 2000-10-30/17:14. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwau005ms\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of the Slovene Economy from 21 to 27 October. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 29 October (STA) - Slovene bank Abanka sold shares of another Slovene bank, SKB, last Tuesday. Austrian bank Raiffeisen Zentralbank however told Austrian business daily, Wirtschaftsblatt, that it had bought 11 percent of SKB shares for its clients on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange at the beginning of the week. On Thursday, Chairman of the SKB board, Cvetka Selsek, told Slovene daily Delo that SKB had been informed by Nova Ljubljanska banka (NLB) about Raiffeisen having 6.45 percent of SKB shares on a NLB fiduciary account. SKB is Slovenia's third largest bank in terms of its total assets, holding an over 11-percent market share. \par \par \uc2  CHRONOLOGY SATURDAY, 21 October \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Prices of liquefied gas and natural gas went up 5.42 percent and 9 percent, respectively. \par \par \uc2  MONDAY, 23 October \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The 10th information technology show Infos 2000 began, featuring 139 exhibitors. Winding up on Friday, the show mainly focussed on e-commerce, information technology in public administration, knowledge for development, challenges of e-technology and information infrastructure. \par \par \uc2  TUESDAY, 24 October \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The management of the Pension and Disability Insurance Institute (ZPIZ) discussed the financial situation of the institute, establishing that in order to pay out pensions by the end of this year a loan of SIT 31 billion (euro 148.3 million) will have to be taken out. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - President of the European Court of Auditors Jan Karlsson concluded a two-day visit. On Monday he met with a delegation from the Slovene Court of Auditors to discuss Slovenia's preparations to enter the EU, and with the leadership of the Slovene Chamber of Commerce (GZS), who introduced him to the Slovene macro-economic policy and GZS's role in it. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The 7th international biannual fair Medilab opened, through Friday. Medilab is one of the most important professional shows in Slovenia, reflecting the development of Slovene and international laboratory technology, pharmaceutics, rehabilitation equipment and services. The show featured 368 exhibitors from 21 countries. \par \par \uc2  WEDNESDAY, 25 October \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Speaker of the Parliament Janez Podobnik presented to Minister of Transport and Communications Anton Bergauer an ISO 9002 certificate for quality. The Ministry of Transport and Communications and is the only Slovene ministry to have received the quality certificate. \par \par \uc2  THURSDAY, 26 October \par \par \uc2  BLED - Management College of Bled hosted a first European conference on knowledge management, which had been organized by Hanley Management College of Great Britain. The conference was an opportunity for participants from Europe and elsewhere to meet and exchange their views and experiences in management, development and improvements in information systems and technologies. \par \par \uc2  VELENJE - A 3rd two-day conference on human resource management began, organised by the Slovene business weekly, Gospodarski vestnik. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The government decided that the Slovene Development Corporation (SRD) will supply Paloma with fresh capital by changing this manufacturer of paper-based products for personal hygiene's debts into a share in the company. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The government also decided to supply fresh capital to the Fund for Regional Development Encouragement, the Slovene Export Corporation and the Fund for Small Business Development. \par \par \uc2  FRIDAY, 27 October \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - A Bled-based hotel was sold to a rubber manufacturer. A contract to sell Grand hotel Toplice Bled to Sava Kranj was signed with the Slovene Compensation Corporation, which brought lengthy efforts to denationalise and privatise the hotel to an end. By purchasing the company, Sava prevented it from going bankrupt. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Central Bank Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believed that the current growth of the exchange rate was too low. The base exchange rate had thus been fixed at SIT 210.25 for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. The base exchange rate and the maximum difference serve as the basis to calculate the lowest purchase price and the highest selling price at which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold by banks bound by contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These banks will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lowest price of SIT 209.8295 for one euro and sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at up to SIT 210.6705 for one euro as of 27 October. The rates apply to the market on which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purchased and sold by companies. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Gorenjska Banka's Total Assets up 18 Percent \par \par \uc2  Gorenjska banka made a gross profit of SIT 1.4 billion (6.69 million euros) in the first nine months of this year, which puts its return on capital at 8.9 percent. At the end of September the bank's total assets amounted to SIT 153.3 billion (733.4 million euros), up 18 percent from the end of 1999, when the bank placed 7th biggest bank on the Slovene market. \par \par \uc2  NLB Takes out Syndicated Loan of 140 Million Euros \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's largest commercial bank, Nova Ljubljanska banka (NLB), and a consortium of 27 foreign banks signed a contract on a mid-term syndicated loan of 140 million euros. This is the seventh syndicated loan taken out by NLB on the foreign money market. The bank will use about 30 percent of the funds to pay off the past loans, and the rest to finance export-oriented Slovene companies. The loan has to be paid off within five years at a variable interest rate called Euribor. \par \par \uc2  SKB Takes out Syndicated Loan of 50 Million Euros \par \par \uc2  Slovene bank SKB and a consortium of 13 foreign banks signed a contract on a long-term syndicated loan of 50 million euros. This is the seventh syndicated loan taken out by SKB on the foreign money market since 1994. The funds will be used to finance export-oriented Slovene companies. The loan was taken out at a raised variable interest rate Euribor of 0.40 percentage point. The bank did not wish to disclose other details about the loan. \par \par \uc2  INSURANCE MARKET Contract Signed Between Slovene First Pension Fund and Dutch Bank \par \par \uc2  A contract on long-term co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  was signed between Slovenia's First Pension Fund and the Dutch ABN AMRO Bank which allows the bank to purchase more than nine percent of First Pension Fund's shares. The partnership between the two companies will enable First Pension Fund to enter the international financial market; by using the Dutch bank's infrastructure, the Slovene company will reduce operating costs and improve the safety of investment, thus increasing long-term return of capital. Amsterdam-based ABN Amro Bank as a financial institution ranks 16th in the world and fifth in Europe, employing 110,000 persons in 76 branch offices world-wide. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Shareholders of Pivovarna Lasko Opt for Increase in Share Capital \par \par \uc2  Shareholders of one of Slovenia's two major breweries took a decision at an extraordinary meeting to raise the company's share capital by issuing fresh shares in the nominal value of SIT 2.2 billion (10.5 million euros). \par \par \uc2  Microsoft Slovenia Records Fall in PC Sales \par \par \uc2  The sales of Microsoft Slovenia products totaled US$ 13.4 million in the period from July 1999 to June 2000. The number of PCs sold in this period dropped from 84,000 in the same period a year earlier to 80,000. A fall in PC sales was recorded for the first time by Microsoft Slovenia. However, larger clients with which long-term enterprise agreements are signed went up. \par \par \uc2  POWER INDUSTRY Use of Electricity Up \par \par \uc2  A total of 7.8 billion of Kilowatt hours of electricity was used in Slovenia in the first nine months of this year, up 4.4 percent from the same period last year and 4.6 percent above the plan. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves Reach US$ 3.9 Billion at the End of August \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves totalled US$ 3.998 billion at the end of August. Foreign reserves thus decreased by US$ 115 million from a month before, which is still lower than the overall foreign debt by US$ 1.786 billion, which totaled US$ 5.784 billion on the last day of August, according to the latest issue of the Bulletin of the Bank of Slovenia. The bulk of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, US$ 2.893 billion, were the reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while the rest belonged to commercial banks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped by US$ 165 million, while those of commercial banks increased by US$ 48 million over the end of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Debt at US$ 5.8 Billion \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's long-term foreign debt totaled US$ 5.687 billion and short-term debt US$ 97 million. Of the long-term debt US$ 2.73 billion was public and state-guaranteed debt and the rest of US$ 2.957 billion was private debt. Slovenia's foreign debt increased by US$ 293 million over the end of 1999. Long-term debt was up by US$ 313 million and short-term debt down by US$ 20 million. \par \par \uc2  Industrial Output up 3.7 Percent in September \par \par \uc2  September saw a 22.1-percent increase in industrial output from August, with a yearly increase standing at 3.7 percent, preliminary data released by the National Statistical Office show. Industrial output was up 7.7 percent in the January-to-September period in comparison with the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia Visited by Over 1.5 Million Tourists in from January to September \par \par \uc2  In September, however, Slovene tourist resorts were visited by 190,353 tourists, up 12 percent from September 1999. The number of foreign tourists totalled 121,213, a rise of 24 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE High Turnover on Ljubljana Stock Exchange \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Share Index, SBI 20, gained 1.55 percent during last week and finished at 1680.79 points. The authorised investment companies' index, PIX, followed the upward trend as it posted a gain of 2.69 percent and finished the week at 1354.03. The free market index, IPT, fell sharply on the other hand, losing 2.45 percent during the week to stand at 1472.19. Stock-brokers reported a total of 6,683 deals during the week, totaling some 33.65 million euros, which is more than twice the turnover seen a week before. A total of 32 package deals amounted for some SIT 5.1 billion or 72 percent of the week's total turnover. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Up \par \par \uc2  The euro edged up SIT 0.17 to SIT 209.30 last week. The Swiss franc lost SIT 0.14 to SIT 138.46, the US dollar gained SIT 4.11 to SIT 251.36 tolarja, the D-mark increased by SIT 0.10 to SIT 107.02, the Austrian schilling by SIT 0.02 to SIT 15.21, and 100 Italian lira by SIT 0.01 to SIT 10.81. (konec)eho STA004 2000-10-29/13:12. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwat005ia\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  News Roundup - Thursday, 26 October. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 26 October (STA) - Below is a roundup of major events on Thursdaqy, 26 October: \par \par \uc2  BUCHAREST - Slovenia expects from the new authorities of Yugoslavia, a country which was admitted to the club of countries taking part in the Balkan Stability Pact on Thursday, to fulfil their promises and to respect international law as well as procedures applicable to membership of international organisations. Furthermore, the FRY is expected to resume talks on succession issues regarding the former Yugoslavia in which all successor states are in an equal position, STA was told by the deputy national coordinator for the Stability Pact, Jozica Puhar, the head of the Slovene delegation at a two-day Bucharest gathering focussing on human rights and democratisation. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The government approved aid to the people of Yugoslavia at its session. The proposal to send medicines, food and school requisites worth nearly DEM 47,000 to Yugoslavia was put forward by the Foreign Ministry and Slovenia's Red Cross. By sending the relief Slovenia wants to contribute to alleviating difficult conditions in Yugoslavia and consolidating democratic changes in the country, the Foreign Ministry said. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - Despite an agreement between the Slovene and Croatian police the number of illegal immigrants coming to Slovenia from Croatia has not fallen, Director General of the Slovene Police Marko Pogorevc wrote in a letter to the Croatian Deputy Interior Minister. The Slovene police has apprehended this week large groups of illegal immigrants along the border with Croatia. As soon as they were apprehended, the immigrants asked for asylum in Slovenia. Only on Wednesday more than 200 illegal immigrants were apprehended. Pogorevc also expressed hope that Croatian policemen will more active in preventing illegal migrations be in the future. \par \par \uc2  BUENOS AIRES - Two Slovene tourists were shot dead in an armed robbery in Bolivia on Tuesday, while one was injured and is being treated in hospital in the Bolivian capital, the Slovene Foreign Ministry confirmed for STA. Three Slovene tourists were riding horses in the wilds of Bolivia when they were joined by two men. They were all riding together for several hours when the two men surrounded the Slovene tourists and opened fire. One of the Slovene citizens pretended to be dead and was later found by local people who took him to hospital. His fellows were shot dead. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Slovene Diplomats' Trade Union criticized the personnel policy of the Foreign Ministry, stating in a press release that diplomacy should be professional in order to be able to successfully represent the interests of the country. Diplomats should be highly skilled and creative experts, the trade union wrote, adding that diplomatic posts should not be rewards for past work or even a place "to deposit redundant members of political parties". \par \par \uc2  THE VATICAN - A mass was celebrated before 4,000 pilgrims who took part in a Slovene pilgrimage to Rome in the Holy Year 2000. After the mass, which was offered in St. Peter's Basilica by Ljubljana Archbishop and Slovene Metropolitan Franc Rode, pilgrims were joined by the Pope John Paul II, who addressed them in Slovene. Slovene Catholics' pilgrimage to Rome in the Holy Year 2000 began on Tuesday, it is attended by eight Slovene bishops and will wind up tomorrow. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market tomorrow to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes that the current growth of the exchange rate is too low. The base exchange rate has been fixed at SIT 210.25 for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. The base exchange rate and the maximum difference serve as the basis to calculate the lowest purchase price and the highest selling price at which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold by banks bound by contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These banks will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lowest price of SIT 209.8295 for one euro and sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at up to SIT 210.6705 for one euro as of 27 October. The rates apply to the market on which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purchased and sold by companies. \par \par \uc2  PIRAN - Competitive pressure will increase considerably in Central and Eastern European countries once they have become EU member countries. Moreover, the social element of economy will no longer be present, for which reason EU candidate countries should put social policy before integration into the EU, with trade unions having to play a central role in accession, said Wolfgang Weinz, coordinator for Central and Eastern Europe, IUF, International Union of Food, Agriculture, Hotel, Restaurant, Catering, Tobacco and Allied Workers' Associations, at the beginning of a two-day meeting of the Slovene trade unions of agriculture and food industry, and of tourism which began on the Slovene coast. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - The Slovene share index, SBI 20, edged up 0.69 percent to stand at 1681.52 points at today's close. \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY - Athlete Dragica Lapornik won a bronze medal in shot put, securing Slovenia a third medal at the 2000 Paraolympics. \par \par \uc2  SARAJEVO - Two actors from a Ljubljana-based theatre ensemble received the Gold Laurel Wreath for best actress and best actor in Moliere's Misanthrope, a Slobodan Unkovski-directed production which was shown at the Sarajevo festival of experimental theatre, MESS 2000, last Sunday. Polona Juh and Jernej Sugman from the Slovene National Theatre (SNG) were awarded on Thursday evening in Sarajevo. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - A retrospective of one of Slovenia's finest minimalist artists opened at the Museum of Modern Art featuring 100 paintings and drawings from the past 30 years. Gustav Gnamus, a full professor at the Ljubljana Academy of Fine Arts since 1990, is a recipient of the 1992 Preseren Fund Prize and the 1996 Jakopic Award for outstanding achievement in fine arts. The artist, born in 1941, studied at the Ljubljana Academy of Fine Arts with several most distinguished Slovene modernist, including Gabrijel Stupica (1913-1990). After his first solo exhibition in 1969, Gnamus has exhibited a lot as part of group exhibitions at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  KRANJ - Uros Zupan, one of the most promising Slovene poets, is the recipient of the 2000 Jenko Award, a prize awarded by the Slovene Writers' Association for best collection of poems from the past two years. The prize-winning ceremony will take place tonight at the Preseren Theatre in the city of Kranj, 25 kilometres north of Ljubljana. Zupan, a lyric poet par excellence who was awarded for his collection of poems entitled "A Tree and a Sparrow", a kind of a cut through ten years of his career, was chosen by a five-member jury. \par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA - A Slovene mountaineer has died attempting to climb Dorje Lakpa in the Himalayas. Andrej Zaman died just below the mountain peak, the Slovene Mountaineers' Association said, but had no further information as yet available. (konec)sk/eho STA027 2000-10-26/18:59. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwaq005jv\par }\page {\par\fs20\b  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  to  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  on the  \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange \highlight\uc2  Market. \b0\par\par\uc2 303 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 26 October (STA) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Friday to influence the growth of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  believes that the current growth of the exchange rate is too low. The base exchange rate has been fixed at SIT 210.25 for one euro with the maximum difference of 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  The base exchange rate and the maximum difference serve as the basis to calculate the lowest purchase price and the highest selling price at which foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sold by banks bound by contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . These banks will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the lowest price of SIT 209.8295 for one euro and sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at up to SIT 210.6705 for one euro as of 27 October. The rates apply to the market on which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is purchased and sold by companies. \par \par \uc2  In exchange offices the exchange rate for the Deutsche mark calculated in euros can be up to 0.2 percent higher than the highest selling price or lower than the lowest purchase price. In the case of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the other members of the European Economic and Monetary Union, Switzerland, the U.K. and the U.S. the maximum difference is 0.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Slovenia will change the base exchange rate according to trends on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and the domestic money market and in agreement with the objectives of its monetary policy. The exchange rate change has to be respected by all 22 commercial banks that signed a contract on co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March 2000. (konec)ep STA017 2000-10-26/16:00. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dwaq005ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of the Slovene Economy from 16 to 22 September. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,567 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2000\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 24 September (STA) - At Thursday's session the government conceded to the demands of Slovene transport workers and reduced the petrol tax on diesel oil by 11.3 percent, thus preventing protests around the country. At its session the government also decided to dismiss the Acting General Manager of Slovenia's electricity supplier (ELES) Vekoslav Korosec and replaced him with Vitoslav Turk, the head of the trade department in ELES. According to the electricity supplier, the decision to dismiss Korosec is based on changes to the act on the government and to the ELES statute, while Korosec believes that the move is purely of a political nature. \par \par \uc2  CHRONOLOGY: SATURDAY, 16 September LJUBLJANA - The average selling price of natural gas from the underground network increased by seven percent to stand at SIT 36.22 per litre. \par \par \uc2  MONDAY, 18 September LJUBLJANA - Robert S. Kaplan, the world's leading management authority was on visit to Slovenia upon the invitation of Gospodarski vestnik publishing house and company Aktiva Group. The method of measuring business excellence called the Balanced Scorecard (BSC), Kaplan's brainchild, was presented to over 300 Slovene economists and businessmen. The BSC has been one of the most influential business ideas for the past 75 years. LJUBLJANA - The Slovene Development Corporation (SRD) finished its marathon session at which the annual report for 1999 and \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first five months of 2000 was discussed. At the session the conditions in certain Slovene companies currently under SRD control was also discussed. \par \par \uc2  TUESDAY, 19 September LJUBLJANA - Prices of petrol increased around the whole country. The price of the most widely used 95-Octane Eurosuper increased by SIT 4.3 to SIT 153.8. LJUBLJANA - The 11th Furniture Fair in Ljubljana opened its doors. The guest speaker, Minister for Economic Affairs Joze Zagozen, stressed that the Slovene wood industry needs to create export-orientated products, which will assist to increase competitiveness and the level of value added on foreign markets. LJUBLJANA - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  completed its two-month \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during which it set a base exchange rate and a maximum ratio between the purchase and the selling rates. The 21 banks which signed a co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March will therefore be able to set their purchase and selling exchange rates independently. The aim of such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to curtail rapid increases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the growth of exchange rates was estimated to be too fast at the time of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  WEDNESDAY, 22 September MARIBOR - The management of the Maribor-based bank Nova KBM signed a borrowing contract to the value of 5 million euros with the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD). \par \par \uc2  THURSDAY, 21 September LJUBLJANA - At its weekly session the government decided to reduce excise duties on diesel oil by 11.3 percent and thus kept the promise given by the government negotiating team to the Union of Slovene Transporters (SAS) earlier this week. Other demands of the bus and lorry drivers were also met, so the SAS ceased all their activities regarding public protests and road blockages. LJUBLJANA - At its weekly session the government decided to dismiss acting General Manager of Slovenia's electricity supplier (ELES) Vekoslav Korosec. In turn the Ministry of Economic Affairs proposed that Vitoslav Turk, the head of the trade department in ELES, should replace Korosec \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ceases \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday completed a two-month period of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by setting a base exchange rate and a maximum ratio between the purchase and the selling rates. The 21 banks which signed a co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  contract with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March will therefore be able to set their purchase and selling exchange rates independently. The aim of such an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to curtail rapid increases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the growth of exchange rates was estimated to be too fast at the time of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  BANKS NKBM Signs a Borrowing Contract with EBRD The management of the Maribor-based bank Nova KBM on Wednesday signed a borrowing contract to the value of 5 million euros with the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD). The NKBM will grant favourable investment loans to entrepreneurs and companies with up to one hundred employees for the financing of new business projects. The interest rates for the loans with the redemption period of up to five years will be EURO LIBOR + 2.5 percent. Clients can get a loan to the value of up to 125,000 euros. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Tyre Producer Issues Bid to Take Over Chemical Company Sava, a Slovene tyre producer, on Friday issued a bid to purchase shares of the chemical company Color Medvode. The takeover bid follows Sava's new strategy to launch a new line of activities in the company focusing on the rubber industry, chemistry, trade and tourism. The bid refers to 845,832 ordinary registered shares, 104,559 of which are already owned by Sava. The share's nominal value is SIT 2,000 (9.6 euros). Sava has offered five of its ordinary registered shared for 19 Color shares, or SIT 4,000 for one Color share. The offer closes on 20 October. \par \par \uc2  Telekom Shareholders to Receive Dividends of 1.5 Euro per Share Shareholders of Telekom, Slovenia's telephone services provider, adopted a decision at a general meeting on Thursday, to pay out dividends for 1999 in the amount of SIT 2.026 billion (9.7 million euro) or SIT 310 (1.5 euro) per share. Gross profits from 1995 with a revalued adjustment in the amount of SIT 76.2 million, gross profits from 1996 in the amount of SIT 612.4 million, and SIT 1.337 billion gross profits in 1997 will be used for dividends. Half of gross profits from 1999 in the amount of SIT 3.949 billion will remain undistributed, while the other half has already been placed in company reserves. \par \par \uc2  Emo Receives ISO 14001 Ecology Standard Certificate Celje-based (76km east of Ljubljana) tools' manufacturer Emo Tools received the ISO 14001 ecology standard certificate. DM 350,000 have been spent in an effort to acquire the "green standard" certificate, which was launched back in 1998, said Renato Fijavz, in charge of the office for quality at Emo Tools, and added that the main reason behind this undertaking was to raise ecological awareness of the employees. \par \par \uc2  Brest Furniture Company Receives ISO 14001 Ecology Green Standard Certificate The furniture producer Brest presented the first electronic shop in Slovenia's furniture industry and four new furniture lines and thus presented itself at the 11th Furniture Fair in Ljubljana, which opened its doors on Tuesday. The Cerknica-based (some 40 km south of Ljubljana) furniture company, employing around 400 workers, also received the ISO 14001 green standard certificate. It is one of the first Slovene companies to receive such quality certificate for improving ecological conditions. \par \par \uc2  INDUSTRY Government Adopts Programme for Privatisation of Steel Industry At its session on Thursday the government adopted a programme for the privatisation of the Slovene steel industry. The programme envisages state assistance until 2003 to the amount of 210 million euros. The programme foresees the privatisation of core companies in the steel industry by 2002, while non-core companies would be privatised by the end of the year. The number of employees in the core companies of the Slovene steel industry is set to fall from 3000 to 2500. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS 11.9-Percent Unemployment Rate in July The national statistics office recorded a labour force of 874,554 people in July, 770,167 of whom were persons in employment, a drop of 0.3 percent from June, but a 0.9 percent increase on a year-to-year comparison. According to the statistics office, the level of registered unemployment stood at 11.9 percent in July, a rise of 0.1 percentage point over June. \par \par \uc2  STOCK MARKET As many as 6,654 deals were closed last week on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange. The overall turnover totaled SIT 2.76 billion (13 million euros), one billion of which accounted for lot deals. Most share indices were down last week, as the SBI share index lost 0.77 percent to stand at 1666.62 points at Friday's close. The free market index IPT was up 1.05 percent to 981.06 points, while the authorised investment companies' index PIX decreased by 0.35 percent to finish at 1273.11 points on Friday. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\b0 \highlight\uc2  The Slovene tolar strengthened against the U.S. dollar during the week; the dollar stood at SIT 239.61, down SIT 1.63, on Friday. However, the tolar weakened against the major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 : it was down SIT 0.54 at SIT 136.88 against the Swiss franc and down SIT 0.18 at SIT 208.44 against the euro. One Deutsche mark stood at SIT 106.57 up SIT 0.09, while the Austrian schilling strengthened by SIT 0.02 to be worth SIT 15.15. 100 Italian lira remained level against the tolar at SIT 10.76. (konec)gj STA010 2000-09-24/16:24. \par \par \uc2  (c) 2000 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010817dw9o006kd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic