\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia \highlight\uc2  to end tolar rate  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 1999\par 15:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Aug 25 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it would discontinue imposing an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling rate for euros and German marks implemented on July 19. \par \par \uc2  "From August 26, the chosen banks are no longer obliged to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling rate," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  From July 19, banks included in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to companies at a maximum of 197.37 tolars to the euro and at 100.91 tolars to the mark. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ended because commercial banks' rates had already started falling. \par \par \uc2  This week banks sold euros to companies at an average of 197.16 tolars and around 100.80 tolars per mark. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no sense any longer, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was still limiting growth of rates which were already falling," said Dusan Kosanovic, head of treasury at Societe Generale. \par \par \uc2  He said the rates were likely to continue falling over the short-term but would become more volatile in September when corporate demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to increase. ((Novica Mihajlovic, Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-546, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv8p01zjn\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 17 through 23 July. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 25 July (STA) - The government endorsed an increase in the prices of oil derivatives and liquidised natural gas at a session on Thursday. The price of petrol increased by 9.9 percent, the VAT excluded, while the retail price including the VAT rose by 3.4 percent to SIT 123.9 (DM 1.2). \par \par \uc2  As of 24 July a litre of 91-octane unleaded petrol costs SIT 110, a litre of 95-octane SIT 110.6 and a litre of 98-octane unleaded petrol SIT 115.80. The price of liquidised gas in bottles was raised by 8.9 percent from SIT 127.89 to SIT 139.27 per one kilogram. The estimated impact on the consumer prices index is 0.15 percent in case of petrol and 0.003 percent in case of liquidised gas. Major reasons for the price raises are an increase in the exchange rate of the U.S. dollar and in a surge of crude oil prices on the world market. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION SID Signed an Agreement with Macedonian Development Bank \par \par \uc2  Slovene Export Corporation (SID) and Macedonian Development Bank signed a bilateral cooperation agreement aimed at strengthening bilateral economic relations. According to SID chairman Marjan Kramar, the agreement represents a basis for further cooperation between the two countries that share the same goal - economic development. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia Intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Market \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BS) introduced on 19 July an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales rate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Six banks - Abanka, SKB banka, LB Banka Zasavje, Banka Velenje, Banka Koper and Nova Ljubljanska banka - can sell \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at SIT 197.37 to the euro and SIT 100.91 to the German mark at maximum. The exchange rate in exchange offices can be 0.2 percent out at the most for the German mark and 0.4 percent for other EMU countries, Switzerland, Great Britain and the U.S. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves at 4.5 Billion in End-May \par \par \uc2  Slovenia had US$ 4.458 billion of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves at the end of May. The reserves decreased by US$ 151.3 million m/m, coming short of the nation's total external debt of US$ 5.224 billion by US$ 786 million, the Bank of Slovenia reported in the June issue of its Bulletin. Reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  made up US$ 3.438 billion and those of other banks US$ 1020 million. \par \par \uc2  Exchange Offices Turn Over DM 735 Million in a Month \par \par \uc2  Exchange offices in Slovenia turned over almost 735 million German marks last June. The foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased amounted to DM 369 million and exceeded the amount of the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold by DM 3 million. June's turnover marked a rise of 4.7 percent (DM 33.3 million) in a year-on-year comparison. Bureaux de change turned over DM 3.706 billion in the first half of this year, the purchase totalling DM 30 million and surmounting the sale by 1.6 percent. The turnover was down by DM 402 million over the same period last year. The mean exchange rate for the German mark averaged SIT 99.81 last June. Last year about 330 exchange points across the country turned over almost DM 8.2 billion. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Dolenjska Banka Successful in the First Half of 1999 \par \par \uc2  The Dolenjska banka bank made a profit in the value of SIT 330 million in the first six 1999 months, which is slightly more than a half of the 1998 profit. The total assets grew up by 2.7 percent to SIT 79.5 billion in the same period. With an approximately three-percent market share, the bank is placed ninth among Slovene commercial banks. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Talks on N-Plant Carried On Despite HEP's Statement \par \par \uc2  Talks between Slovenia and Croatia on a settlement of outstanding issues related to the jointly owned Krsko N-plant are under way at full speed at all levels despite a statement by a senior manager of the Croatian electricity supplier (HEP) that HEP would like to sell its share in the plant, the Slovene Economics Ministry told STA. The ministry explained it had not received from HEP any official information about the sell of its share in the Krsko plant. According to the ministry, both Slovenia and Croatia would like to sign the agreement on the plant which would define the status and ownership of the plant as soon as possible. The ministry added that the Croatian partner in negotiations is not HEP, but the government. President of HEP Board Damir Begovic informed the public about his company's intention to sell its share in the plant at a press conference in Zagreb. He said the opening price would be around one billion German marks. \par \par \uc2  WAGES Minimum Salary Rises by 6.3 Percent \par \par \uc2  The Economic and Social Council raised the minimum salary by 6.3 percent to SIT 72,521 (DM 725). The increase takes effect from 1 August. Trade union representatives stressed the importance of establishing adequate salaries ratio between manufacturing and non-manufacturing sectors. The Economic and Social Council decided to launch discussion and analysis of the ratios this autumn in order to eliminate any discord with the agreement on salaries policy. \par \par \uc2  Telekom Received the SAP CCC Certificate \par \par \uc2  The Slovene telecommunications company Telekom was the first company in Slovenia to receive the SAP CCC (Customer Competence Center) Certificate. SAP is an integrated information system backing information support in companies, used by more than 200 companies of the kind worldwide. The SAP system is used in approximately 20 Slovene companies. \par \par \uc2  PRIVATISATION SIT 101 Billion Available for Privatisation Gap \par \par \uc2  The total value of property available to fill the privatisation gap amounts to SIT 101.1 billion (DM 1 billion). As the overall privatisation deficit amounts to SIT 179.7 billion according to the latest government estimates the difference will be covered by financial assets of the state, completion of the decree on securing state property and by increasing the share of investments of the Slovene Development Corporation (SRD). Government committees have already discussed a bill on state property privatisation, which is expected to be submitted to the parliament soon. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM Slovene Tourist Board Opens Information Office in Zagreb \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Tourist Board has recently opened its information office in the Croatian capital, Zagreb, to enhance its promotional activity in Croatia, a country from which a lot of foreign tourists spend their holidays in Slovenia. As to tourist nights spent in Slovenia, Croatia places fourth (211,863 in 1998) after Germany, Italy and Austria. To reduce the cost of promotion, the board has opened the office in Zagreb in collaboration with the Zagreb branch office of Slovenia's tour organiser Kompas Turizem. To attract foreign tourists in the 1999/2000 season, the board has already decided on three major promotions (in Vienna, Munich and Milan). Moreover, it will use six information offices (London, Zurich, New York, Rotterdam, Budapest and Zagreb) for additional campaigns and the provision of information about Slovenia. \par \par \uc2  Nearly 10-Percent Drop in Tourist Income in January-to-May Period \par \par \uc2  The influx of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from tourism fell 9.9 percent in the first five months of 1999 from the same period last year, the latest data from the Tourist and Restaurant Business Association at the Chamber of Commerce and Industry (GZS) show. All five months saw a drop in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but a 3-percent rise in foreign tourist nights was recorded in the period before the war broke out in Yugoslavia. In March, the gambling industry recorded the sharpest decline of over 25 percent from a year earlier. Interestingly, the influx of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from foreign tourists into Slovene tourist companies was above last year's figures. Tourist companies' revenues make up 17.8 percent of the entire inflow of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from tourism into the country (14.9 percent in 1998). Foreigners continue to buy less in Slovene duty-free shops; a drop of 7.8 percent was recorded in the first five months from the same period a year earlier. With the number of Slovene tourists up 9 percent in the first five months, th total number of tourists only fell by 2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Tourist Industry Figures Turn Downwards at Bled \par \par \uc2  Hotels and camps in the Alpine resort of Bled (northwest) recorded a 16-percent decrease in bookings in the first half of July over the same period last year, the local tourist association reported. From 1 to 18 July tourist facilities in Bled accommodated 24,723 persons, among which 22,099 were foreigners and 2,624 Slovenes. The number of tourist nights by foreign subjects decreased by 19 percent in a year-on-year comparison, while the number of those by domestic tourists increased by 41 percent. \par \par \uc2  SHOPS The Sales Begin \par \par \uc2  The majority of Slovene clothing and shoe shops and department stores started the sales on 20 July. The sale prices are 20 to 40 percent of the normal price on average. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE LSE Supervisory Board Meets \par \par \uc2  The Supervisory Board of the Ljubljana Stock Exchange (LSE) discussed and adopted a report on the LSE \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the half-year LSE performance and on the project of a new stock exchange information system (BIS). \par \par \uc2  Dullness on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange \par \par \uc2  Trading on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange was calm last week. The SBI share index closed the week at 1770.66 points, up 0.81 points compared to last Friday; the BIO bond index lost 0.09 points to close the week at 106.76. From Monday to Wednesday, only SIT 160 million were turned over, while on Thursday the trade amounted to SIT 100 million due to two package deals (SIT 71 million with the Hoteli Bernardin bonds and SIT 31 million with the third issue SKB bonds). On Friday, the trade totalled SIT 1.223 billion due to package deals with the tenth issue treasury bonds in the value of SIT 707 million and with the Dolenjska banka shares in the value of SIT 356 million. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Higher Exchange Rates of Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  All major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw their exchange rates rise according to the Bank of Slovenia mean exchange rate over the past week. The Euro rose by SIT 0.3 to SIT 195.7, German mark added SIT 0.2 to SIT 100.1, and Swiss franc edged up by SIT 0.2 to SIT 121.9. Austrian schilling and Italian lira remained flat at SIT 14.2 and SIT 10.1 respectively. The U.S. dollar dropped by SIT 5.4 to SIT 186.3. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv7p002cy\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 10 through 16 July. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juli 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 18 July (STA) - The absence of a negotiated agreement on the Krsko Nuclear Power Plant (NEK) was claimed by official sources to be the main reason for postponing the meeting of Slovene and Croatian government delegations this week, at which the countries were to sign the NEK agreement and the bilateral property agreement. Following their meeting on Friday, Economics Ministers of Slovenia and Croatia, Tea Petrin and Nenad Porges, were positive about the progress made by negotiating teams, announcing the tams would settle the remaining problems over the next days. \par \par \uc2  First steps were also taken this week to close the notorious privatisation gap, the difference between the value of privatisation vouchers issued to population and that of the property to be privatised. The First Pension Fund Act took effect offering Privatisation Investment Fund (PIF) shareholders the option to exchange their shares for pension vouchers; the latter may be sold later on or invested to the First Pension Fund. According to the Association of Management Companies, which presented its view on the act on 12 July, the monthly rate is more favourable for older persons. The entry into force of the First Pension Act at the same started the three year period during which PIFs must transform to ordinary investment or joint stock companies. \par \par \uc2  Two weeks after the value added tax was introduced, the Finance Ministry estimated VAT's effects were as expected. Prices of food and non-alcoholic beverages rose by 2.63 percent and prices of restaurant services by 2.34 percent according to preliminary estimates, while the prices of those goods that should be cheaper because of lower tax rates in most cases did not change. Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur announced that government-controlled prices would be gradually adjusted by September. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Tuesday/13 July - Ljubljana: First Pension Fund Bill takes effect. Wednesday/14 July - Ljubljana: Meeting of Transport Ministers of Slovenia and Croatia, Anton Bergauer and Zeljko Luzavac. Wednesday/14 July - Zagreb: Export Corporation of Slovenia and the Croatian Bank for Reconstruction and Development sign cooperation agreement. Friday/16 July - Strmol: Working meeting of Economics Ministers of Slovenia and Croatia, Tea Petrin and Nenad Porges, and of negotiation groups for the Krsko Nuclear Power Plant (NEK). Friday/16 July: National Assembly passes Regional Development Promotion Act and the 1999 Government \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Agriculture Act. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION \par \par \uc2  Slovene-Ukrainian Commission on Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sign Protocol \par \par \uc2  The Slovene and Ukrainian delegations signed, on 16 July, a protocol of the first meeting of the Slovene-Ukrainian commission on co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in economy and trade. Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  between the two countries is relatively low, with the full volume of trade amounting to only US$ 58 million. This year's data regarding the growth in the past years even indicate a fall of the volume of trade which decreased almost by one half this year over the past year. However, the commission agreed there are still huge potentials to increase trade between the two countries, with medicines and pharmaceutical products being the area with the most possibilities for the co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of companies. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Export Corporation Signs Agreement on Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a Croatian Bank \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Export Corporation (SID) and the Croatian Bank for Reconstruction and Development (HBOR) signed an agreement on co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Zagreb on 14 July. The aim of the agreement is to provide support to export activities of the Slovene and Croatian companies on local markets as well as the markets in third countries. To date SID has mainly co-operated with HBOR in information exchange. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Food Companies in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  A Slovene business delegation led by the chairman of the Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia, Mr. Cveto Stantic, started a seven-day visit to the United States on 12 July to attend the Fancy Food trade show in New York, business conferences in New York and Cleveland, and meet representatives U.S. food companies. The delegation also attended a seminar on export potentials of Slovene companies in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  PRICES Government-Controlled Prices to be Adjusted by September \par \par \uc2  Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur announced on 12 July that government-controlled prices for all groups of products would be adjusted on a gradual basis until September after they had been frozen temporarily because of the introduction of VAT. Among the first steps according to Senjur will be to raise gasoline and electricity prices, although this will not be due to VAT but to higher crude oil prices and a stronger dollar. In turn, electricity prices will only change after the base prices without VAT influences is calculated. By the end of September other controlled prices will be adjusted as well, including the prices of public transportation, gas and public hygiene. Prices of obligatory school books, telecom services and post services will not change according to Senjur. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chooses Six Banks for \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Exchange Rate \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  chose six banks which will be obliged to sell foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling rate in case the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides to introduce an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  period. These banks inlcude: Abanka, SKB, LB Banka Zasavje, Banka Velenje, Banka Koper and NLB (Nova Ljubljanska banka). From 19 July on, the banks will be allowed to sell Euro at a maximum of SIT 197.37, and Deutsche mark at no more than SIT 100.91. Exchange offices and the selected banks are also allowed to sell foreign cash at rates that exceed the set \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by a maximum of 0.2 percent (DM) and by a maximum of 0.4 percent for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the EMU member states, Switzerland, the United Kingdom and the United States. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Higher Interest Rates on Deposits in August \par \par \uc2  As recommended by the Association of Slovene Banks, higher interest rates on deposits will take effect on 1 August after 19 out of 25 banks have backed the recommendation. Interest rates on over three-month deposits will thus be higher by 0.25 to 0.50 percentage point. The 19 banks that went along with the measure account for 91 percent of the Slovene banking system's total assets. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Petrol, Ina Sign Precontract to Establish Joint Venture \par \par \uc2  Petrol CEO Janez Lotric and Director General of Croatia's INA, Davor Stern, signed a precontract to sell the stock of the Interina corporation which is owned by Ina Zagreb. The contract was a precondition for the two oil companies to sign a contract establishing a Slovenia-based joint venture to market liquified oil gas. \par \par \uc2  Lower Sales and Profits for Color this Year \par \par \uc2  The chemicals manufacturer Color made SIT 3.18 billion of sales this year, down by 4.2 percent y/y. Profits in the period dropped by 12 percent to SIT 185.2 million. \par \par \uc2  Autocommerce Buys Perftech \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana-based Autocommerce trade company bought late last week the controlling share of the Bled-based Perftech internet provider, expanding its range of services to information technology. Established 10 years ago, Perftech currently employs 60 IT experts. The company has developed a comprehensive range of internet services since 1995. \par \par \uc2  PRIVATISATION SIT 179.7 Billion to Fill the Privatisation Gap \par \par \uc2  The government passed a report on the covering of the privatisation gap on 15 July. The report specifies the volume of funds needed to cover the gap to be at SIT 179.7 billion. The amount was calculated on the assumption that at least three quarters of shareholders would use the option to exchange Privatisation Investment Fund shares for pension vouchers under the First Pension Fund Act. According to the government decision, the property to cover the privatisation gap, the difference between the value of privatisation vouchers and the property to be privatised, would be made available by 31 July 1991. \par \par \uc2  TRANSPORT Slovenia and Croatia Discuss Transport Corridors \par \par \uc2  Slovene Minister of Transport and Communications Anton Bergauer and his Croatian counterpart Zeljko Luzavec discussed, at a meeting on 14 July, the development of railway and road transport, chiefly from the aspect of the construction of corridors no. 5 and 10. Both sides were of the opinion that the railway connection between the Croatian capital, Zagreb, Slovenia's Maribor and Austria's Graz should be upgraded as part of corridor n. 5. as soon as possible. But while Slovenia would like to retain the connection via Zidani most, Croatia would prefer a shortcut via Pragersko. According to Bergauer, the countries will further discuss this option as it has not been considered by Slovenia at all. As to the construction of motorways, the ministers tried to harmonise their countries' interests; while Croatia would like to see the Slovene section Maribor - Ptuj - the border crossing Macelj built as soon as possible, the Slovene side has not yet included it in its national motorway programme. The programme is th refore to be changed next year and likely ratified in parliament in 2001. The route of a motorway linking Croatia's Rijeka and Italy's Trieste was also discussed. \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana Ring Complete by End-August \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana's eastern by-pass will open for traffic by the end of next month according to the Slovene Company for Motorways (DARS). Final works are currently underway on a 14-kilometer motorway section, including the Golovec tunnel, which will be the only two-tube three-lane tunnel in Slovenia. Over the six years of implementing the national programme (since January 1994), a total of 143.8 kilometers of roads have been built, and another 47.4 kilometers of two-and four-lane roads at to open for traffic by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  ENERGY Power Consumption Up by 0.7 Pc in June \par \par \uc2  807.3 million KWH of electricity was spent in Slovenia in June, up by 0.7 percent year-on-year. Power consumption was higher in both direct users who took 170.1 million KWH from the network as well as in distribution 637.2 million KWH). \par \par \uc2  AGRICULTURE Nearly DM 40 Million to Help Agriculture \par \par \uc2  The National Assembly adopted, on 16 July, the bill on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures in agriculture in 1999. The law worth SIT 3.95 billion (DM 40 million) will help pig-breeders, milk producers and wheat and hops growers. Nearly SIT 2 billion will be allocated for easing the situation in the milk industry at various stages of production. Half a billion tolars will go to the pig-breeding industry, and just as much to hops producers. Approximately one billion will be allocated to wheat and rye producers. \par \par \uc2  REGIONAL DEVELOPMENT Parliament Endorses Bill on Balanced Development of Regions \par \par \uc2  The National Assembly adopted on 16 July a bill to encourage balanced development of Slovene regions, an umbrella act specifying the goals and principles of regional development, the allocation of state funds and the criteria required to qualify for state subsidies. Besides, the law defines which regions need a fresh developmental impetus. Under the law, the government will have to earmark at least 0.5 percent of GDP for balanced development of regions in the first year after the law takes effect. In the second year, however, the figure will have to total at least 1 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  Petrol's Initiative for Development of Lendava Region \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's oil company Petrol has launched activities to revive the region of Lendava, the northeasternmost part of Slovenia where an oil refinery in which Petrol has a 55 percent stake is located. The initiative group of the consortium in charge of drafting and later implementing a development strategy met on 16 July at Lendava Castle on the initiative of President of Petrol Management Board Janez Lotric and Mayor of Lendava Joze Kocon.The next meeting of the initiative committee is expected to be held this autumn. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS Poor Foreign Trade Results Caused by Depression in the Balkans and Russia \par \par \uc2  Slovenia recorded a considerable deficit in trading with foreign countries in May, which was chiefly due to political and economic problems in the regions of ex-Yugoslavia and the former Soviet Union. Exports to the EU member states - the largest and most important market for Slovenia - have been on a steady increase, marking a rise of 3.1 percent in the first five months of 1999 over the same period last year. The preliminary data show a deficit of US$ 149.9 million in May, which is expected to rise further after the data is supplemented. Last May Slovenia's exports totalled US$ 708.8 million, down by 4.2 percent over May 1998, imports were up by 0.6 percent to US$ 858.7 million in the same period. \par \par \uc2  Average Nett Salary Totals DM 1060 in May The average nett salary totalled SIT 106,558 (around DM 1060) in May, up 0.4 percent from April and 8.4 percent from a year earlier. May gross salary stood at SIT 168,628 (DM 1680), a monthly rise of 0.1 percent and a yearly increase of 8.4 percent. In real terms, however, the average gross salary fell by 0.5 percent over April and rose by 3.9 percent from May 1998. In the January-to-May period, the average clear pay amounted to SIT 105.665 (DM 1050), a 8.3 percent rise from the same period last year.Average gross wages were up 8.5 percent to SIT 167,141 (DM 1670) in the same period. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Share Prices at LSE Rising \par \par \uc2  Share prices at the Ljubljana Stock Exchange fell on Monday and Tuesday, but the trend reversed on Thursday and Friday, with the SBI share index closing the week at 1765.49 points, up 11.56 week-on-week. Also up was the BIO bond index which gained 0.61 points to close the week at 106.87 points. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Higher Exchange Rates of Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  All major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw their exchange rates rise according to the Bank of Slovenia mean exchange rate over the past week. The Euro rose by SIT 0.3 to SIT 191.7, German mark added SIT 0.2 to SIT 99.9, and Swiss franc edged up by SIT 0.3 to SIT 121.7. Austrian schilling and Italian lira remained flat at SIT 14.2 and SIT 10.1 respectively. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv7i002bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
