\par\par \b \uc2  Slovene c.bank to cut banks' euro selling rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 1999\par 17:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, July 15 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it would determine commercial banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro selling rate at a maximum of 197.37 tolars from Monday, while the maximum mark selling rate was set at 100.91 tolars. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not say at what rate it would be selling \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  to commercial banks participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not explain the move but analysts said it was designed to boost the value of tolar against the euro and mark. \par \par \uc2  This week commercial banks were selling euros to companies at an average rate of some 197.43 tolars and about 100.94 tolars per mark. \par \par \uc2  ((Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv7f04gms\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 20 through 26 March. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,808 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 28 March (STA) - The coalition leadership has eventually made a first move in privatising state banks and insurance companies. According to Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek, the banks will be partially privatised within this year, while in the case of insurance companies, the coalition has agreed on the basic principle and the methods of their privatisation to be integrated in the bill awaiting third parliamentary reading. \par \par \uc2  At the end of last week the board of the Krsko Nuclear Power Plant gathered for their first meeting in the last eight months and adopted the 1999 business plan. The Croatian members were absent, but Croatian Economics Minister Nenad Porges hosted his Slovene counterpart Metod Dragonja. Although it is not at all clear how the story of TAM will end, a contract stating national economic interests in the purchase of TAM was initialled by the Ministry of Economic Activities, Slovene Development Corporation and the prospective buyer, MTI Evropa. Mobitel, the so far only GSM mobile service provider in Slovenia, got a rival last week after Simobil launched its \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Monday/22 March - Crnomelj: Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek meets business representatives of Bela Krajina (south). Tuesday/23 March - Krsko: President Kucan visits the region of Posavje region and meets with business people there. Tuesday/23 March - The Hague: Minister of Economic Activities and Development Marjan Senjur begins a three-day visit to Holland. Tuesday/23 March - Ljubljana: the National Assembly passes acts on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions and immaterial securities. Wednesday/24 March - Ljubljana: a salaries policy agreement is signed. Friday/26 March - Zagreb: Economic ministers of Slovenia and Croatia, Metod Dragonja and Nenad Porges, meet. Friday/26 March - Krsko: The Krsko N-plant board holds its first session this year. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION Slovene and Croatian Economics Ministers Meet in Zagreb \par \par \uc2  The Slovene and Croatian economics ministers, Metod Dragonja and Nenad Porges, met in the Croatian capital on 26 March. Dragonja and Porges discuss ways of settling the outstanding issues regarding the jointly-owned Krsko nuclear plant. The meeting was agreed upon by Slovene Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek and his Croatian counterpart Zlatko Matesa, who met on 12 March at Otocec. \par \par \uc2  Minister Senjur Visits Holland \par \par \uc2  Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur paid a working visit to the Netherlands from 23 to 25 March. He met with Dutch Economics Minister Annemarie Jorritsma and Minister of Foreign Trade Geritt Ybema and opened a business conference and a panel on business and investment possibilities in Slovenia. In 1998 bilateral trade amounted to US$ 367 million. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's Trade with Yugoslavia Weakened \par \par \uc2  NATO airstrikes on Yugoslavia are expected to make Slovenia's trade with Serb and Montenegrin companies difficult. Nevertheless, economic cooperation between Slovenia and Yugoslavia has now been hampered for several years because the countries failed to reach an economic agreement. Approximately 10 Slovene companies have their branch offices in both Yugoslav republics, Serbia and Monte Negro, among which there are pharmaceuticals company Krka and white goods-producer Gorenje. Moreover, nearly one thousand Slovene companies cooperate with Yugoslav partners. The tourist industry is expected to be most severely affected by the escalation in the Balkans, mainly due to the closure of the airspace. Slovenia's exports to Yugoslavia totalled US$ 112.4 million in 1997, but fell to US$ 102.7 million in 1998. Imports from Yugoslavia rose by 60.6 percent to US$ 67.5 million in 1998 from 1997. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stops \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Purchase Price \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by fixing a purchase price. Effective since 25 January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase price period ended on 23 March, the Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  BANKS State-Owned Banks to be Privatised as Early as 1999 \par \par \uc2  Government coalition leaders reached an agreement on the privatization of state-owned banks. A 35-percent stake in the state-owned banks is to be privatised as early as this year, of which 10 percent is to be earmarked for filling the privatisation gap, Prime Minster Janez Drnovsek told a press conference on Friday. The government would retain the rest of its bank capital. An agreement was also reached at the meeting with the governor of the Bank of Slovenia and Minister of Finance that all relevant institutions would seek to act in a coordinated fashion. \par \par \uc2  Dismissal Procedure in SKB banka Found to be Legal \par \par \uc2  Governor of the Bank of Slovenia France Arhar informed SKB banka the procedure to dismiss the chairman of the bank's board was not found to be legally incorrect in any way. The supervisory board of SKB banka will meet again on 2 April, when it is expected to appoint a new member of the management team. \par \par \uc2  Nova Ljubljana Bank (NLB) Acquires a 20 Percent Stake in Dolenjska Bank (DB) \par \par \uc2  Nova Ljubljanska Bank (NLB) acquired Monday a 20-percent holding in Dolenjska Bank (DB). Before that, NLB only had a 7.7-percent stake in DB. Its stake increased to 20 percent after it purchased 53,687 DB shares from two authorised investment companies, including Maksima 2 and Maksima 3. The Nova Kreditna banka Maribor bank (NKBM) is also interested in buying a stake in DB. Last December, the bank purchased a nearly 24-percent stake in DB. At last week's meeting with Finance Minister Mitja Gaspari, the leaderships of NLB and NKBM were urged to harmonise their further decision on this issue with an appropriate development strategy of the Slovene financial markets. It was agreed that both banks' supervisory boards would inform the finance ministry in future about the details of the transactions regarding the purchase of DB shares. Both NLB and NKBM are predominantly state-owned. NLB bought the DB shares at an average price of SIT 21,845 per share. \par \par \uc2  Dolenjska banka Increases its Profit by 17 Percent \par \par \uc2  Last year Dolenjska banka made a gross profit of SIT 657 million (US$ 3.8 million), an increase of 17 percent over 1997. The bank enhanced the volume of business by 20 percent, increasing total assets to SIT 77.5 billion (US$ 445 million) by the end of 1998. Deposits by companies and citizens were up by more than 25 percent (SIT 12.5 billion), while investments in non-banking sector, i.e. companies and citizens, increased by 54 percent (SIT 15.5 billion). Loans to legal entities were last year up by 97 percent, which means the bank has no problems in placing funds, Director Franci Borsan said in a news conference in Novo mesto (southeast) on Wednesday. Along with a rise in the volume of business on the domestic market, the bank recorded an increase in the amount of business with partners abroad. \par \par \uc2  Probanka Makes a Gross Profit of SIT 460 Million \par \par \uc2  The Maribor-based bank Probanka made a gross profit of SIT 460 million last year, achieving a 9.6-percent return on revalued capital. The volume of trade amounted to SIT 47 billion, an increase of 26 percent over 1997. The bank, which holds a 1.5-percent share on the Slovene market, enhanced its assets by 16 percent or SIT 4.8 billion. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Contract to Sell TAM Initialled \par \par \uc2  A contract regulating national economic interests in the purchase of TAM, a vehicle-producer in receivership, in full was initialled Thursday by outgoing Economics Minister Metod Dragonja, President of the Slovene Development Corporation Bogdan Topic and Director of the Maribor-based MTI Evropa Martin Budna. The contract aims at specifying those parts of relations between the state and a potential buyer, MTI Evropa, that are not specified in the sales contract. These include, for instance, the preservation of production and its gradual increase, the preservation of the same number of jobs, investments in technological development and modernisation in the next five years. The initialled contact binds MTI Evropa to protect and develop all industrial property rights, particularly the trademark TAM.According to Dragonja, the contract will be signed and sent to the government only after MTI Evropa has paid a down-payment. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Ironworks Were Quite Successful in 1998 \par \par \uc2  Manufacturing companies within the Slovene Ironworks concern incurred a nett loss of SIT 1.38 billion last year, which is only 16 percent of the amount of loss recorded in 1997. The rehabilitation programme, on the other hand, forecasted a loss of SIT 2.12 billion. Minor loss followed after the state and the state electricity company Eles furnished fresh capital. The 1998 operating loss, however, did not meet the target and amounted to 4.2 billion. The Slovene Ironworks concern recorded a nett loss of over SIT 10 billion, a decrease of four billion tolars over 1997. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Post Made a Loss Last Year \par \par \uc2  Despite a 90-percent increase in the volume of services, Slovene Post made a loss of SIT 651 million last year. Stamp prices in the internal post exchange cover only 50 percent of expenses. Slovene Post has therefore asked the government to raise the price of stamps by seven tolars. In case the government does not accept the proposal, the post will incur a loss of SIT 1.6 billion this year. \par \par \uc2  Elan Gets New Management Team Members \par \par \uc2  The supervisory board of the Begunje-based sports gear maker Elan appointed two new members of the company's management team on Wednesday. Ales Jerala will be in charge of the winter product line, while Davor Noc will take charge of the annual product range. The management nominated Edin Leho financial director of the Elan group. Elan's former management team was dismissed by the supervisory board at its session on 23 February. Elan's leadership and the trade union signed an agreement as part of the general saving measures on 26 March. The Zagreb-based Privredna banka, the majority shareholder, made further provision of liquidity funds conditional to strict austerity measures. \par \par \uc2  Intereuropa Enhances its Profit \par \par \uc2  Intereuropa, a Koper-based forwarder with 2,000 employed, made an income of SIT 11.356 billion last year, the unaudited and unconsolidated data show. The company thus increased its income by 5 percent from 1997. The 1998 nett profit amounted to SIT 1.369 billion and was by 2.3 percent higher than that in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Autocommerce Acquires a Majority Stake in Coming \par \par \uc2  Autocommerce signed a contract to acquire a majority stake in Coming. Last year the company turned over 34 million German marks. It is a leading company in the audio-visual sector in Slovenia and has a market share of 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  Hit Supervisory Board Discusses Investments \par \par \uc2  The supervisory board of Hit, a Nova Gorica-based hotel and casino owner, endorsed the 1999 investment plan draft at a session on 26 March. The company will need about SIT 5.6 billion for the whole investment cycle, the reconstruction of hotel Sabotin and investments in information technology included. \par \par \uc2  Cimos International AGM on Debt Enforcement \par \par \uc2  The AGM of Cimos International adopted several resolutions to grant a successful debt enforcement on 20 March. The shareholders passed a decision to increase the capital stock and a resolution to transform claims into ownership shares. Half of the debts will be written-off, 42 percent will be transformed in the company's capital, while eight percent (about SIT 5.7 billion) will be paid back by Cimos within ten years. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENE DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION Development Corporation Will Support Development Oriented Companies \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Development Corporation (SRD) will have a total of SIT 6.45 billion (US$ 37 million) at its disposal for this year. Seventy percent of the funds will be used for investments into development and the rest for investments in rescue \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Apart from that fresh capital as financial assistance to companies, there are 8.5 billion tolars' worth of guarantees SRD will be able to grant its companies so that they can take loans with commercial banks, SRD Board Director Bogdan Topic told a news conference on Wednesday. The business plan in addition earmarks a write-off of SRD's old claims amounting to SIT 17 billion originating in the period from 1994 to 1996. \par \par \uc2  SRD to File Complaints against Smelt Intag's Former Management \par \par \uc2  Slovene Development Corporation, that is its company Gio, which holds a 100-percent stake in Smelt Intag, has drafted complaints against the former management team of Smelt Intag. Before filing them with the Swiss public prosecutor's office, Gio will complement them in view of material responsibility of the three-member board headed by Joze Zagar. The exact amount, which is said to exceed 10 million Swiss francs, is yet to be established by an independent Swiss auditor. \par \par \uc2  SALARIES Social Partners Sign a Deal on 1999-2001 Salaries Policy= \par \par \uc2  The government, trade unions and employers signed an agreement on salaries policy for the 1999-2001 period on 24 March. It defines basic principles and targets the social partners would like to achieve in salaries policy. The deal was signed only by two trade unions: the Association of Free Trade Unions of Slovenia (ZSSS) and KS 90 of Slovenia; as well as by the Employers' Association of Slovenia and chambers of commerce and trade. \par \par \uc2  TELECOMMUNICATIONS Simobil Launches \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on GSM Market \par \par \uc2  Simobil, Slovenia's second GSM mobile telecommunications provider, launched its services on 25 March. According to the company, its prices are on the average 10 to 15 percent lower than those offered by the competition. The company expects to get at least a thousand subscribers on the first day of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and increase their number to at least 20,000 by the end of the year. The GSM telephone exchange in Ljubljana has a capacity of 250,000 subscribers. The company, in which Slovene stockholders hold a 75-percent share and Swedish GSM operator Telia the rest, plans to open a similar centre in Maribor before the end of the year, followed by centres in other Slovene towns. Simobil, which signed a concession contract with the government in October 1998, currently covers approximately 60 percent of Slovenia'a population and projects to reach a 90 percent covering by the end of the year. The company has signed contracts with over 50 providers worldwide. Slovenia's first network operator of the GSM mobile t lephony Mobitel, which has been on the GSM market since December 1995, has a 95-percent market share, while the rest five percent is held by Debitel, which uses Mobitel's network. Mobitel's share is expected to drop to 80 percent with the arrival of Simobil. \par \par \uc2  POWER INDUSTRY N-Plant's Board Passes 1999 Business Plan \par \par \uc2  This year's business plan of the Krsko Nuclear Power Plant was endorsed on Friday by the N-Plant's Slovene-Croatian board, meeting at Krsko without Croatian members. According to the decree with which the Slovene government transformed the N-plant into a public company, both state electricity companies, Slovenia's ELES and Croatia's HEP, have to sign a contract on power supply with the N-plant. Since HEP has not done it yet, it is unlikely to use Krsko-generated electricity this year, the board's chairman and general manager of ELES, Ivo Banic, told a news conference after the board's session Croatia's debt for the Krsko-generated power supplied last year amounts to SIT 1.572 billion (US$ 9 million). There is also SIT 1.453 billion for the plant's decommissioning. The debt has not changed since July 1998, when HEP stopped using electricity from Krsko. ELES sells electricity at a market price at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  TRADE SHOWS A Show of Culinary Arts Opens in Ljubljana \par \par \uc2  A show of culinary arts featuring the food-procession industry opened in Ljubljana on Tuesday. Over 315 exhibitors from 17 countries were promoted at a surface of 8000 square meters. \par \par \uc2  Kranj Hosts an Agricultural Fair \par \par \uc2  The 38th international agriculture, food and forestry fair opened on 26 March to feature 612 exhibitors, as many as 117 of them from abroad. The exhibition grounds cover an area of 20,000 square metres. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Shares Depreciate on Ljubljana SE \par \par \uc2  The SBI share index lost 46.48 point last week and closed at 1847.91 point. The BIO bond index depreciated by 0.96 point to 106.5 point. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Appreciate \par \par \uc2  All major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciated last week according to the exchange rate of the Bank of Slovenia. The euro was up by SIT 0.21 to SIT 190.34, the U.S. dollar by SIT 1.87 to SIT 175.95 and the German mark by SIT 0.11 to SIT 97.32. The Austrian schilling appreciated by SIT 0.02 to SIT 13.83, 100 Italian liras by SIT 0.01 to SIT 9.83 and the Swiss franc by SIT 0.3 to SIT 119.3. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv3s001an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
