\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Slovenia raises banks' euro buying rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par 16:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, March 15 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would raise commercial banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro buying rate to 190.6 tolars from 190.3 tolars, effective Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from chosen commercial banks participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but did not disclose the rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not explain the move but analysts said it was designed to lower the value of the tolar against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate was effective from February 18, when it was raised from 190.11 tolars. \par \par \uc2  ((Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv3f03160\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly review of Slovene economy from 27 February through 5 March. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 6 March (STA) - The week was dominated by events in the business sector. Economics Minister Metod Dragonja stepped down after he was appointed vice chairman of the board in the pharmaceuticals maker Lek. Both Slovenia's pharmaceuticals manufacturers, Ljubljana's Lek and Novo mesto-based Krka last year reported lower profits than the year before. Several other companies announced their 1998 business results this week, the story about selling the car-maker TAM remained unended, and Minister of Ecomomic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur soothed public anxiety stressing there were no signs of recession. Another resounding notice this week was the recommendation of several commercial banks for ceiling interest rates, which caused the most concern in the Bank of Slovenia. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Monday/1 March - Ljubljana: Inter-bank recommendation for ceiling deposit interest rates took effect. Monday/1 March - Begunje: Elan management dismissed. Monday/1 March - Maribor: Tam and MTI Europe did not sign the purchase contract. Monday/1 March - Ljubljana: Slovenia, Hungary signed contract to build railway line connecting the countries. Wednesday/3 March - Ljubljana: Economics Ministers Metod Dragonja stepped down. Wednesday/3 March - Ljubljana: Authorized investment companies (PID) filed motion to assess constitutionality of privatization conclusion act. \par \par \uc2  LEGISLATION Government Confirms Law on Budgets \par \par \uc2  The government passed and laid before Parliament for first reading the bill on national budgets on 4 March. Finance Ministry State Secretary Martin Cvikl said it was the key systemic piece of legislation for the area of public finances. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL COOPERATION Chambers of Commerce of Slovenia and Ukraine Sign Cooperation Agreement \par \par \uc2  The chambers of commerce of Slovenia and Ukraine signed the cooperation agreement on 2 March at a business conference in Ljubljana, attended by 13 Ukrainian and 40 Slovene companies. Both parties undertook in the agreement to promote economic cooperation between Slovenia and Ukraine. The representatives of both chambers underlined several other bilateral agreements, for instance investment protection and promotion agreement, should be signed. Slovene-Ukrainian trade has been up since 1992. In 1998 it totalled US$ 57.5 million and rose by US$ 1.5 million over 1997. Slovene exports to Ukraine amounted to US$ 42 million. Slovenia mostly exported pharmaceutical products and electrical devices. Nearly 200 Slovene companies have had business contacts with Ukraine; 19 of them have their branch offices or joint ventures there. \par \par \uc2  Slovene-Turkish Economic Relations Debated in Istanbul \par \par \uc2  A two-day seminar "Developing Slovene-Turkish Business Relations" intended to review economic relations between Slovenia and Turkey and devise ways for their consolidation started Thursday in Istanbul. The participants agreed that Slovene companies have good potentials for sales in Turkey's 60 million market. Slovenia will give a presentation of its economy in Turkey this August, the mixed economic commission is to sit in autumn, and the business council of both countries is to convene during the annual Regional Business Meeting Point at Bled. In 1998, bilateral trade toppled US$ 100 million for the first time. Slovene exports to Turkey amounted to US$ 57 million, recording a 74 percent rise y/y. Trade balance was positive for Slovenia for the first time after 1995. Exchange of services also increased last year. In 1997, exports of services amounted to US$ 6.9 million, while imports were at US$ 5.9 million. \par \par \uc2  A Line of Credit Opened Between Slovenia and Bosnian Federation \par \par \uc2  A cooperation agreement was reached in Sarajevo on Thursday by the Slovene Export Corporation (SID) and the Bosnian Federation Investment Bank. The credit line opened between both institutions should stimulate Slovene exports to the Bosnian Federation. It will create conditions under which Slovene exporters will be able to do business with the federation, Marko Plahuta, SID executive director of finances and guarantees, told journalists. SID expects that a great deal of Slovene exporters would be interested in taking out insurance policies through this line of credit. The credit line is worth is 25 million euros. \par \par \uc2  MACRO-ECONOMIC TRENDS Minister Senjur: No Signs of Recession \par \par \uc2  There are no signs of economic recession in Slovenia, Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur told press on Tuesday. He stressed most economic indicators had improved in 1998, and although lower growth is expected this year - it is to range between 3.5 and 4 percent - there are no signs of recession. Unemployment remains high and is not likely to fall until industry starts employing more people, while this year's inflation rate is not expected to exceed 3 percent before June.At a news conference on 2 March, Minister Senjur also spoke about duty free shops. He said transforming duty-free shops to retail shops would equal their termination since their specific locations and structure of demand by daily tourists from neighbouring countries and passengers in transit would only allow them to sell tobacco products. Introducing excise duties on 1 July, in turn, means gradual elimination of the duty-free element from Slovene duty-free shops and their termination in 2005. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Will \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Case of Lower Savings \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do all it can to maintain the current level of development and preserve relative stability of the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the tolar, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Franc Arhar said Wednesday at a meeting with businessmen from the Gorenjsko region. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will formulate its policy in accordance with the international situation. In case of negative international trends, the governor expects the national budget to be amended and hopes reason would dominate the wage policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if domestic savings decrease, Arhar said. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Some Banks Lower Interest Rates \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Banks' Association (ZBS) has recently recommended that ZBS members reduce the highest deposit interest rates on domestic and foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits after 1 March. It suggested that the highest yearly interest rates should range from 1 percent on call deposits to the base rate plus 4 percent (TOM+4) on over 2-year deposits. Although not binding, the ZBS recommendation took effect on Monday, the day when the inter-bank agreement on the highest deposit interest rates expired. The association recommended interest rates for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits that would be comparable to those in the European money market. \par \par \uc2  INSURANCE COMPANIES Generali SKB Insurance Company to Build Own Office Network in Slovenia \par \par \uc2  Generali SKB Insurance Company plans to set up its own office network, company leaders told a news conference Thursday presenting plans and problems the insurance agency is facing in the Slovene market. Generali SKB allocated over DM 10 million in the last two years to increase its presence in Slovenia, set up its own infrastructure and ensure smooth conduct of insurance businesses. Another DM 30-15 million of investments is to be made to the agency's office network over the next three years. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES \par \par \uc2  Economics Ministers Dragonja Resigns to Take up Job in Lek Board of Managers \par \par \uc2  Incumbent Minister of Economics Metod Dragonja was appointed vice chairman of the board of managers of the pharmaceuticals maker Lek by the supervisory board at a meeting on Tuesday afternoon. Dragonja will be responsible for finances, economics and capital integration. The supervisory board also dismissed member of the board responsible for finance and capital markets, Igor Kusar. The dismissal was proposed by chairman of the board Andro Ocvirk. Minister Metod Dragonja offered his resignation to Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek on Wednesday. Drnovsek accepted his resignation. \par \par \uc2  Government Saving Cimos International \par \par \uc2  The government called for debt enforcement in the Koper-based automotive supplier Cimos International at its regular session on Thursday. In case debt enforcement is not agreed, Cimos would go bankrupt and about 2500 jobs within and outside the company would be lost, while the government could not have SIT 1.7 billion of its claims paid. The government agreed that its claims be transformed to an 8.6 percent stake in Cimos International during the debt enforcement procedure. It advised other para-state institutions which have over SIT 400 million of claims in Cimos to do likewise. \par \par \uc2  TAM and MTI Evropa Fail to Sign Sales Contract \par \par \uc2  Maribor-based MTI Evropa did not sign a sales contract to purchase TAM, a vehicle manufacturer in receivership, on Monday, 1 March, the deadline for signing the contract. According to Mirko Zagar, TAM trustee in bankruptcy, the contract has not been signed because MTI Evropa failed to pay a deposit of SIT 1.05 billion, one tenth of the contract's worth. The bankruptcy senate of the Maribor district court ruled on 15 January that TAM can be sold to MTI Europe for SIT 10.5 billion (about US$ 64.8 million). The deal had previously been approved by TAM's creditor committee. Further steps are to be decided upon by the bankruptcy senate of the Maribor district court and TAM's creditor committee. \par \par \uc2  Elan Management Dismissed Because of Mismanagement \par \par \uc2  Supervisory board of the sports-equipment manufacturer Elan dismissed members of the managing board on 1 March citing poor business results in recent years that have plunged the company in the red. The management has been unable either to stop or reverse the negative trends which led to massive losses that are expected to amount to eight million German marks at the end of the current business year which ends on 31 March. The supervisory board rejected the proposed business plan for the next season and has not yet appointed a new management. Elan's majority owner, the Croatian bank Privredna banka Zagreb, plans to sell its majority shareholding in the company, the new chairman of the managing board Vladimir Selebaj told a news conference on Monday. The bank has already made an offer to Slovene investors believing such a deal highly desirable for both sides as well as for Elan. No concrete bid has been made so far, which means all options are still open, including the one for management buy-out. \par \par \uc2  Comet Reports Lower Results \par \par \uc2  Comet last year made SIT 4.99 billion turnover, creating SIT 178.5 million profit, down 42.13 pc y/y. CEO Marjan Lorger named unfavourable exchange rate, lower sales and slightly higher taxes as the key reasons for lower results. \par \par \uc2  Kolinska Successful in 1998 \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana-based food factory Kolinska met expectations in 1998. While it failed to carry out its plans in full, it did make nine percent more profits, which according to CEO Lojze Dezelak, is an achievement to be proud of. The company's net revenues amounted to SIT 9.5 billion, with net profits reaching SIT 792.5 million. This year, Kolinska will pay dividends for the fifth time after privatization. \par \par \uc2  Mercator Exceeds Plans \par \par \uc2  One of Slovenia's largest trade companies, Mercator made SIT 1.35 billion of net profit, about one quarter more than planned, according to unrevised and unconsolidated data. According to CEO Zoran Jankovic, the key reasons for the rise include a 9.7 percent increase in group-level revenues, lower costs at all levels and unification of procedures throughout the business system. Mercator plans to make SIT 1.58 billion profit this year. \par \par \uc2  Krka Reports Lower Net Profit \par \par \uc2  Krka pharmaceuticals reported SIT 4.16 billion (US$ 23.77m) net profit in 1998 according to unrevised and unconsolidated data, or 19.7 percent less than in 1997. Net profits from sales amounted to SIT 49.67 billion (US$ 283.8m), up 2.7 percent y/y. Operating profit was also up by as many as 76.2 percent from SIT 4.22 billion in 1997 to SIT 7.43 billion (US$ 42.45m). The increase is believed to be due to lower operating costs. Profit from regular \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  decreased by 16.2 percent to 4.7 billion (US$ 26.85m). Compared to 1997, revenues from funding were down while outcomes from funding up. \par \par \uc2  TRANSPORT Renovation of Ivanje selo Motorway Viaduct to Continue \par \par \uc2  The left half of the Ivanje selo viaduct between Logatec and Unec on the Ljubljana-Koper motorway will be closed for traffic from 1 March to 31 July. Traffic will be directed to the other half of the viaduct until the tourist season starts. A third lane will be opened at the beginning of the tourist season. \par \par \uc2  Contract for Railway Construction between Slovenia and Hungary Signed \par \par \uc2  Slovene Railways signed a contract Monday with three Slovene building companies for the construction of the Slovene part of the railway connection with Hungary. Under the contract, the main contractor SCT Ljubljana and its two contractual partners Primorje Ajdovscina and GIZ Gradis Ljubljana have until the end of the year 2000 to build 24.5 kilometers of the new railway from the village of Puconci via Hodos to the national border. Slovene railways will take government-guaranteed loan from a consortium of Slovene banks to fund the SIT 11 billion (about US$ 64 million) project. \par \par \uc2  AGRICULTURE Hmezad Kmetijstvo Goes Bankrupt \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Food and the Economics Ministry held several talks with banks to convince them to reprogram credits to Hmezad Kmetijstvo based in Zalec, central Slovenia. However, after banks set unacceptable conditions, the ministries agreed on 3 March that bankruptcy was the only way out for the highly indebted company. \par \par \uc2  Agriculture Ministry Says Situation in Pig Market will Improve \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Food believes the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase of fattened pigs of Slovene origin would show its full effects next week so the situation in the market would improve rapidly. A total of 9510 fattened pigs were bought until 5 March, four days after the measure took effect. The ministry also said a protest rally of pig breeders, announced for next Wednesday, was not necessary. Imports of live pigs and pork halved in the last 14 days. At the same time, import prices from Austria rose slightly in the period. \par \par \uc2  TRADE SHOWS Fair Alps Adria - Home Winds Up \par \par \uc2  The fair Alps Adria - Home featuring building materials, furniture and heating equipment, winds up on Sunday afternoon. According to the estimations of the organizers, the number of visitors has met the expectations. The fair, which opened in Ljubljana on 23 February, featured about 400 exhibitors from 22 countries. Apart from the Slovene companies, companies from Austria, Croatia, India, Italy, Germany and Estonia were also directly present at the fair. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM All Elements for Tourism Promotion Act Ready \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Small Business and Tourism drafted all subordinate acts for the Tourism Promotion Act and public tenders for tourism for 1999 until the set deadline, the ministry said at a news conference on 2 March, given together with the Centre for the Promotion of Tourism. Minister Janko Razgorsek also announced that the government would grant seven concession for organizing games of chance by the end of the week. Hit Nova Gorica is to get five concessions and Casino Portoroz two. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS 92.1 Million Passengers in Slovenia in 1998 \par \par \uc2  92.1 million passengers crossed Slovene borders in 1998, down 12.5 percent over 1997. The number of Passengers increased by 2.9 percent m/m in December. The figure was down 11.9 percent over December 1997. \par \par \uc2  February's Factory-gate Prices Down from January \par \par \uc2  Factory-gate prices fell 0.1 percent in February from January, according to preliminary data released by the national statistical office. However, a rise of 2 percent was recorded in comparison with February 1998. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE LSE: Lower Share Prices \par \par \uc2  Most shares at the Ljubljana Stock Exchange were down this week. A total of 16 shares saw their prices sink, bringing the SBI index down 46.69 points to 1888.31 points. Conversely, the bond index BIO gained 1.02 points to 107.08 points. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Higher Exchange Rates of Major Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  US dollar gained SIT 3.1 to SIT 175.13, and Euro SIT 0.19 to SIT 189.73 this week according to the Bank of Slovenia mean exchange rate. Swiss franc was up SIT 0.01 to SIT 119.27, German mark added SIT 0.10 to SIT 97.01, and Italian lira (100) SIT 0.01 to SIT 9.80. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv37000rr\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia \highlight\uc2  Will  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  in Case of Lower Savings. \b0\par\par\uc2 391 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 3 March (STA) - The Bank of Slovenia will do all it can to maintain the current level of development and preserve relative stability of the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the tolar, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Franc Arhar said Wednesday at a meeting with businessmen from the Gorenjsko region. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will formulate its policy in accordance with the international situation. In case of negative international trends, the governor expects the national budget to be amended and hopes reason would dominate the wage policy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if domestic savings decrease, Arhar said. \par \par \uc2  The governor rejected complaints by exporters who argued that money was pouring to importers because of the existing exchange rate policy. He said there was stronger need for a good, and consequently more expensive, monetary policy in smaller economies. I hope managers also voice their demands in bank supervisory boards while making plans for the next few years. As opposed to real economy, banks are specific. Companies can get overindebted and close down, banks can't, Arhar said. \par \par \uc2  Slovene banks granted SIT 240 billion of loans last year, nearly two times more than the year before. In consequence, Arhar said, this meant higher risks, but about 90 percent of the total are still good loans, which is a high figure compared to other countries in transition. The governor also pointed out the possibility of raising funds without financial agencies. There are quite a few sound companies in Slovenia that could issue bonds. Because of this the banks will need to lower their margins and consider mergers in order to remain competitive. Mergers and other forms of integration should not lead to renewed monopolies but make fewer banks more competitive, Arhar said. \par \par \uc2  Speaking of the changes made last year, Arhar listed lower inflation and decreasing real interest rates. Also, banking legislation was amended to liberalize capital flows and allow new treatment of risks involved in changing deposit insurances. \par \par \uc2  The governor stressed Slovenia should deal with the value added tax as soon as possible. He added that no near price rises are expected until June. On the contrary, prices might go down in the period. Slovenia will not change its inflation target because of VAT's introduction in June, Arhar said. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv33000oz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
