\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Slovenia raises banks' euro buying rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 122 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 1999\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Feb 17 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it would raise commercial banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro buying rate to 190.3 tolars from 190.11 tolars, effective from Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from chosen commercial banks participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but did not disclose the rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not explain the move but analysts said its purpose was to depress the value of the tolar against the euro. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate was effective from February 2, when it was raised from 188.7 tolars. \par \par \uc2  ((Marja Novak, Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2h04a2a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 30 January through 5 February. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,995 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 7 February (STA) - Slovenia's becoming an associate member of the EU on 1 February brought no major change to the Slovene economy as the Interim Accord had been in force since 1997. Major changes, however, will apply to the field of finances. The Europe Accord enables EU banks to found its branch offices in Slovenia and relaxes the inflow of foreign loans. Customs for trading with the 15-nation bloc will be completely abolished in 2001 but Slovene insurance companies will have a four-year transition period to get ready for the advent of foreign competition. \par \par \uc2  While speculations about a merger of Slovene and Croatian pharmaceutical companies, Lek and Pliva, marked the past week, a Slovene-Croatian meeting took place to discuss the future of Slovenia-located ski factory Elan, whose major owner is a Croatian bank. \par \par \uc2  A new trade union association was set up in the past week, called Alternative. \par \par \uc2  Fed up with waiting for the government to supply the funds to fill the privatisation gap, the authorised investment companies (PIDs) decided to bring charges against the government. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Monday/1 February - Ljubljana: Slovene and Belgian foreign ministers, Boris Frlec and Erik Derycke signed the agreement to protect and stimulate investment. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF SLOVENIA Changes to Fiduciary Accounts \par \par \uc2  A new regulation about fiduciary accounts took place on 1 February. The Bank of Slovenia has shortened from 7 to 4 years the period within which foreign investors are not allowed to sell Slovene securities. But the regulation applies only to the contracts signed after 1 February. When a foreign buyer purchases Slovene securities for less than 4 years s/he has to pay a fee set once every quarter by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The fee is currently 2.5 percent per quarter. Another change is that those purchases of shares that reach over a tenth of voting rights, that is a tenth of the initial capital of the company will be classified as direct investments. A record of them will not be kept any more on fiduciary accounts. \par \par \uc2  A decision to introduce fiduciary accounts was taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February 1997. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sets New \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Purchasing Exchange Rate \par \par \uc2  On 1 February the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  set a new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchasing exchange rate of SIT 190.11 for one euro. At the end of January this rate was set at SIT 189.52. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Leaderships of Two Banks Meet \par \par \uc2  The leaderships of Nova Kreditna Banka Maribor and Dolenjska Banka met on 2 February in Maribor, northeast, for the first time after the Bank of Slovenia gave approval to the purchase of a 24.05 percent stake in Dolenjska Banka. The banks discussed cooperation in the future. \par \par \uc2  Majority of Slovene Banks Not Prepared for Liberalisation \par \par \uc2  Moody's, a New York-based agency assessing investment conditions, has said in its report that most of the Slovene banks are insufficiently prepared for liberalisation of the banking sector. Slovene banks were said to be too small and burdened with too high operating costs, a limited number of products and services as well as with uncompleted restructuring of the Slovene economy. \par \par \uc2  TAXES Finance Minister on VAT \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mitja Gaspari said on 2 February it would be bad timing to abolish the basic interest rate TOM in 1999, when the value added tax is to be introduced. The minister added there was no need to postpone the introduction of VAT. Minister Gaspari stressed at the Chamber of Commerce management board session that the application of the nominal interest rate could lead to higher interest rates because of in-built inflation expectations. Previous inflation is included in the present base rate TOM. \par \par \uc2  ENTERPRISES Management Boards of Slovene and Croatian Pharmaceutical Companies Meet \par \par \uc2  The management boards of Lek and Pliva, Slovene and Croatian pharmaceutical companies, met in Ljubljana on 4 February to discuss the issue of both companies' cooperation, possibilities of joint projects and their cooperation on the Slovene and Croatian markets. Lek, which hosted the meeting, said the issue of capital integration was not addressed, with the exception of the issue of Pliva's stake in Lek. Representatives of the Croatian company said Pliva did not own any Lek shares. Capital integration may follow as a result of successful business cooperation, Lek stated in a press release. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Dragonja and a Croatian Bank Meet over Elan \par \par \uc2  Talks were held on 3 February about the Privredna Banka Zagreb's shares in Elan, a ski factory located in the Slovene town of Begunje. Slovene Economics Metod Dragonja and the chairman of the board of PBZ, Bozo Prko, also discussed some other issues regarding Elan's finances. Dragonja and Prko agreed to exchange information about a potential sell of the PBZ's stake in Elan and about some other financial issues. PBZ, the biggest Croatian bank, holds 77.25 percent of Elan's stock. It has been seeking for months a suitable bidder for Elan, a company it bought in December 1991 for just over 32 million Deutsche marks. \par \par \uc2  Mercator Supports 1999 Business Plan \par \par \uc2  The 1999 business plan envisaging a profit of SIT 1.58 billion was approved as well as investments of SIT 13 billion to capital goods for this year at a meeting of the supervisory board of Mercator, Slovenia's major trading company, on 4 February. Last year's results, which exceeded the company's plans, were on the agenda, too. The supervisory board also fully supported the Mercator's merger strategy. \par \par \uc2  Hit's Turnover Higher than in 1997 \par \par \uc2  Hit's gross revenues totalled SIT 32.6 billion in 1998, a rise of 12 percent form a year earlier. With its gambling department accounting for almost SIT 29 billion, restaurant and hotel services made up SIT 3.6 billion of total gross revenues. The company paid taxes of nearly SIT 12.5 in 1998, up 16 percent from 1997. Last year's net profit is estimated at SIT 1.3 billion. A fall of 3 percent in visitors to Hit's facilities was recorded in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Sava Tyres Reduces Production Output \par \par \uc2  Mostly due to a fall in demand in the Middle East, Croatia and some parts of Europe, tyres for lorries in stock exceeded the demand at the end of 1998. Sava Tyres, whose 60-percent owner is U.S. Goodyear, has thus decided to reduce its tyre production in February and March. As a result some employees were asked to go on leave. \par \par \uc2  PRIVATISATION PIDs Bring Charges against the Government \par \par \uc2  Charges were brought on 5 February against the government by all those authorised investment companies (PIDs) that could not yet replace the privatisation vouchers (certificates) for property due to the unsettled issue of privatisation deficit. The PIDs insist that the government give them shares of and stakes in those enterprises that are owned by the government in exchange for privatisation vouchers. The Association of Management Companies, gathering PIDs, said PIDs were forced to sue the government in order to protect the interests of shareholders after the government had failed to provide state funds to fill the privatisation gap. \par \par \uc2  TRADE UNIONS Fifth Trade Union Association Founded in Slovenia \par \par \uc2  Eight trade unions founded on 1 February a new Slovene trade union association called Alternative. It currently has 8000 members. Its President Slavko Kmetic announced Alternative would become the second largest trade union association in Slovenia, (the largest is The Association of Free Trade Unions of Slovenia) after it is to gain members from all over the country. Membership of over 20,000 is expected. \par \par \uc2  TELECOMMUNICATIONS Digitel Wins a Case against a Government Decision \par \par \uc2  The Supreme Court ruled on 1 February that the government decision taken on 11 June 1998 to grant a GSM concession to Simobil and not to Digitel should be done away with. The court said the government decision contained no reasons about the factors that were deciding for the decision taken. The government will appeal against the ruling within 15 days. Nevertheless, the ruling brings no change to the appointment of Simobil as second GSM mobile telephony concessionaire, Simobil said, adding the concession contract with the government has been reached and is valid. The management team of Digitel said in a press release that the ruling had proved the government violated international public tender procedures. On 11 June 1998 the government chose Simobil second GSM concessionaire. The company was recommended by the government expert commission. From among Simobil and Digitel, which applied for public tender to be given concession to use radio-frequency specter to provide GSM services in Slovenia, the expert c mmission had chosen Simobil, saying its offer was more appropriate than that of Digitel as regards expert standards and criteria in the submitted documentation. \par \par \uc2  Simobil Starts Marketings it Services on 25 March \par \par \uc2  At the launch of Simobil's GSM mobile telephony services on 25 March the signal will cover 60 percent of the Slovene population but is expected to increase to 90 percent by the end of 1999. 80 million Deutsche marks have already been invested to the construction of infrastructure and to the concession fee. Additional 50 million Deutsche marks will be invested this year. While Simobil-provided GSM services will be cheaper than Mobitel's, an immediate and sharp drop in prices could not to expected. Mobitel is the other mobile telephony operator in Slovenia providing the service at the frequency of 900 Mhz. Simobil will shortly reach a contract on a syndicated loan of 70 million Deutsche with a consortium of Slovene banks. \par \par \uc2  AGRICULTURE Decree on Financial \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Agriculture \par \par \uc2  SIT 16 billion will be allocated as financial assistance in order to preserve and develop agriculture and food production in 1999. A decree to that effect was issued on 4 February by the government. \par \par \uc2  ADVERTISING Advertising in the Slovene Media Turns Over DM 338 Million in 1998 \par \par \uc2  Advertising in the Slovene media last year accounted for revenues worth almost 338 million Deutsche marks, of which 56 percent was turned over by television stations, 18 percent by papers and magazines, 14 percent by dailies and 4 percent by advertising posters. As the radio stations were excluded from the advertising research carried out by the Institute for Media Research it has only been estimated that 8 percent of the revenues belonged to radio stations. A rise of 50 percent in total gross advertising revenues was thus recorded in 1998 from 1997. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS Factory-gate Prices of Goods Unchanged in January \par \par \uc2  Prices of manufactured goods remained unchanged in January form December, but rose by 2.4 percent from a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE SBI Index Rises \par \par \uc2  Most shares on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange closed the past week at similar prices like a week earlier. With 16 shares up and 9 down, the SBI index rose by 14.19 points to 1901.06 points. The BIO index also was up 0.21 point to 110.52 points. \par \par \uc2  As of 1 February the Ljubljana Stock Exchange started publishing the PIX index about the shares of authorised investment companies (PIDs). PIX lost 5.86 points to 1089.61 points in the last week. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Portfolio Investment in January \par \par \uc2  Foreigners' sells of securities on the stock market totalled 7 million Deutsche marks net in January. 71 percent of all deals were concluded on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange. Net deals were at 2.2 million Deutsche marks until 15 January. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Up \par \par \uc2  Major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose in the past week according to the mean rate of the Bank of Slovenia. The euro gained SIT 0.16 to SIT 188.98, the Deutsche mark SIT 0.08 to SIT 98.63, the U.S. dollar SIT 1.17 to SIT 167.05, the Austrian schilling SIT 0.01 to SIT 13.73, 100 Italian liras SIT 0.01 to SIT 9.76 and the Swiss franc SIT 1.12 to SIT 118.19. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv27000g3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
