\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Slovenia raises euro buying rate. \b0\par\par\uc2 129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 1999\par 16:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Feb 1 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it would raise commercial banks' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro buying rate to 190.11 tolars from 189.52 tolars, effective from Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  It said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from chosen commercial banks participating in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but did not disclose the rate. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not explain the move but analysts said it was designed to depress the value of the tolar against the euro. \par \par \uc2  Prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on January 27, commercial banks were buying euros from companies at an average rate of 188.7 tolars. \par \par \uc2  ((Marja Novak, Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010908dv2105eic\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Review of Slovene Economy from 23 through 29 January. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,772 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 1999\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 31 January (STA) - Decisions taken at the last session of the Bank of Slovenia (BS) Council were presented by Governor of the BS France Arhar. The Council passed changes to the fiduciary account regime for portfolio foreign investments and to the payments of tolar deposits on financial loans taken abroad. It also adopted the objectives of the monetary policy for 1999, which include the 16 to 24 percent growth rate of the M3 monetary aggregate. The bank Nova Kreditna Banka Maribor was given a go-ahead on the purchase of 24.05-percent stake in the Dolenjska Banka bank. \par \par \uc2  The government unanimously refused to issue a permit for Mercator's purchase of Emona Merkur's 170,325 common registered shares. The decision was taken after basic guidelines for the national development strategy had been considered. Dissatisfied with the government decision, the Mercator management team decided Friday to start administrative proceedings at the Supreme Court. \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices have increased by 1 percent in January, putting the January inflation rate at 1 percent. A year-to-year rise in consumer prices is 6.1 percent. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Monday/25 January - Singapore: Minister of Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur starts two-day visit to Singapore. Tuesday/26 January - Ljubljana: Minister of Labour Anton Rop visited by Panagoitis Roumeliotis, the coordinator of the EU's regional initiative for stability and good neighbourly relations in Southeastern Europe (The Royaumont initiative). \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL RELATIONS Minister Senjur in Singapore \par \par \uc2  Slovene Minister for Economic Relations and Development Marjan Senjur met during his official visit to Singapore with Foreign Minister Jayaumar on Tuesday. The ministers discussed the Asian financial crisis and IMF assistance to the region. Minister Senjur briefed the Singaporean official about the measures the Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had recently introduced to prevent the free flow of 'hot capital'. Senjur said the geopolitical position of Slovenia was an important meeting point enabling cooperation not only with Slovenia's neighbours but also with other Central and Eastern European partners. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia and U.S. for Closer Economic Cooperation \par \par \uc2  A seminar How to Do Business with the United States was organized in Ljubljana Thursday by the Slovene Chamber of Commerce (GZS). U.S. Ambassador to Slovenia Nancy Halliday Ely-Raphael said relations between Slovenia and the U.S. were good but there was still enough room for closer economic cooperation. Participants in the seminar were of the opinion that several bilateral agreements should be concluded in order to strengthen the cooperation in economy. In addition, Slovene companies could benefit more from the contacts established by the Slovenes living in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  The trade exchange with the U.S. accounts for just 5 percent of the total Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 1997 Slovenia's exports to the U.S. totalled US$ 243 million, while the imports from there amounted to US$ 284 million. \par \par \uc2  FINANCES Bank of Slovenia Introduces Several Changes \par \par \uc2  Governor of the Bank of Slovenia France Arhar presented Monday the decisions taken by the BS Council. The Council has adopted the objectives of the monetary policy for 1999, which include the 16 to 24 percent growth rate of the M3 monetary aggregate. The M3 rose by 20.2 percent last year, mainly due to domestic crediting. \par \par \uc2  The Council also passed changes to the fiduciary account regime for portfolio foreign investments and to the deposits in tolars to be paid for financial loans taken abroad. The first change means shortening of the seven-year period to four years during which a foreign holder of Slovene shares is obliged not to sell them. The other decision will soften borrowings abroad because a deposit in Slovene tolars worth 40 percent of the loan will not be required any more. It will be reduced and should range from 0 to 30 percent. Both measures take effect on 1 February. \par \par \uc2  Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chooses Banks for \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Buying \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided Tuesday that four Slovene banks, namely Dolenjska Banka, Abanka, Nova Ljubljanska Banka and SKB Banka will be obliged to purchase foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate of SIT 189.52 for 1 euro. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Sales Contract for TAM Initialled \par \par \uc2  The sales contract of TAM, a Maribor-based vehicle producer in receivership, was initialled Friday by official receiver Mirko Zagar, Buyer of TAM John Eapen, Director of MTI Evropa Martin Buden and lawyer Pavel Pensa. SIT 10.5 billion for TAM has to be paid within 6 months. Only then the right to property will be transferred to the buyer. \par \par \uc2  Sava Tires Manufactures Over 5 Million Tires \par \par \uc2  Sava Tires, a Kranj-sitted company jointly owned by concern Goodyear (60) and the Sava joint-stock company (40), produced over 5 million tires last year and thus fully met its business plan. Sava Tires employes over 1700 workers. Its plan is to produce almost 5.5 million tires in 1999. \par \par \uc2  Jewellry Manufacturer Sold to AL Inzeniring \par \par \uc2  The Zlatarna Celje joint-stock company in receivership will be sold to AL Inzeniring, a Ljubljana-based company, for SIT 296 million, the bankruptcy senate of the Celje District Court decided at the beginning of the week. Following two failed auctions, a public invitation for tenders was issued at the end of last year with the opening price of SIT 291.6 million, that is 67.5 percent of the company's value. Two tenders arrived and AL Inzeniring was chosen after its programme and financial scheme were assessed as more favourable. \par \par \uc2  MERGERS Government Opposes Mercator's Bid to Take Emona Merkur Over \par \par \uc2  The government unanimously refused Thursday to issue a permit for Mercator's purchase of Emona Merkur's 170,325 common registered shares. The decision was taken after basic guidelines for the national development strategy had been considered. By worsening the economic situation of Slovenia as a whole as well as of the regions in which Emona Merkur is present, the takeover resulting from such a purchase would have a negative impact on the development of the Slovene food retailers in the long run. Dissatisfied with the government decision, the Mercator management team decided Friday to start administrative proceedings at the Supreme Court. \par \par \uc2  Shareholders of Kranj-based Trading Company Vote for "Triplet" \par \par \uc2  Shareholders of Zivila Kranj, a Slovene chain of supermarkets, decided on Friday to merge with two Slovene rivals, namely Emona Merkur and Emona Obala into a new company called Emona Zivila. 99.8 percent of the shareholders voted for the merger. \par \par \uc2  Trading Companies Zivila and Mercator Fighting for Loka \par \par \uc2  Zivila Kranj issued a bid to buy shares of its Skofja Loka-based rival Loka. Zivila will be purchasing Loka shares until 22 February. The bid will only be successful if 46 percent of Loka shares are bought. Having signed a letter of intent about a friendly takeover with Mercator last September, Loka opposes a Zivila takeover. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM Slovenia Recorded One-Percent Decline in Tourists in 1998 \par \par \uc2  Slovene tourist resorts were visited by 1,797,217 tourists in 1998, recording a year-on-year decline of one percent. The first statistical data on the turnover in the Slovene tourist industry show the number of tourists was up by 4 percent to 103,174 last December from December 1997. Slovenia recorded a considerable decrease in the number of domestic tourists last year, while the number of foreign visitors remained on the level of 1997. The number of domestic visitors was down by 3 percent to 821,267, while the number of foreign tourists remained unchanged at 975,950. Between January and December 1998, domestic guests allowed for 3,229,061 tourist nights and foreign for 3,061,164 nights. The figures were down by two and one percent, respectively. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Tourism Promoted Abroad \par \par \uc2  Slovenia participated for the first time at the Holiday World Experience in Dublin from 21 through 24 January. The promotion was organized by the Slovene Tourist Board (CPTS), air carrier Adria Airways and the Globtur travel agency in cooperation with Crystal Holidays of Great Britain. The CPTS believes Ireland is an interesting market for the Slovene tourist industry, especially after Adria has introduced regular flights between Ljubljana and Dublin. The most favourite Slovene destination of British and Irish tourists is Alpine lakes and the mountains. The Slovene tourist industry is currently promoted also at the IRU fair in Austria's Linz as well as at a number of penal discussions promoting Europe in U.S. towns. The latter promotion is part of activities of the European Tourist Commission. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS January's Inflation Totals 1 Percent \par \par \uc2  Consumer prices have increased by 1 percent in January, putting the January inflation rate at 1 percent. A year-to-year rise in consumer prices is 6.1 percent.The National Statistical Office has said the sharpest increase was recorded in prices of food and nonalcoholic drinks (2.7) and in prices of communications services (1.6); the latter were up mostly due to a price rise in some postal services (6.7416000n average). \par \par \uc2  Volume of Industrial Production Up 3.7 Percent in 1998 \par \par \uc2  The manufacturing sector increased the volume of its production by 3.7 percent last year from a year earlier, the National Office of Statistics has said. At the end of 1998 the growth of productivity in industrial companies exceeded the growth in 1997 by 5.4 percent as a result of reduction in workers and increase in production in 1998. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Shares of Pharmaceuticals Companies in the Lead \par \par \uc2  The last week saw a 4.6 percent rise in the SBI index, which closed the week at 1886.87 points after gaining 83.51 points. The BIO index edged up 2.64 points to 110.31 points. Fifteen shares were up and twelve down in the Friday-to-Friday period. Major rises were recorded in the shares of two rivals in the pharmaceutical industry. Gaining 14.4 percent, Lek closed the week at SIT 44,284, and Krka, gaining 12.8 percent, reached SIT 28,864 on Friday. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES No Major Changes \par \par \uc2  In the past week the euro increased by SIT 0.07 to SIT 188.81 and the U.S. dollar by SIT 2.76 to SIT 165.87 according to the mean exchange rate of the Bank of Slovenia. Gaining SIT 0.03 to SIT SIT 96.54, another foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the rising side was the Deutsche mark. The Swiss franc, on the other hand, lost SIT 0,80 to SIT 117.06. Both the Austrian schilling and Italian lira remained unchanged. One schilling cost SIT 13.72 and 100 liras SIT 9.75. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1999 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010911dv1v000d7\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovenia's Economy from 20 through 26 September. \b0\par\par\uc2 3,564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 1998\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 27 September (STA) - After hard negotiations Minister of Economic Activities Metod Dragonja and Board Director of the Petrol oil company Janez Lotric signed a contract for the Nafta Lendava refinery on 24 September. It provides conditions for the short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the refinery. It determines the rights and obligations of both owners - Petrol has a 55-percent stake in Nafta Lendava, while the rest is owned by the state - until 30 August next year. The state will stand surety for Nafta Lendava to take a loan of SIT 500 million (US$ 3 million) to start the production. To subsidize Nafta Lendava's loss from day-to-day \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  the state is to earmark up to SIT 65 million per month. \par \par \uc2  The state pledged to provide current assets of SIT 500 million by means of a special contract binding the Commodity Reserves Fund to buy oil, its derivatives or tanks from Nafta. Last week the government in addition passed a decree abolishing the eco tax on fuel oil of domestic production, while the state will ensure Nafta the supply of natural gas at a price enabling the refinery to manufacture methanol at a competitive price. Petrol pledged to supply 25,000 tons of crude oil to Nafta each month. \par \par \uc2  Both contractors agreed not to burden Nafta with the costs resulting from the financing of old liabilities until the end of August 1999. They also expressed readiness to change the claims - Petrol's amount to about two billion tolars and those of the state to SIT 3.5 billion - to the company's capital stock. \par \par \uc2  CHRONOLOGY \par \par \uc2  Wednesday/23 September - Brdo pri Kranju: Government started debate on the 1999 draft budget. Wednesday/23 September - Maribor: Three-day 1st Slovene Congress Transport, Traffic, Logistics, with international attendance was opened. Thursday/24 September - Ljubljana: Health care trade unions, the Health Care Chamber and the Ministry of Health signed an agreement on the rider to the Collective Agreement for health care and an accord on the measures to improve the organization and quality of health care. Friday/25 September - Ljubljana: The Slovene Railway Union called a six-hour warning strike for 8 October at the Ljubljana-Zalog and Celje-Tovorna shunting stations. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENIA - EUROPEAN UNION Slovenia At Multilateral Screening for Agriculture \par \par \uc2  Slovenia attended on 21 and 22 September the multilateral screening for the agriculture section covering fruit and vegetable. According to delegation leader, Agriculture Ministry State Secretary Franc But, the meeting showed the screened area would not pose particular problems, but other agriculture areas, cereals in particular, are expected to prove more demanding. To align with the European Union, Slovenia will have to amend its legislation and, most importantly, set up appropriate institutions. Harmonization of Slovenia's with the EU legislation for fruit and vegetables will be examined in the bilateral screening next week, on 29 and 30 September. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia Attends Multilateral Screening for Customs Union \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's adjustment to EU rules for the customs union will require a lot of work to build up staff and appropriate institutions, primarily the customs service, Vojka Ravbar, state secretary in the Ministry of Economic Relations and Development, who headed the Slovene delegation at the two-day multilateral screening, said after the screening session on Thursday. She added an important task will be to adjust customs tariffs, which Slovenia plans to carry out gradually until its accession to the Union. Slovenia has a quality customs service, but it will need to continue investing in its development according to Ravbar. By joining the EU Slovenia will enter an area without internal borders that controls goods on entry or exit from the common area under a unified system. In that, Ravbar said, of particular importance will be the EU information system used to monitor incoming and outgoing goods. \par \par \uc2  EU: External Factors Influence Economic Situation in Slovenia \par \par \uc2  The recovery of EU economies had a positive influence on the macroeconomic situation in Slovenia, the European Commission said in its autumn report on the economic situation in EU candidates. A higher growth rate of Slovenia's GDP was chiefly a result of an increase in demand for Slovene exports in the EU. A low salary growth rate, however, made home demand rather weak. As external factors brought about a higher growth rate of GDP, the European Commission stressed the need for reforms that would improve public finances, facilitate rehabilitation of companies and the financial sector, and resulted further privatisation of state-owned companies. \par \par \uc2  NATIONAL BUDGET Government Opens Budget Debate \par \par \uc2  The government started the debate on the 1999 draft budget bill during its regular session at Brdo pri Kranju on Wednesday. The key goal of the government's fiscal-budgetary policy in 1999 is a maximum public finance deficit of less than one percent GDP, Premier Janez Drnovsek said at the session. The draft budget proposes maximum government expenditure of SIT 933 billion, up SIT 63 billion from the 1998 budget. Nominal growth of government expenditure is planned not to exceed 7 percent. On the income side, the budget plans SIT 900 billion of revenues. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF SLOVENIA \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Council Expects Balanced 1999 Budget \par \par \uc2  Dealing with monetary and economic trends at home and abroad, the Council of the Bank of Slovenia established at its latest session that the recent events on world markets announced a possible deterioration of the situation in some large parts of the world, for which reason the period of favourable export demand could change very quickly for Slovenia. This can have a negative effect on Slovenia's further economic growth, the council of Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assessed. In order that the process of stabilization and the introduction of necessary structural reforms should continue successfully, the council expects the government and the parliament to balance the 1999 national budget and to ensure budgetary expenses do not increase in real terms. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Foreign Money Markets Ends \par \par \uc2  Following the resolution of the governor of the Bank of Slovenia and in agreement with the 5th item of the bid for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign money markets by means of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying rate dating from 10 September, \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the period of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended on 21 September. The banks involved are not obliged to sustain the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying rate as of Tuesday, 22 September, the Bank of Slovenia said. \par \par \uc2  CHAMBER OF COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY OF SLOVENIA Chamber's Position on 1999 Budget Bill \par \par \uc2  The Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia (GZS) supports a development-oriented budget, but says the planned expenditure of SIT 930 billion is excessive. In its position on the 1999 draft budget, adopted on Thursday, the GZS also said the proposed budget deficit should not be spent on day-to-day expenditure. The position of exporters has already deteriorated because of the oncoming wave of global recession that resulted mainly from the crises in Russia, Asia and the Latin America. According to the Executive Committee, the government should pay special attention to downward trends in foreign markets and to deteriorating conditions for doing business of the Slovene industry, and take immediate action to improve business conditions primarily for exporters. \par \par \uc2  FAIRS Craftsmanship Fair Concludes \par \par \uc2  Having received about 210,000 visitors, the 31st International Craftsmanship Fair closed in Celje on 20 September. It featured 1,794 exhibitioners from 30 countries. The exhibition grounds extended over 55,000 square meters. \par \par \uc2  Furniture Fair Opens in Ljubljana \par \par \uc2  The 9th Ljubljana Furniture Fair was held from Tuesday through Sunday. The fair, presenting the latest products of furniture industry, was attended by 324 companies from 17 countries. All leading Slovene furniture manufacturers were present as well as a number of foreign producers. Furniture and other equipment for homes as well as for offices were exhibited at the fair. \par \par \uc2  First Show of Tourism, Hotel Services and Gastronomy \par \par \uc2  The first international fair of tourism, hotel services and gastronomy, EXPO-TAG, opened in the Portoroz Marina on 24 September. Covering the area of 1,000 square meters, the show features 50 exhibitors from Slovenia and Italy. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Color Expects Lower Income This Year \par \par \uc2  Color, Medvode-based chemical industry, recorded sales revenue of SIT 3.3 billion in the first six months of this year. The overall profit amounted to SIT 211 million. Color sells 28 percent of its production to the former Soviet Union countries. General Manager Bojan Dolar said the company would not fulfill its plans by the end of the year. The deviation from the envisaged results will depend upon further developments in Russia, but the profit will be lower than expected. \par \par \uc2  Sava Kranj Records Increase in Sales \par \par \uc2  Sava of Kranj turned over SIT 17 billion in the first half-year, an increase of 5.2 percent over the same period last year. The volume of production was up by 3.7 percent in the period. According to the consolidated profit and loss account, the half-year net profit of the pneumatics producer amounted to SIT 560 million, 42.4 percent up from the same period last year. Sava exports most of its production, with the share of exports in overall sales surmounting 86 percent. \par \par \uc2  Droga Successful in Half-Year \par \par \uc2  Droga food industry recorded an income of SIT 5.9 billion in the first six months of this year. The nett profit amounted to SIT 357 million, five percent up from the same period last year. The Portoroz-based company looks upon its half-year \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as successful. According to Finance Director Assistant Ales Skraba, a nett profit of over a billion tolars is expected by the end of the year. The company's performance in the first half-year was strongly influenced by relatively high inflation in the first quarter and mild winter. \par \par \uc2  Gorenje Business Results in the First Half of 1998 \par \par \uc2  President of Gorenje Joze Stanic estimates the company's business results in the first half of 1998 were good, although they could be better. In the first six months Gorenje's company Gospodinjski aparati (kitchen appliances) manufactured over one million appliances with the turnover totalling 324 million German mark, which is a 15-percent increase over the same period last year. The Gorenje management, however, was not satisfied with the net profit that was only seven million mark instead of the expected 14 million. Gorenje remains the biggest Slovene manufacturing company and Slovenia's most important net exporter. Recently the sales have been very good in Germany, France, Croatia, Austria, Denmark and Poland, while in Russia they have been, due to the crisis, worse than planned, Gorenje PR department announced. \par \par \uc2  Petrol Sells Less Oil Derivatives \par \par \uc2  The Petrol business group recorded a 12-percent fall in sales of oil derivatives in the first half of the year, which reduced its clear revenues by 11 percent in nominal terms in comparison with the January-to-June period 1997. A clear profit of nearly SIT 520 million is 28 percent lower from the same period last year. Return on equity fell to 4.8 percent at the annual level. Petrol has explained its poor business results with a fall in the consumption of oil in Slovenia, which was down 10 percent in the first half of the year after a long period of steady growth; as a result of measures taken by neighbouring countries much less oil derivatives were sold in the regions along the state borders (the so called oil tourism), where over a fourth of all Petrol petrol stations are based. \par \par \uc2  Kovinotehna Incurs a Loss in Half-Year \par \par \uc2  Kovinotehna, a Celje-based international trade company, incurred a loss of over SIT 358 million in the first half of 1998, while it had made a profit of SIT 11.2 million in the same period last year. The main reasons for bad performance are according to Board Director Saso Gerzina high extraordinary expenditures resulting from considerable write-offs and too large amount of tied-up current assets, especially stocks. Kovinotehna \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  revenues amounted to SIT 17.4 billion in the first six months of this year, a 10-percent increase over the same period last year. Revenues from the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Kovinotehna group totalled about SIT 21 billion, while the loss amounted to SIT 354 million. \par \par \uc2  Donit Filter to Expand Production with New Owner \par \par \uc2  The company Donit Filter, based in Medvode outside Ljubljana, registered as Filtrauto Slovenija Ltd. this summer after French Filtrauto bought it in June last year, allowing it to continue marketing its products under the Donit Filter brand name. Providing fresh capital upon purchase and transferring part of its production to Slovenia, the new owner is currently engaged in preparations for Filtrauto Slovenija to build a new production facility that would unite all production on one single location. The company this year also plans to start production of cockpit filters, next year it is to move all production to new premises, while in 2001 it plans to make SIT 3.2 billion (US$ 20 million) of turnover with the annual production of 9.1 billion filters, according to its director general Krajnc. The company is currently still in the red, with its losses in 1997 amounting to SIT 200 million (US$ 1.25 million). Director general Krajnc expects the business result to become positive after moving to the new factory. \par \par \uc2  Ormoz Sugar Factory Starts Production of Sugar \par \par \uc2  The Ormoz-based sugar factory is to start the processing of this year's sugar beet later this week. This year the factory in Ormoz, eastern Slovenia, is to buy 350,000 tons of sugar beet; in three months some 45,000 to 47,000 tons of sugar are to be made, depending on the contents of sugar in the beet. Last year the factory bought 288,000 tons of beet, making slightly less than 43,000 tons of sugar. Farmers tend to increase the production of sugar beet and by the year 2002 Slovenia should cover its needs - some 80,000 tons of sugar per year - with the domestic production. According to Dogsa, this would be a good basis for the definition of quota when entering the European Union, as the quotas are defined in accordance with the average production in five years' time.The Ormoz factory, employing 309 people, is owned by Dutch Consun (57-percent stake) and Italian Sucadria (25-percent stake). Last year's turnover totalled SIT 6.351 billion, while the net profit was SIT 89 million. This year both are to be increased, although the factory is facing problems due to high price of beet and low price of sugar. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENE RAILWAYS Slovene Railways Sign Contract to Purchase Pendolino Trains \par \par \uc2  Slovene Railways (SZ) and the Italian FiatFerroviaria company signed a contract of SIT 4.33 billion on Monday for the Slovene company to purchase three new Pendolino trains. The contract covers three trains, spare parts, a special maintaining equipment and the necessary documentation. To pay for the order, SZ will take a loan from a consortium of Slovene banks, for which the government has already issued a guarantee through passing a special law. The trains, whose maximum speed is 200 km/h, will be delivered in the middle of 2000, and will run between the towns of Ljubljana and Maribor. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENE IRONWORKS Gov't Examines Performance of Slovene Ironworks \par \par \uc2  The government examined the rehabilitation process of the Slovene Ironworks at its session of 24 September. According to State Secretary Boris Sustar, the Slovene Ironworks output was increasing both in terms of its size and value, although the company Acroni was lagging behind the rehabilitation plan. The Slovene Ironworks have cut the losses by half to SIT 1.3 billion in the first six months of this year. This marks an important step forward from last year, but it is still far from the objective set in the rehabilitation plan. The government believes the rehabilitation as well as the privatization processes should be accelerated, complementing it by internal management rationalization. According to Sustar, demand for thick stainless plates has already decreased because of the anti-dumping procedure against the Slovene Ironworks. \par \par \uc2  Expert Group Set up to Refute Allegations of Dumping \par \par \uc2  A group of domestic and foreign experts has been set up by the Slovene Ironworks and Acroni of Jesenice to refute the allegations about the dumping of stainless steel which moved the European Commission to launch an inquiry against Acroni. The Slovene Ironworks and Acroni see the complaint as a legitimate move in their competitors' struggle for the market. Last week the ironworks received a comprehensive and demanding questionnaire from the EU. They are determined to explain by means of appropriate answers that the sale of stainless steel was not a case of dumping. \par \par \uc2  Internal Distribution of Shares Begins in Ironworks \par \par \uc2  The management of the Slovene Ironworks decided, in agreement with the Privatization Act, that the internal distribution of shares to the employed and former employed in the companies of the concern start on 25 September. The distribution will conclude on 30 October. The beneficiaries - the employed, former employed and the retired - will be able to exchange their privatisation vouchers and receipts of unpaid salaries for shares. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM This Year's Tourist Season Successful \par \par \uc2  Minister of Small Business and Tourism Janko Razgorsek met heads of 36 largest hotel and health resort companies in Slovenia on 21 September. According to him, this year's season has been successful so far. By the end of August the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  influx from hotel, health resort and casino services was by about 15 percent higher than in the same period last year. Sales of petrol and cigarets to foreign subjects, however, were down on account of measures taken by Italy and Austria, so the total of this year's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  influx from tourism will be lower than last year's. \par \par \uc2  Logo of Slovene Tourism on Adria Airways Aircraft \par \par \uc2  Five aircraft of Slovene air carrier Adria Airways will now carry the logo of Slovene tourism - the word Slovenia surrounded by flowers. Adria Airways last year brought to Slovenia some 44,000 foreign tourists, which is a 7-percent increase over the year 1997. Most tourists flew in from Germany, followed by Great Britain, Russia, Israel, Denmark, the Netherlands, Switzerland, Spain and Turkey. The increase is a consequence of cooperation between Adria and the Centre for Tourism Promotion - the centre's representatives namely often work in Adria's offices abroad. \par \par \uc2  TRAFFIC Contract Signed for the Reconstruction of Logatec-Robic Road \par \par \uc2  The national Direction for Roads signed a contract with four contractors on 24 September, launching the reconstruction of a 56-kilometer long section of the main road between Logatec and Robic. The contract amounts to SIT 1.468 billion. The project will be funded by the loan of the European Bank of Reconstruction and Development. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Truckers Need More Permits for the EU \par \par \uc2  The Ministry of Transport and Communications has responded to truck drivers' warnings of the lack of permits for international haulage. The ministry proposed that the Chamber of Commerce and Industry as well as the Chamber of Trade should have a more active role in the distribution of permits in the future. As the ministry established, permits for the EU countries are lacking most, while the situation is reversed in the case of East European countries. As far as Italy is concerned, only eight percent of 53,000 permits are used properly (in both directions), the ministry found. In the beginning of October the Slovene-Italian commission is expected to meet and the Slovene side will try to negotiate an increase in the quota of permits. It will in addition ask the Italian side to supply the information about the cases of fake permits detected with Slovene truck drivers so that the ministry will be able to start legal actions against the perpetrators. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS Lower GDP Growth in Q2 \par \par \uc2  Following a high, 6.5 percent growth rate in the first quarter of the year, GDP growth was much lower in the second quarter. Y/Y growth over Q2 1997 was 3.2 percent, while compared to Q1 1998 it was .5 percent. GDP growth in the first six months of 1998 over the same period last year was 4.8 percent. Compared to the second quarter of 1997, GDP growth was the highest, 5.8 percent, in the traffic, warehousing and communications sectors. Somewhat lower were the figures for financial services (5.6) and the processing sector (4.7). Basic industries recorded 3.7 percent annual growth, while service 2.6 percent growth. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Shares Up on the Average \par \par \uc2  Prices of shares on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange increased on the average last week. The SBI share index gained 34.87 point and concluded the week at 1676.36 point. The BIO bond index was up too, by 1.12 point to SIT 105.42. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES U.S. Dollar Down \par \par \uc2  The mean price of the U.S dollar was down by three tolars to SIT 157.25 in a week according to the Bank of Slovenia. The Swiss franc depreciated by SIT 0.6 to SIT 114.29, while other major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not change much: the German mark was up by SIT 0.04 to SIT 94.45, the Austrian schilling stayed put at SIT 13.42, as did 100 Italian liras at SIT 9.55. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010927du9r001v4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
