\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia \highlight\uc2  ends tolar rate  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 1998\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it had concluded its tolar exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that began on September 14. \par \par \uc2  "From Tuesday, September 22, the chosen banks are no longer obliged to maintain the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange rate," the bank said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  From September 14, banks were buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from companies at a rate of 94.7 tolars to the German mark, up from 94.4 tolars at the start of the month. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to slow the appreciation of the tolar. \par \par \uc2  Bank foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying rates from companies were at the start of September almost the same as those at the beginning of the year although consumer prices have risen 3.8 percent over the first eight months of 1998. \par \par \uc2  ((Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9m01orj\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 6 through 12 September. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1998\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 13 September (STA) - Following Croatia's comments on the government decree on the Krsko Nuclear Plant's reorganization into a public company, the Slovene side on Monday submitted its stands on the comments to Croatia. The document containing Croatian comments consists of 15 points that can be summarized in three demands: co-ownership with a 50-percent stake, consensus in decision-making and terms of payment and guarantee. After a thorough examination of all the points, the Slovene side could only consider a part of them, especially in the part where both partners shared the same views, but could not accept those whose adoption would disagree with Slovene legislation. \par \par \uc2  Current developments between electricity industries of Slovenia and Croatia were discussed by the Administrative Board of the Power Industry Association at its Tuesday session. Members of the body working within the Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia (CCIS) were addressed by Ivo Banic, director of the Slovene electricity company ELES, and Alojz Kovse, state secretary for power industry. The Krsko N-plant operated at full capacity, achieving a 95.7-percent capacity utilization and generating 441,435 MWh of electricity. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Tuesday/8 September - Ljubljana: the Council of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopts a decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign money markets Wednesday/9 September - Nova Gorica: a contract signed to supply and assemble the equipment for the reconstruction of hydroelectric power plants on the lower Isonzo river Thursday/10 September - Ljubljana: the government and the Petrol oil company launch negotiations over Nafta refinery Thursday/10 September - Ljubljana: the government adopts a strategy of shoe industry restructuring Friday/11 September - Prague: CEFTA summit begins Friday/11 September - Celje: the 31st International Craftsmanship Fair opens \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Council Decides for \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Foreign Money Markets \par \par \uc2  The Council of the Bank of Slovenia discussed a banking bill and adopted several resolutions at a session on Tuesday. It passed a decree on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on foreign money markets by means of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying rate. Banks chosen through tenders will buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the rate SIT 94.7 for one German mark as of 14 September. The banks' rate for the purchase of foreign cash in exchange offices may not be lower than 0.2 percent in case of German marks or 0.4 percent in case of other foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  GOVERNMENT Gov't and Petrol Negotiate on Nafta Refinery \par \par \uc2  Chairman of the Board of Petrol Janez Lotric and Minister of Economic Activities Metod Dragonja on Thursday began a new round of negotiations over the contents of a contract between the oil company and the government to re-start the Nafta Lendava refinery. On Friday working groups harmonized a draft contract, having negotiated compromise solutions in some points. These were discussed by Petrol Supervisory Board on the same day. In the talks the state insisted on it that Petrol secure crude oil supplies so that the refinery could start operating even this month. \par \par \uc2  Gov't Adopts Shoe Industry Restructuring Strategy \par \par \uc2  The government adopted a strategy of restructuring and jobs renewal in the shoe industry on Thursday. The strategy had been prepared by the Ministry of Labour, Family and Social Affairs in cooperation with the Ministry of Economic Activities. The document envisages certain financial investments, by the state as well as by individual companies, Labour Ministry State Secretary Zdenka Kovac told a news conference after the cabinet session. The total of required investments over the following years is estimated at SIT 14 billion. The state is to provide three to four billion tolars in the next five years. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL ECONOMIC COOPERATION Slovenia Attends CEFTA Summit in Prague \par \par \uc2  A two-day meeting of Central European Free Trade Agreement (CEFTA) member countries' prime ministers began in the Czech capital on Friday. It gathered heads of governments of the Czech Republic, Hungary, Poland, Rumania, Slovakia, Slovenia and for the first time also Bulgaria. Addressing his counterparts, Slovene Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek made a special note of the signing of Additional Protocol No. VI to CEFTA last December. It stipulates liberalization of trade in agricultural products between Slovenia and CEFTA members. The seven CEFTA prime ministers on Friday signed a final declaration stressing CEFTA's contribution to the development and growth of trade among its member states as well as its contribution to a speedier accession to the European Union. \par \par \uc2  Chamber of Economy Delegation Visits Slovakia \par \par \uc2  A 23-strong Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia (CCIS) delegation led by CCIS Chairman Jozko Cuk started a three-day visit to Slovakia Tuesday, during which it held a business conference and several business meetings with Slovak partners in Bratislava, Nitra and Kosice, where representatives of 17 Slovene enterprises discussed concrete forms and possibilities for business cooperation. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Business Representatives also in Siberia \par \par \uc2  A Slovene economic delegation on Tuesday participated in a business conference in Tyumen, the capital of a west Siberian region of the same name, which the delegation visited led by the Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia (CCIS). The meeting was attended by ten representatives of Slovene companies and 46 Russian counterparts. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENE DEVELOPMENT COMPANY Restructuring Process Successful \par \par \uc2  The restructuring process in the enterprises Litostroj and Andria Mobil is getting on very well, and positive results have also been recorded in other companies run by the Slovene Development Company (SRD), SRD officials said in a news conference on Wednesday. SRD board of managers also presented the sale of Maribor's textiles manufacturer MTT, the activities concerning the Rogaska health resort and the acquisition of the 90 percent stake in the Goricane Paper Mill. SRD transformed part of its claims to a capital investment of DM 6 million, to provide easier access to bank loans to Goricane. At the end of July, SRD also bough the Austrian stake in the company, becoming the over 90 percent owner of the paper mill. SRD chairman of the board, Bogdan Topic also introduced a new member of the board, Barbara Kuerner Cad, a former economic court judge. \par \par \uc2  SLOVENE EXPORT CORPORATION Slovene Export Corporation, Hermes Sign Insurance Contract \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Export Corporation (SID) and Germany's exports-credit agency Hermes signed a joint insurance contract in Hamburg on Thursday. The contract, signed by SID chairman of the board Marjan Kramar and president of Hermes, Hans Janus, is intended to promote exports of Slovene and German enterprises by providing insurance and more favourable funding for joint export projects and investments in third countries. It will also joint actions by enterprises from both countries in third markets. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM Incomes from Tourism This Year Lower than Expected \par \par \uc2  Slovene tourism foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  influx in 1998 was lower than expected, secretary of Slovene Tourist Association Alojz Soster told a news conference on Monday. According to him, the expected proceeds in tourism amounting to US$ 2 billion will not be realized. Similar trends can be also seen in other European countries where tourism is developed, the main reason for this being journeys to the more distant and exotic places, which are getting cheaper. "We will be glad if the last year's proceeds amounting to slightly more than US$ 1.2 will be achieved," Alojz Soster explained. \par \par \uc2  AGRICULTURE Agriculture and Food Industry Association on Perspectives of Accession to the EU \par \par \uc2  A lively discussion on the opportunities of Slovenia's food industry in accession to the EU was conducted among Agriculture and Food Industry Association officials at the CCIS headquarters on Tuesday. The Slovene food industry is on the right path according to the EU report, there is nevertheless still much to be done, CCIS representatives maintained. Among the required measures, the EU report includes an increase in investments needed to improve on the industry's efficiency and especially restructuring and merging of Slovene food companies to increase their competitive power. \par \par \uc2  CEFTA Agreement of Little Benefit to Slovene Agriculture \par \par \uc2  The study on the Slovene agriculture and the CEFTA agreement, which was presented at the Zootechnical Department of the Biotechnical Faculty at Domzale on Monday, shows the Slovene agriculture has not profited much from the CEFTA agreement. It says the agreement has distinctive deficiencies and provides opportunities only to a limited number of branches. The agreement's hazards are so great that they endanger the basic strategic principles of development of the Slovene agriculture, also from the aspect of Slovenia's accession to the EU. The study claims Slovene farmers' incomes are very likely to decrease or, at the best, stay at the present level. \par \par \uc2  POWER INDUSTRY Nuclear Power in Central Europe Discussed at Catez \par \par \uc2  A regular annual international expert meeting of the Nuclear Expert Society of Slovenia (DJSS) was held at the Terme Catez health resort from Tuesday through Thursday. The meeting on 'Nuclear Energy in Central Europe' featured presentations of the latest achievements in the field of nuclear safety, papers on \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  problems of research reactors and nuclear power plants as well as disposal of nuclear waste. The participants talked about the progress in the technical reconstruction of the Krsko Nuclear Power Plant in Thursday's round-table discussion. For the first time this year several papers focused on the use of nuclear methods in medicine, technics and industry. \par \par \uc2  Isonzo Hydroelectric Power Plants Sign Contract on Reconstruction \par \par \uc2  A contract on the supply and assembling of the equipment for the reconstruction of the Doblar II and Plave II hydroelectric power plants on the lower Isonzo River was signed on Wednesday in Nova Gorica (west) by representatives of Soske elektrarne of Nova Gorica, Rudis of Trbovlje, Litostroj of Ljubljana and Koncar of Croatia. The investment value of the equipment according to the contract is SIT 2.5 billion. Electrical generator sets of both power plants will have a joint capacity of 58.4 MW (Doblar 40 MW and Plave 18.4 MW). \par \par \uc2  TRANSPORT AND COMMUNICATIONS A By-Pass Opened at Ormoz \par \par \uc2  A 3.75 kilometer motorway by-pass around Ormoz, eastern Slovenia, that will ensure smooth and safe traffic in the region, was opened by the Minister of Transport and Communications on Friday. The Ormoz by-pass constitutes the first part of the new motorway from Slovenjska Bistrica via Ptuj to Ormoz, to be partially renovated or constructed until 2002 under the national motorway construction programme. The by-pass was built in 14 months by building companies GIZ Gradis and SCT Ljubljana together with Cestno podjetje Celje. The investment is worth SIT 1.4 billion (about US$ 8.5 million). \par \par \uc2  SMALL BUSINESS A Seminar on How to Promote Development of Small Business \par \par \uc2  The Faculty of Business and Economics in Maribor on Monday hosted a seminar on encouragements for a quicker development of small business in Slovenia organized by the Ministry of Small Business and Tourism. Minister Janko Razgorsek said assistance was most needed in companies' access to financial funds, business knowledge and experience, access to information, infrastructure, technology, innovation, development and internationalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  FAIRS Minister Razgorsek Opens 31st International Craftsmanship Fair \par \par \uc2  Minister of Small Business and Tourism Janko Razgorsek on Friday opened the 31st International Craftsmanship Fair (MOS), the second largest craftsmanship and business fair exhibition in Europe and one of the key business events in Slovenia. This year the fair, covering 55,000 square meters of exhibition surfaces, is attended by 1794 exhibitors from 30 countries. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES New Lek-Bosnalijek Plant Opened in Sarajevo \par \par \uc2  Slovene President Milan Kucan and president of the Bosnian presidency, Alija Izetbegovic on Monday opened the renovated and modernized solid drug forms factory of the Bosnalijek corporation, in which the Slovene pharmaceuticals maker Lek is present with a US$ 1.5 million investment. In May this year Lek and Bosnalijek signed another contract for the equipment installation and technological installation worth 0.5 million D-marks. The works are scheduled to finish in October. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Ironworks Incur a Loss of SIT 1.6 billion in Seven Months \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Ironworks Group made SIT 1.6 billion of losses or SIT 400 more than expected in the first seven months of the year, with Acroni Jesenice accounting for the bulk of the loss of SIT 1.5 billion, chairman of the board, Miran Tasic told a news conference on Thursday. Tasic condemned the proposed anti-dumping procedure that was to be started against Acroni Jesenice by the European steel builders association Eurofer because of Acroni's exports of rolled steel plates. Tasic tackled also the issue of the ironworks' privatization and rehabilitation. General Manager of Acroni Vasilij Preseren met with European Commission representatives on Friday in an attempt to convince them that the allegations about dumping of stainless steel by the Slovene company were not grounded. \par \par \uc2  Simobil Increases Initial Capital by SIT 1.7 Billion \par \par \uc2  The management of Simobil, joint-stock company providing telecommunication services, passed a resolution to increase the initial capital by 1.7 billion from SIT 3 million to SIT 1.703 billion. The decision to increase the capital by shareholders' additional investments was adopted at the second general meeting of the company. \par \par \uc2  Union Brewery Awarded Gold Medals for Quality \par \par \uc2  Monde Selection, Brussels-based international institute of quality assessment, this year awarded gold medals for quality to six products of the Union brewery: beers Union and Union Pils, beer and lemon juice beverage Union Radler, ice tea Sola peach and red cranberry flavors and mineral water Zala. The latter was awarded a great gold medal. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS Average Catering Prices remained Unchanged in August \par \par \uc2  Prices of catering in August 1998 on the average retained the level of a month before. Catering prices were up by 5.8 percent from December 1997 and up by eight percent from August 1997. \par \par \uc2  Exports above US$ 800 Million for the First Time in July \par \par \uc2  Slovene exports amounted to US$ 818.7 million in July and imports to 840.8 million. Trade deficit was US$ 22.1 million, while exports/imports ratio was 97.4 percent, up from July 1997 when trade deficit was at US$ 56.7 million and exports/imports coverage 93.1 percent, the National Statistical Office said. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE SBI Index Down Again \par \par \uc2  Fourteen shares depreciated and ten appreciated in stock quotations of the Ljubljana Stock exchange last week. The SBI share index lost 29.41 point and concluded the week at 1769.51 point. The BIO bonds index slid by 0.44 point to 104.90 point. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Most Major Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Depreciates \par \par \uc2  Apart from the Swiss franc and Austrian schilling, major foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated last week according to the mean rate of the Bank of Slovenia. The franc was up by SIT 0.31 to SIT 115.07, while the schilling stayed put at SIT 13.41. The U.S. dollar was down by SIT 4.99 to SIT 159.47, the German mark by 0.01 to SIT 94.37 and 100 Italian liras by SIT 0.01 to SIT 9.55. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010927du9d00284\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
