\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia \highlight\uc2  cenbank says to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on tolar market. \b0\par\par\uc2 162 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1998\par 16:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday it would begin tolar exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "From September 14 onwards the chosen banks will be buying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a rate of 94.7 tolars to the German mark," the bank said in a statement. \par \par \uc2  Currently banks buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from companies at a rate of about 94.4 tolars to the mark. \par \par \uc2  The bank did not say at what rate it would buy \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the banks in question. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to slow down the appreciation of the tolar. \par \par \uc2  Current bank foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying rates from companies are almost the same as those at the start of the year although consumer prices have risen 3.8 percent since the start of 1998. \par \par \uc2  ((Marja Novak, Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9a00t4h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from 5 to 11 July. \b0\par\par\uc2 2,301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 1998\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 12 July (STA) - Prime Minister Janez Drnovsek's economic college, which comprises ministers dealing with economic affairs, discussed Monday the analysis of economic trends in 1998 and a development projection for next two years, placing special emphasis on drafting the 1999-2000 national budget, basic macroeconomic projections and conditions in public finance. Based on the latest data, economic ministers again confirmed that positive economic trends in Slovenia continued. \par \par \uc2  The ministers also established that results of the spring report 1998 by the Institute for Macroeconomic Analyses and Development proved the government was staying firmly on course towards macroeconomic objectives and had this year achieved the set goals. About six percent higher economic growth than predicted in the beginning of the year was recorded in the first quarter of 1998. GDP growth is expected to at least topple four percent. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Monday/6 July - Brussels: Slovenia with EU on economic priorities Wednesday/8 July - Ljubljana: Economic-Social Council passes grey economy prevention bill Wednesday/8 July - Ljubljana: Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  ends period of using \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase exchange rate Wednesday/8 July - Ljubljana: Government passes regulation setting the procedure of closing tally accounts of privatisation voucher holders and the use of privatisation vouchers Thursday/9 July - Ljubljana: Government inter alia debates situation in the processing industry Friday/10 July - Potoroz: Splosna plovba supervisory board opts for privatisation and the search of strategic partner Friday/10 July - Krsko: Krsko Nuclear Power Plant Interim Board of Directors session ends unsuccessfully \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA\b0 \highlight\uc2  Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Has Stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Foreign Money Markets \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced the period of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by setting purchase prices of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended on Wednesday. The banks selected according to the bid are no longer obliged to keep up the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase price as of 9 July. \par \par \uc2  GOVERNMENT Hike in Electricity Price Rejected; Reports on Processing Industry and Slovene Ironworks Discussed \par \par \uc2  The government rejected at its session Thursday a proposal made by Eles, a power transmission enterprise, to rise the price of electricity by 14.5 percent as of 1 July. The latest rise took place on 1 January (8 percent). Reports prepared by the Economics Minister on the situation in the processing industry were confirmed by the government, together with measures to improve the condition of the procession industry which will be sent to parliament. The government also debated the report on the iron-manufacturing companies gathered in the Slovene Ironworks (SZ), establishing that reorganization in the branch was bearing fruit. \par \par \uc2  INTERNATIONAL ACTIVITIES Multilateral Screening for Fisheries \par \par \uc2  The Slovene delegation on Monday attended the multilateral screening, with the other five European Union membership applicants, for the common fisheries policy. Delegations of Slovenia, Poland, the Czech Republic, Hungary, Estonia and Cyprus and European Commission enlargement group members examined the development of capacity to implement and enforce the common fisheries legislation. Bilateral screening for fisheries for Slovenia is scheduled for 14 July. \par \par \uc2  EU-Slovenia Subcommittee about Mid-Term Guidelines for Economic Development \par \par \uc2  The EU-Slovenia Subcommittee on Economic Issues was harmonizing Monday a draft Joint Assessment of Mid-Term Economic Policy Priorities of Slovenia. Divided in three chapters, the Joint Assessment analyzes the current economic situation in Slovenia, points to some economic problems and manners of settling them plus defines a medium-term macroeconomic target scenario. It in fact represents a common idea of what policy should be adopted to transform successfully the Slovene economy and prepare it for membership of the EU. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC-SOCIAL COUNCIL Grey Economy Prevention Bill Should be Passed Soon \par \par \uc2  The Economic and Social Council on Wednesday discussed and passed a bill aimed to prevent black economy. Social partners said the bill was good and had to be adopted as soon as possible. It will make a major contribution to improve the situation in the area, Minister of Labour and Council Chairman Anton Rop said. The session, which was attended also by European Affairs Minister Igor Bavcar, passed the decision to appoint representatives of the trade unions and employers, one of each, to the working groups reviewing the agreement of Slovenia's legislation with that of the EU. Grey economy has been increasing constantly over the last years also in Slovenia, where the share of moonlighting amounts to 17-22 percent of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  PRIVATISATION Gov't Passes Decree to Close Tally Accounts of Voucher Owners \par \par \uc2  The government on Wednesday issued a decree on the procedure to close tally accounts of privatisation voucher owners and enable the use of vouchers on the basis of the act on the conclusion of privatisation and privatisation of legal entities owned by the Slovene Development Company. The decree took immediate effect, It regulates the procedure of tally accounts closing; the procedure of exchange of vouchers for shares in internal distribution or internal buy-out in those companies in which these will be carried out by the end of October as well as the procedure of using vouchers in privatisation of the Triglav National Park Development Promotion Fund. \par \par \uc2  Privatisation Report in Slovene, English and German \par \par \uc2  The national Agency for Restructuring and Privatisation on Thursday presented a publication Privatisation Report, published in Slovene, English and German. According to the Report, the performance of privatised companies has improved during the last 3 years, but there is quite a difference between internally owned companies and companies controlled by outside owners. \par \par \uc2  VALUE ADDED TAX Finance Ministry on VAT \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday organized the first working meeting with the press concerned with the introduction of value added tax. From now on such meetings will take place every week. The first one dealt with two topics: VAT exemption and VAT zero rate. \par \par \uc2  CONFERENCES Seminar within the 'Educate for Europe' Programme \par \par \uc2  The Slovene Management Research Institute on Tuesday organized a seminar on 'Liberalization, Privatization, Integration and Concentration on Finance and Capital Markets'. The seminar was addressed by France Arhar, the governor of Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who said the privatization of the greatest Slovene commercial banks Nova Ljubljanska banka and Nova kreditna banka Maribor was not to be carried out headlong. Such educational seminars are aimed to inform the participants, the leading managers of Slovene economy, about the economic, system and market conditions of Slovenia's adjusting to Europe. \par \par \uc2  ENERGY Krsko N-Plant Generated 425,000 MWh in June \par \par \uc2  The Krsko Nuclear Power Plant operated at full capacity and 95.4-percent capacity utilization last June, generating 425,847 megawatt-hours of electricity. \par \par \uc2  Krsko Interim Board of Directors Session Unsuccessful \par \par \uc2  The interim board of directors of the Krsko nuclear power plant failed to agree on any of the items on agenda of the session held on Friday at Krsko. Krsko CEO Ivo Banic, also director of the national power company Elektro-Slovenija (Eles), said problems appeared as early as at the session agenda. The session was discontinued after four hours when due to opposing views by Croatian members the board failed to reach agreement on the first two items on the agenda, concerning the selection of an international auditor to revise Krsko \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1986-1996 period, and modernization funding. \par \par \uc2  TOURISM Higher Prices of Tourist Services Result in Less Tourist \par \par \uc2  Trends in the Slovene tourist industry ahead of the high season were presented at a news conference on Wednesday by the Ministry of Small Business and Tourism and the Slovene Tourist Board. Seemingly downward trends in the first third of 1998 resulted from the 'green' winter in the season of 1997/98, a rise in prices of tourist services, unemployment in some Western countries, the financial crisis in Asia and therefore low prices in the Far East, the fear that the Kosovo conflict might spread to Slovenia, the fact that the Slovene \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overvalued, and perhaps also the EXPO world exhibition in Lisbon and the Football World Cup, Minister of Small Business and Tourism Janko Razgorsek said. In the January-to-April period, Slovene tourist destinations were visited by 1 percent less tourists than in the same period 1997, with overnight stays going down just the same. The number of foreign tourists in the same period fell 1 percent, too, but overnight stays made by them decreased by 2 percent. While there were less tourists from Austria, Belgium, Switzerland, the Netherlands and Germany, more guests came from Hungary, Italy, Great Britain, the USA, Croatia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES Kovinotehna Explains Changes in Management and Presents Plans \par \par \uc2  Representatives of Kovinotehna, a Celje-based trade company, on Tuesday talked about the reasons for changes in the management. Supervisory Board on Friday, 3 July, unanimously accepted the resignation of the management, who had established they were not achieving the 1998 targets on the basis of previous \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  results analysis. The company's officials presented their plans for the future. Supervisory Board appointed Aleksander Ivan Gerzina new Management Board Chairman, observing the proposal of outgoing Chairman Ales Ilc. \par \par \uc2  Autocommerce Becomes Owner of Globtour Praha Travel Agency \par \par \uc2  Autocommerce, a Ljubljana-based joint-stock company, acquired a majority stake in Globtour Praha, one of the three largest tour operators in the Czech Republic, in the first half of July. Autocommerce thus continues with the implementation of its strategic objective to develop its tourist activities by investing in successful tourist companies engaged in organizing travels in a wider geographic area. \par \par \uc2  Port of Koper Holds 3rd General Assembly \par \par \uc2  Port of Koper shareholders allocated 1997 profit and undistributed profit from previous years at the third general assembly on Friday. They elected a new member of the nine-member supervisory board, and postponed deliberations on a proposal to reduce capital stock until autumn. It was decided that SIT 500 million (US$ 2.9m) of the SIT 1.9 (US$ 11.6m) net profit be allocated for the own share fund and SIT 1.4 billion (US$ 8.2m) remain undistributed. Of the undistributed profit from previous years amounting to SIT 837 million (US$ 4.9m), SIT 212 million (US$ 1.2m) belongs to the government according to the general assembly decision; SIT 8.5 million was allocated for awards to supervisory board members, and SIT 614 million (US$ 3.6m) for dividends. Gross dividend will thus amount to SIT 86 (US$ .508) per share. \par \par \uc2  Petrol Presents Modernized Laboratory \par \par \uc2  Slovene oil company Petrol's management team on Thursday presented a modernized chemical laboratory, the only Slovene chemical laboratory with the accreditation of the Office for Standardization and Metrology. The laboratory has gained the title of the central Slovene oil laboratory. Representatives of Petrol spoke also about the current business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a annual general meeting to be held on 31 July and problems regarding Slovenia's oil refinery Nafta Lendava, owned by Petrol, the majority stakeholder, and the government (45 percent). \par \par \uc2  Slovene Shipowner's Board Opt for Privatisation \par \par \uc2  Supervisory Board of Splosna plovba on Friday examined an analysis of conceivable ways of privatisation of the only Slovene shipowner. Board members established the analysis indicated good starting points for a successful realization of company's privatisation as it considered the agreement for a 20-percent workers' share and demands by the Banka Koper bank. Wishing to secure state property, Supervisory Board advocated the idea that a strategic partner be found by international tender. \par \par \uc2  Kolicevo Paper Mill Changes Hands \par \par \uc2  The ownership structure in the Sarrio Slovenija paper mill has changed: the 40-percent stake of Italy's Sarrio SA was bought by Austria's Mayr-Melnhof Karton AG. The company's new name is Kolicevo Karton d.o.o.. \par \par \uc2  Acroni Exceeds Sales Targets \par \par \uc2  Acroni of Jesenice sold 94,000 tons of goods in the first six months, exceeding the target amount by 15 percent. The loss amounted to SIT 600 million in the period, while the annual plan had been set at one billion tolars. Acroni is a metallurgical company operating within the Slovene Ironworks concern. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves Total US$ 4.31 Billion \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves amounted to US$ 4.31 billion in end-April, a rise of US$ 77.5 million, or 1.8 percent over March. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed for US$ 3.3 billion (77 percent) and those of commercial banks US$ 981 million. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves increased by US$ 55 million in end-April over the beginning of 1998, when they reached US$ 4.26 billion. \par \par \uc2  Foreign Trade Declined in May \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's exports totaled US$ 736.6 million in May and were down by 1.9 percent from a month before, with imports down by 5.6 percent to US$ 809.9 million. The deficit amounted to US$ 73.3 million at a 90.9-percent exports/imports ratio. Compared to May 1997, exports rose by 8.5 percent and imports by 2.4 percent. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE Shares Up on Ljubljana Stock Exchange \par \par \uc2  All three markets within the Ljubljana Stock Exchange recorded appreciations in shares last week. The SBI share index was slightly down only on Monday, and then up in the following days. It increased by 52.94 points and concluded the week at 1743.51 points. The opposite trend was recorded in the BIO bond index; it was down by 0.34 points to 104.02 points. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES Most Leading Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Appreciate \par \par \uc2  The mean prices of leading foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased last week according to the Bank of Slovenia. The U.S. dollar was up by SIT 0.84 to SIT 171.09, the German mark by SIT 0.05 to SIT 93.85 and the Austrian schilling by SIT 0.01 to SIT 13.34. A decline was recorded in the Swiss franc, by SIT 0.31 to SIT 111.11, while the mean price of the Italian lira remained unchanged at SIT 9.52 for 100 liras. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010927du7c0017l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
