\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Bank of Slovenia slows tolar strengthening. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marja Novak \par \uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 1998\par 14:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LJUBLJANA, July 2 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the third time in six weeks to rein in the appreciation of the tolar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Economists said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy was proving a success and should have been implemented earlier. \par \par \uc2  "In fact it should have come some five months earlier," Franci Krizanic, an economist at Ljubljana University's Faculty of Law, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Had the bank not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the appreciation of the tolar would seriously hurt Slovenian exporters and their profitability which could cause reduction of exports and....a lack of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added. \par \par \uc2  Slovenia has seen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate over the last few years due to high \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows from exports, foreign investment and tourism. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal is to neutralise surplus foreign inflows in order to reach a stable tolar exchange rate," Bank of Slovenia Governor France Arhar told Reuters earlier. \par \par \uc2  Maintaining a stable tolar exchange rate and controlling inflation were the bank's main goals. \par \par \uc2  In its latest move on Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said banks cooperating in its exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from companies at a minimum of 94.4 tolars to the mark, compared to 94.1 tolars since June 15 and 93.9 tolars since the end of May. \par \par \uc2  "Raising the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is in line with the bank's strategy regarding the appreciation of the tolar," Francek Drenovec, head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's analytic and research centre, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovenia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the end of May, cooperating with Nova Ljubljanska Banka, SKB  , Dolenjska Banka\tab \tab    , Banka Celje and Abanka.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bankers expect the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Slovenia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate at least once a month over the next five months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  wants to reduce the difference between the exchange rate and inflation it will have to raise the tolar rate to the mark by about 0.6 tolars each month," said Vanda Mocnik Kohek, head of treasury at Bank Austria.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Slovenia's consumer price index rose 4.9 percent from the end of December to the end of June but the government is still forecasting that price inflation for the whole year will be eight percent, down from 8.4 percent in 1997. Analysts said the government may slow the pace of increases in officially-controlled fuel and electricty prices in order to hit that target.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Ljubljana newsroom, 386-61-17-00-545, ljubljana.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7201lib\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2  Weekly Review of Slovene Economy from June 14 through 20. \b0\par\par\uc2 3,150 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1998\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 STA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o\par \par \uc2  Ljubljana, 20 June (STA) - President of the Chamber of Small Business (OZS) Transport Section Darko Hlupic officially called off a protest of truck drivers scheduled for Monday, 22 June, after an agreement was signed on Friday afternoon between the government, Chamber of Small Business (OZS) and Chamber of Commerce and Industry (GZS). At Friday's correspondence session, the government adopted several measures thus meeting the demands made by Slovene truck drivers. The government agreed to reduce motorway tolls and yearly payments for the use of roads which in the future would be calculated on the basis of lorry axle pressure and the lorry's weight, as stipulated by the EU standards. Moreover, it was agreed that in the border area at border crossings and on national roads the government will not allow the collection of municipality taxes. \par \par \uc2   By 2 July at the latest, the government will have adopted a decree (which will take effect on 15 July) allowing lorries to have a free access to the customs house. By 30 October, a working group should bring some other issues into line with truck drivers' demands, one of which is a change to the recently passed Road Safety Act. Minister of Transport and Communications Anton Bergauer said a working group would start dealing with the remaining open issues as early as on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Chronology: Wednesday/17 June - Ljubljana: Trade union SKEI congres; Wednesday/17 June - Ljubljana: Three-day meeting of high-ranking representatives of the nuclear safety administrations in the EU, Central and Eastern Europe and former Soviet Union nations, taking place in the framework of Concert Group; Wednesday/17 June - Celje: The beginning of the fair 'For A Healthier Life with Nature'; Thursday/18 June - Ljubljana: The beginning of 27th regular annual economic colloquium of NATO; Thursday/18 June - Ljubljana: Resumption of negotiations to conclude a new social agreement. \par \par \uc2  GOVERNMENT Slovenia temporarily abolished concessions for the import of wheat from Hungary \par \par \uc2  For the first time since Slovenia has become a member of CEFTA, the Slovene government on 18th June adopted a protective customs measure, the aim of which is to reduce the import of inexpensive wheat from Hungary. With the modification of the decree on temporary use of additional protocol no. 6 to CEFTA agreement, it temporarily abolished the 15-percent preferential customs rate for the import of wheat and replaced it by a regular 5.3-percent customs rate and a special import levy (SIT 17.2). The customs duty for the import of wheat from Hungary has thus increased by 7.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Employment Programme Adopted \par \par \uc2  The government passed on 18 June an active employment policy programme for 1998 in an effort to reduce unemployment by 5000 to 10,000 persons. According to Minister of Labour Anton Rop, the programme is designed both for those unemployed and those workers whose job is uncertain. A part of the employment policy programme is also the scheme to reorganize enterprises, carried out by the Slovene Development Corporation (SRD). Two basic schemes on which the active employment policy programme is based are a programme to train the unemployed and a public works programme. The two programmes are expected to involve 10,000 unemployed. SIT 16 billion is to be allocated for their implementation. \par \par \uc2  EU - SLOVENIA Legislation of EU Candidates in Area of Free Movement of Goods Screened \par \par \uc2  Slovene producers should not face any severe difficulties when EU standards are enforced, said State Secretary in the Slovene Ministry of Economic Relations and Development Vojka Ravbar, who attended a week-long multilateral meeting at which EU candidates' legislation regulating the free movement of goods was screened. Nevertheless, Slovenia will have to adopt several regulations to do away with some technical obstacles obstructing the free movement of goods, and apply them, too. Slovenia is scheduled to hold a bilateral meeting with EU officials at which the same topic will be discussed on 30 June, to wind up on 8 July. \par \par \uc2  Slovene Agriculture Presented to European Negotiators \par \par \uc2  Slovene Ministry of Agriculture, Food and Forestry officials briefed a EU delegation on visit to Slovenia on 18 June about Slovenia's integration into the EU from the aspect of agriculture. The visiting EU delegation consisted of head of the EU negotiating team with EU candidates Nikolaus van der Pas, chief negotiator in charge of Slovenia Jaime Garcia Lombardero and head of the European Commission delegation to Slovenia Amir Naqvi. EU officials were updated on the situation in Slovene agriculture, and compared it with agriculture in some other EU candidates; they were also informed about how Slovenia is preparing its agriculture for EU membership. \par \par \uc2  BUSINESS CONFERENCES, CONSULTATIONS Slovene Enterprises Want to Increase Exports to Poland \par \par \uc2  Polish experts presented the characteristics of the Polish market and specific ways of marketing industrial products and consumer goods there at a seminar entitled How to Export to Poland, organized for Slovene business people by the Trade and Investment Promotion Office and business magazine Gospodarski Vestnik. The seminar was attended by over 40 representatives of Slovene business that are interested in exporting to the 40-million Polish market. The Slovene-Polish trade is marked by a surplus of some US$ 100 million on the Slovene side. With Poland buying a lot of Slovene products, its main imports from Slovenia are pharmaceutical products, whose worth reached US$ 60 million last year, and electric switches. \par \par \uc2  A Conference of Slovene and Belgian Business Officials \par \par \uc2  A Slovene-Belgian business conference was organized in the framework of the visit by a Belgian business delegation to Slovenia on 16 June by the Chamber of Commerce and Industry of Slovenia (CCIS). It was attended by 14 Belgian companies from the field of agriculture and food industry, metal and chemical industries and banking as well as 32 Slovene companies. A cooperation agreement between the CCIS of the Belgian Foreign Trade Board was signed on the occasion by Matevz Bambic, head of the CCIS department for international cooperation, and Paul Vindelinckx, secretary general of the Belgian Board. Trade between Slovenia and Belgium amounted to US$ 101.1 million in the first four months of this year, which is nearly by 20 percent more than in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES The Most Successful Slovene Companies in 1997 \par \par \uc2  Company Revoz from Novo mesto heads the list of 300 biggest Slovene companies with regard to revenue in 1997. Petrol and Elektro Slovenija are placed second and third in the list, respectively, followed by Poslovni sistem Mercator and Gorenje from Velenje. The total revenue of Revoz amounted to SIT 156 billion, the revenue of Petrol amounted to SIT 128 billion, and the revenue of Elektro Slovenija to SIT 87 billion. With net profit amounting to SIT 5.7 billion, Telekom Slovenije - according to unaudited data - is placed first on the list of the most successful Slovene companies regarding the net profit. The pharmaceutical company Krka is placed second with net profit amounting to SIT 5.1 billion in 1997, and the pharmaceutical company Lek is placed third with the net profit of SIT 3.7 billion in 1997. \par \par \uc2  Iskrateling Signed a Deal with Russians \par \par \uc2  Slovenia's Iskrateling has recently signed a contract with Russia's North Caucasus Railways. The equipment supplied and installed by Iskrateling will launch the digitalization of the railway network there. Iskrateling will supply 237 digital telephone exchanges and install optical cables. Apart from telephone exchanges, Iskrateling will provide the other teleinformation equipment necessary for the operator. The first deal is worth US$ 12 million. The railway of the region of North Caucasus is among the largest of a total of 17 railway regions in Russia. With its routs totaling some 7000 km, North Caucasus Railways employ 120,000 workers. Iskrateling is negotiating similar contracts with some other Russian railways. \par \par \uc2  Bankruptcy Proceedings of Delta Ptuj \par \par \uc2  Bankruptcy proceedings were initiated for the company Delta in Ptuj, which employs 330 people. The decision was reached on 15 June by the management of Labod and trade unions representatives. 180 of the employed will be temporarily included in the other Labod's company in Ptuj, the company Valida, whereas the other employees will be directed to the National Employment Office. \par \par \uc2  British Airways Opened Its Branch Office in Ljubljana \par \par \uc2  The company British Airways opened its branch office on 16 June in Ljubljana. Apart from selling tickets, the company will also be offering services of its subsidiary company British Airways Holidays. \par \par \uc2  233,800 Passengers Travelled with Adria Airways in the First Five Months \par \par \uc2  As many as 233,800 passengers travelled by Adria Airways' aeroplanes in the first five months of this year, which is by 14 percent more than in the same period last year. The number of passengers increased by 18 percent in the regular passenger services and by four percent in charter. Adria also transported 1490 tons of cargo in the first five months, which is by three percent less than in the same period last year. \par \par \uc2  COMPANIES - GENERAL MEETINGS Cinkarna is to Pay Out Dividends, Last Year's Loss Covered \par \par \uc2  Cinkarna Celje shareholders discussed at the first general meeting on 16 June the shareout of profits since 1993 and last year's loss. The company registered SIT 574 million of revalued profit in the period from 1993 to 1995. They will allocate 39.74 percent of the profit to pay out the dividends, whereas 60 percent of the profit will not be allocated. The total loss of Cinkarna Celje amounted to SIT 230 million last year. \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana Stock Exchange Shareholders Hold General Meeting \par \par \uc2  Ljubljana Stock Exchange shareholders approved the 1997 business report and decided not to divide last year's clear profit of SIT 332.8 thousand. A change passed by the general meeting was that the number of members of the supervisory board would be reduced from 11 to 7. The market capitalization of shares listed on the Ljubljana Stock Exchange increased a great deal in 1997. Another significant factor influencing last year's business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the introduction of the fiduciary accounts by the Bank of Slovenia. \par \par \uc2  Lek Share Brings a Dividend of SIT 660 \par \par \uc2  Lek shareholders adopted some changes to the joint-stock company's statute, passed the 1997 business report, discussed the division of their profit and elected new members of the supervisory board. Lek's clear profit amounted to SIT 3.7 billion in 1997, a rise of 21 percent over 1996, Management Board Director Andro Ocvirk told news conference on Friday. Net profit per share totaled SIT 1,914 last year. While last year's clear profit remains undivided, Lek will divide clear profit made in previous years (amounting to SIT 1.3 billion). The divided per share (either A, B or C) will total SIT 660. The general meeting, held on Thursday, reached two decisions to change the Lek statute. The changes will apply to dematerialization of shares A and B. Director Ocvirk said the company's business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  were positively influenced also by the 12.9-percent growth rate of U.S. dollar exchange rate in 1997. Lek managed to register its drug aciclovir in the U.S. market, one of its key foreign markets. Together with amocsiclav and cimetidin, aciclovir is Lek's major product. Mostly owing to an increase in exports, Lek's revenues totaled SIT 21 billion in the January-to-May period, 15 percent up from the same period 1997. Foreigners hold a 30-percent share of Lek, with Slovene investment funds holding nearly the same share. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF \b \highlight22\uc2 SLOVENIA\b0 \highlight\uc2  New \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Purchase Exchange Rate \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Slovenia\b0 \highlight\uc2  informed on 15 June about the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase exchange rate, amounting to SIT 94.10 per German mark. The banks, which were chosen on the basis of the proposal and are obliged to buy the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the basis of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase exchange rate, started to use the new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase exchange rate on 16 June. \par \par \uc2  Governors of Central Banks of All Former Yugoslav Republics Meet \par \par \uc2  A meeting of governors of central banks of all former Yugoslav republics, first after seven years, was held in Brussels at the initiative of Alfons Verplaetse, Belgian governor and Chairman of the Bank for International Settlements (BIS), Bank of Slovenia reported on Tuesday. The talks, held on Monday, centred on economic situation in the new-formed countries and on the results of monetary policies in the new situation. The governors also discussed possibilities for distribution of the property of the former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Yugoslavia (NBJ) at the Basel Bank, in particular certain options presented by Governor Verplaetse. The governors agreed a new meeting should be organized as soon as possible, the Bank of Slovenia reported. \par \par \uc2  Watts: New Round of Negotiations on Succession to Ex-Yugoslavia in Autumn \par \par \uc2  Sir Arthur Watts, international mediator for negotiations on succession to former Yugoslavia, will start a new round of negotiations with the successor states in September. At their meeting on Wednesday, Watts also informed Slovenia's Foreign Minister Boris Frlec that he would visit Slovenia in September or October. According to Watts, the forthcoming round of negotiations will be the last chance before the international community decides for arbitration. Minister Frlec, who is paying a two-day visit to London, explained to Watts that it was in the interest of Ljubljana for the succession issue to be solved as soon as possible. In his opinion, the solution would also contribute to the normalization of relations between the successor states and to economic and political stability in the region. Slovenia believes that solution of the succession problem would not only help stabilize South-Eastern Europe but would also have a positive impact on the solution of the Kosovo crisis. \par \par \uc2  BANKS Hmezad banka to Merge with Banka Celje this September \par \par \uc2  The General Meeting of the Hmezad banka Zalec bank on 15 June passed a resolution to merge with Banka Celje. As proposed by the managements of both banks, the merger will be carried out on 30 September 1998. Hmezad banka decided to merge with the Celje bank to economize operating, globalize banking transactions, expand the range of services and last but not least to cushion restricted conditions of bank \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and severer foreign competitions. After the merger Hmezad banka will operate as Banka Celje, Zalec subsidiary. Hmezad banka was set up in 1992. A universal bank, Hmezad is licenced to conduct all banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits of individuals. The banks Banka Celje and Hmezad banka signed a contract to set up the banking group Banka Celje in January 1997. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Celje's 1997 Net Profit Over 1 Billion Tolars \par \par \uc2  The 1997 net profit of Bank of Celje amounted to SIT 1.2 billion tolars (USD 72 million). The 1997 income totalled SIT 17 billion while the expenses amounted to SIT 15.7 billion. Bank of Celje increased its market share from 5.14 percent to 5.21 percent and confirmed its position as the fifth biggest bank in Slovenia (of the total of 28). \par \par \uc2  TRANSPORT AND COMMUNICATIONS State Guarantees for Loans Taken up to Build a Railway Line between Puconci and Hungarian Border \par \par \uc2  The National Assembly adopted through a fast-track procedure a law on state guarantees for loans taken up by Slovene Railways to build a railway line between Puconci and the Hungarian border. The principals of the loans taken up must not exceed ECU 71.3 million (SIT 13.2 billion). \par \par \uc2  POWER SUPPLY The Government Appointed a Negotiating Group for Power Plant Krsko \par \par \uc2  The government agreed to the initiative on 11 June to conclude an interstate agreement between Slovenia and Croatia connected with the proprietorship and the status of the power plant. The government appointed the negotiating group, consisting of 14-members and led by Uros Korze. The initiative to conclude the agreement was sent to the parliament and is to be discussed by the international relations committee. \par \par \uc2  Geoplin Lowered the Natural Gas by 10 Percent \par \par \uc2  The company Geoplin lowered on 16 June the price of natural gas by ten percent. The reduction will be in force by the end of this September. The price of natural gas will amount to SIT 20.6 per cubic meter in average. \par \par \uc2  UNEMPLOYMENT Unemployment Decreasing for the Fourth Month in SuccessionThe National Employment Office provided employment to 56,000 unemployed people last year, and included 32,000 unemployed people and 8,000 redundant workers in employment programmes. It offered assistance to 50,000 employers, enabled unemployment benefits to nearly 41,000 unemployed people and provided scholarships to 55,000 college and university students. 124,552 people were registered at the National Employment Office at the end of May, which is by 1.7 percent more than in May 1997. The unemployment is decreasing for the fourth month in succession currently. The level of unemployment was 14.4 percent in May; 13.5 percent among men and 15.6 percent among women. \par \par \uc2  STATISTICS GDP Increased by 1.9 Percent in the First Quarter \par \par \uc2  Gross domestic product (GDP) in fixed prices dating from 1992 increased by 1.9 percent in the first quarter of 1998 compared with the last quarter of the previous year; this is by 0.6 percent higher growth than in the previous quarter (1.3 percent). The growth rate on the annual level also shows strong rise; GDP was in the first quarter of this year by 6.5 percent higher over the first quarter in 1997. Slovenia has not registered such a high growth rate (on the annual level) since the second quarter of 1994. \par \par \uc2  April Net Salary SIT 97,993 \par \par \uc2  The average net salary in Slovene companies and other organizations amounted to SIT 97,993 in April, the National Statistical office reported. The average April net salary was by 0.6 percent higher than in March and by 9.8 percent higher than in April 1997. The average gross salary in Slovenia amounted to SIT 155,145 in April, which is by 0.9 percent more than a year ago. \par \par \uc2  STOCK EXCHANGE The Majority of Shares Decreased in Price \par \par \uc2  The SBI share index fell under 1700 points last week one more time, having lost 37.88 points to 1663.51 points. Seventeen shares depreciated on stock market listings and ten appreciated at Ljubljana Stock Exchange. The BIO bond index was up by 0.15 point to 103.96 points. \par \par \uc2  EXCHANGE RATES U.S. Dollar and Swiss Franc Down \par \par \uc2  Last week saw a decrease in price of the U.S. dollar (by SIT 1.3 to SIT 167.90) and the Swiss franc (by SIT 1.2 to SIT 111.96). The price for 100 Italian lira remained almost unchanged at SIT 9.50 per 100 liras, whereas the Austrain schilling and the German mark increased slightly - by SIT 0.1 to SIT 13.31 and by SIT 0.06 to SIT 93.66, respectively. \par \par \uc2  (c) 1998 STA, Slovenska Tiskovna Agencija d.o.o. \par \par \uc2 Dokument sta0000020010927du6k000sd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
